\section{Some considerations about the asymptotic condition numbers}  \label{sscclast}

Suppose that the matrix $A$ in the ODE (\ref{ode}) does not have all eigenvalues with the same real part, meaning $q > 1$ (recall that $q$ is the number of distinct real parts). The global asymptotic condition number  $K_\infty\left( t\right)$ grows exponentially with 
$t$. Hence, the relative error $\varepsilon$ in the initial value is exponentially
magnified in the relative error $\delta(t)$ in the solution, in the worst case for the initial value and the
perturbation of the initial value.

However, this conclusion is too pessimistic, since this exponential magnification of $\varepsilon$ in $\delta(t)$ appears only in a non-generic situation for the initial value $y_0$. In fact, as illustrated in point
2 of Remark \ref{KtAyz}, in the generic situation $j^\ast=j^{\ast\ast}=1$ and $L^\ast=L^{\ast\ast}=L_1$ for $y_0$ and
the direction of perturbation $\widehat{z}_0$, we have
\begin{equation}
\frac{\delta(t)}{\varepsilon}=K\left( t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0}\right)\sim K_\infty\left( t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0}\right) = \frac{\left\Vert
Q_{1}(t)\widehat{z}_{0}\right\Vert }{\left\Vert Q_{1}(t)\widehat{y}%
_{0}\right\Vert },\ t\rightarrow +\infty,  \label{Kgeneric}
\end{equation}
where $Q_1(t):=Q_{1L_1}(t)$ to semplify the notation. In this generic situation, the magnification of the initial error remains bounded as well as away from zero by varying $t$ (see point 1 of Remark \ref{KtAyz}). 


We have introduced the three asymptotic condition numbers $K_\infty(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$, $K_\infty(t,$\ $y_0)$ and $K_\infty(t)$. The most important asymptotic condition number is $K_\infty(t,y_0)$. In fact, $K_\infty(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$ is given in terms of the (in general) unknown direction of perturbation $\widehat{z}_0$ and $K_\infty(t)$ is a worst asymptotic magnification factor which applies in a non-generic situation for $y_0$.

As illustrated in point 2 of Remark \ref{KtAy}, in the generic situation $j^\ast=1$ and $L^\ast=L_1$ for $y_0$, this most important asymptotic condition number $K_\infty(t,y_0)$, representing the asympotic magnification factor $K_\infty\left( t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0}\right)$ in (\ref{Kgeneric}) for the worst perturbation of $y_0$, is given by 
\begin{equation}
K_\infty\left( t,y_{0}\right) = \frac{\left\Vert Q_{1}(t)\right\Vert 
}{\left\Vert Q_{1}(t)\widehat{y}_{0}\right\Vert }. \label{Kgeneric2}
\end{equation}

Going into more detail, in (\ref{Kgeneric}) and (\ref{Kgeneric2}), the vectors $Q_{1}(t)u$, with $u=\widehat{y}_0$ or $u=\widehat{z}_0$,  and the matrix $Q_{1}(t)$  are given by   (see (\ref{Qjl}), (\ref{Pil=}) and (\ref{Pilu=}))
\begin{eqnarray*}
	Q_{1}(t)u&=&\sum\limits_{\substack{ \lambda _{i}\in \Lambda _1   \\ %
			m_{i}=M_1}}\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\ \omega _{i}t}\sum\limits
	_{\substack{ j=1  \\ m_{ij}=M_1}}^{d_{i}}\alpha_{ijM_1}(u) v^{\left( i,j,1\right) } \notag\\
	&=&\sum\limits_{\substack{\lambda _{i}\in \Lambda _1\\ j\in\{1,\ldots,d_i\}\\ m_{ij}=M_1}}\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\ \omega _{i}t}\alpha_{ijM_1}(u)
	v^{%
		\left( i,j,1\right) }   \notag
\end{eqnarray*}
and
\begin{eqnarray*}
	Q_{1}(t)&=&\sum\limits_{\substack{ \lambda _{i}\in \Lambda _1  \\ %
			m_{i}=M_1}}\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\ \omega _{i}t}\sum\limits
	_{\substack{ j=1  \\ m_{ij}= M_1}}^{d_{i}}v^{%
		\left( i,j,1\right) }w^{\left( i,j,M_1\right) }\\
		&=&\sum\limits_{\substack{\lambda _{i}\in \Lambda _1\\ j\in\{1,\ldots,d_i\}\\ m_{ij}=M_1}}\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\ \omega _{i}t}v^{%
			\left( i,j,1\right) }w^{\left( i,j,M_1\right) }, \label{QQQ}
\end{eqnarray*}
where
$$
M_1:=L_1+1=\max\limits_{\lambda_i\in\Lambda_1}m_i=\max\limits_{\lambda_i\in\Lambda_1}\max\limits_{j\in\{1,\ldots,d_i\}}m_{ij}
$$ to semplify the notation.
This shows that the asymptotic behavior of the magnification in the solution of the initial relative error is determined by
\begin{itemize}
	\item the imaginary parts $\omega_i$ of the rightmost eigenvalues of $A$ with the longest Jordan chains (chains of length $M_1$); in other words, with the largest mini-blocks in the JCF (mini-blocks of order $M_1$);
	\item the eigenvectors $v^{\left( i,j,1\right) }$ of these longest Jordan chains;
	\item the components $\alpha_{ijM_1}(u)$ of $u=\widehat{y}_0,\widehat{z}_0$ along the last generalized eigenvectors of these longest Jordan chains;
	\item for the worst case of $\widehat{z}_0$, the last left generalized eigenvectors $w^{( i,j,M_1)}$ corresponnding  to these longest Jordan chains.
\end{itemize}

For $u\in\mathbb{C}^n$, we say that $u$ satisfies the \emph{Rightmost Last Generalized Eigenvector (RLGE) condition} if $j(u)=1$ and $L(u)=L_1$, i.e. there is a non-zero component $\alpha_{ijM_1}(u)$ along the last generalized eigenvctors of the longest Jordan chain  of the rightmost eigenvalues of $A$. Therefore, we can say that 
(\ref{Kgeneric}) holds if $y_0$ (or $\widehat{y}_0$) and $\widehat{z}_0$ satisfy the RLGE condition and  (\ref{Kgeneric2}) holds if $y_0$ (or $\widehat{y}_0$)  satisfies the RLGE condition. This terminology is utilized in \cite{Maset2022}.


\section{The generic real case. Part I: introduction}

In this section, Part I, and the following four sections, Parts II, III, IV, and V, we tailor the results from the previous Section 4 to a real ODE ($\ref{ode}$) in a generic situation for the matrix $A$, the initial value $y_{0}$ and the direction of perturbation $\widehat{z}_{0}$.

Since, as noted in the previous Section 5, the worst-case scenario of relative error magnification described by the asymptotic condition number $K_\infty(t)$ occurs in a non-generic situation for the initial value $y_0$, we focus our analysis on the other two more significant asymptotic condition numbers $K_\infty(t, y_0, \widehat{z}_0)$ and $K_\infty(t, y_0)$.

Parts I and II introduce concepts and present results essential for the developments in Parts III and IV, where asymptotic condition numbers are addressed. Parts I and III consider an arbitrary vector norm, while Parts II and IV explore the concepts introduced in Parts I and III, respectively, in the case of the Euclidean vector norm and a complex conjugate pairs of eigenvalues.

The concluding section, Part V, investigates the onset of asymptotic behavior, specifically studying when a condition number begins to be close to its asymptotic form and identifying the factors that influence its rapid convergence towards this form.


\subsection{Real and complex induced norms}
It is important to recall the observation made in Remark \ref{remreal} regarding a real ODE (\ref{ode}). Consequently, in this section, the vector norm $\left\Vert\ \cdot\ \right\Vert$ is restricted to real vectors and, for a real matrix $B \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$, we use the real induced norm
\begin{equation}
	\Vert B \Vert = \max_{\substack{u \in \mathbb{R}^{n} \\ \left\Vert u \right\Vert = 1}} \left\Vert Bu \right\Vert, \label{rin}
\end{equation}
instead of the complex induced norm
\begin{equation*}
	\max_{\substack{u \in \mathbb{C}^{n} \\ \left\Vert u \right\Vert = 1}} \left\Vert Bu \right\Vert.
\end{equation*}


For a complex row vectors $b\in\mathbb{C}^{1\times n}$, we utilize both the real induced norm
\begin{equation*}
	\left\Vert b\right\Vert_\mathbb{R}:=\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{R}^{n}  \\ \Vert u\Vert =1
	}}\left\vert bu\right\vert  
\end{equation*}
and the complex induced norm
$$
\left\Vert b\right\Vert=\max_{\substack{u \in \mathbb{C}^{n} \\ \left\Vert u \right\Vert = 1}} \left\vert bu \right\vert.
$$
Observe that we have
\begin{equation}
	\left\Vert b\right\Vert_{\mathbb{R}}\leq \left\Vert b \right\Vert. \label{r<=c}
\end{equation}


\begin{remark} \label{22}
If the vector norm $\Vert \ \cdot \ \Vert $ is a $p$-norm, then, for the real (complex) induced norm $\Vert \ \cdot \ \Vert$ of a real (complex) row vector $b$, we have 
	$$
	\left\Vert b\right\Vert=\left\Vert
	b^T \right\Vert _{q},
	$$
	where $\Vert \ \cdot \ \Vert_q$ is the vector $q$-norm with $\frac{1}{p}+\frac{1}{q}=1$.
\end{remark}
















\subsection{The generic $\Lambda_j$}

Assume $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ in (\ref{ode}). Let $j \in \{1, \ldots, q\}$ be such that the set $\Lambda_j$ consists of either a real simple eigenvalue or a single pair of complex conjugate simple eigenvalues. We say that $\Lambda_j$ is \emph{real} in the first case and \emph{complex} in the second case.


We consider a set $\Lambda_j$ of this type, since it is a generic situation for the real matrix $A$ to have all sets $\Lambda_j$%
, $j\in\{1,\ldots,q\}$, of such a form.

When $\Lambda_j$ is real, we denote by $\lambda _{j}$ the real eigenvalue in $\Lambda_j$. When $\Lambda_j$ is complex, we denote by $\lambda _{j}$ and $%
\overline{\lambda _{j}}$ the complex conjugate pair in $\Lambda_j$, where $\lambda_j$ is the eigenvalue with positive imaginary part. This positive imaginary part is denoted by $\omega_j$. %(so the imaginary part of $\overline{\lambda}_{j}$ is $-\omega_j<0$). 


Let $V$ be the matrix whose columns form a Jordan basis for the matrix $A$ and let $W = V^{-1}$ (see Section \ref{JCFsection}). Let $v^{(j)} \in \mathbb{C}^{n}$ be the eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the simple eigenvalue $\lambda_{j}$ (real eigenvalue or complex eigenvalue with positive imaginary part in the complex conjugate pair) appearing as a column in the matrix $V$. Let $w^{(j)} \in \mathbb{C}^{1 \times n}$ be the corresponding row in the matrix $W$, i.e. the index of $v^{(j)}$ in $V$ matches the index of $w^{(j)}$ in $W$. Observe that $w^{(j)}$ is a left eigenvector  of the matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\lambda_j$.

If $%
\Lambda_j$ is real, then $v^{(j)}$
and $w^{(j)}$ are real vectors (see Proposition \ref{conjVW}). 

The projection onto the eigenspace of the simple (real or complex) eigenvalue $\lambda_j$ is $v^{(j)}w^{(j)}$ (see (\ref{Pi0})). Thus, by
recalling Proposition \ref{Pireal}, we have 
\begin{equation}
Q_{j0}(t)=v^{(j)}w^{(j)}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}, \label{Qj0(t)ureal}
\end{equation}
when $\Lambda_j$ is real and 
\begin{equation}
Q_{j0}(t) =2\mathrm{Re}\left( \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega
_{j}t}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}\right)\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n},  \label{Qj0(t)ucomplex}
\end{equation}
when $\Lambda _{j}$ is complex.

\begin{remark} \label{project1}
Recall Propostion \ref{Pireal}. When $\Lambda_j$ is real, the projection on the eigenspace corresponding to $\lambda_j$ is $Q_{j0}(t)$, independent of $t$. When $\Lambda_j$ is complex, the projection onto the sum of the eigenspaces corresponding to $\lambda_j$ and $\overline{\lambda_j}$ is
$$
Q_{j0}(0) =v^{(j)}w^{(j)}+\overline{v^{(j)}}\ \overline{w^{(j)}}=2\mathrm{Re}\left(v^{(j)}w^{(j)}\right).
$$
\end{remark}  

\subsection{The unit vectors $\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }$ and $\widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }$ and the number $f_j$}
\label{unitvectors}
Let%
\begin{equation}
\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }:=\frac{v^{\left( j\right) }}{\left\Vert
v^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert },\ \widehat{w}^{\left(j\right) }:=\frac{%
w^{\left( j\right) }}{\left\Vert w^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert }\ \text{and%
}\ f_{j}:=\left\Vert w^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert \cdot \left\Vert
v^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert.  \label{normalization}
\end{equation}

In (\ref{normalization}), when $\Lambda_j$ is real, $\left\Vert w^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert$ denotes the real induced norm
\begin{equation*}
\left\Vert w^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert=\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{R}^{n}  \\ %
\Vert u\Vert=1}}\left\vert w^{\left( j\right) }u\right\vert  \label{in}
\end{equation*}%
of the real row vector $w^{(j)}$. On the other hand, when $\Lambda _{j}$ is complex, $\left\Vert w^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert$ denotes the complex induced norm
\begin{equation*}
\left\Vert w^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert=\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{C}^{n}  \\ \Vert u\Vert =1
}}\left\vert w^{\left( j\right) }u\right\vert  \label{inc}
\end{equation*}
of the complex row vector $\widehat{w}^{(j)}$. 
\subsection{The numbers $g_j(t,u)$ and $g_j(t)$, the vector $\widehat{\Theta}_j(t,u)$ and the matrix $\widehat{\Theta}_j(t)$.}
\label{thenumbers}
In the following, in both cases for $\Lambda_j$, real or complex, we write the norm of the real vector $Q_{j0}(t)u$, where $u\in%
\mathbb{R}^n$, as 
\begin{equation}
\Vert Q_{j0}(t)u\Vert =f_j\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)} u\right\vert g_j(t,u)
\label{normQJ0ureal}
\end{equation}
for a suitable number $g_j(t,u)$. Analogously, we write the real induced norm (\ref{rin}) of the real 
matrix $Q_{j0}(t)$ as 
\begin{equation}
\Vert Q_{j0}(t)\Vert =f_jg_j(t)  \label{normQJ0}
\end{equation}
for a suitable number $g_j(t)$. 


Next two lemmas say what $g_j(t,u)$ and $g_j(t)$ are.

\begin{lemma} \label{Lemmareal}
Let $j\in\{1,\ldots,q\}$. If $\Lambda_j$ is real, then 
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&g_j(t,u)=1,\ u\in\mathbb{R}^n,\\
&&g_j(t)=1. 
\end{eqnarray*}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$, by (\ref{Qj0(t)ureal}) we have
\begin{equation*}
Q_{j0}(t)u =v^{(j)}w^{(j)}u=\left(w^{(j)}u\right)v^{(j)}=f_j\left(\widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right)\widehat{v}^{(j)}
\end{equation*}%
and then 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert Q_{j0}(t)u\right\Vert =\left\vert w%
^{(j)}u\right\vert \left\Vert v^{(j)}\right\Vert=f_j\left\vert \widehat{w}%
^{(j)}u\right\vert.  \label{ggjj}
\end{equation}
By the second equality in (\ref{ggjj}), we obtain $g_j(t,u)=1$. By the first equality, we obtain 
\begin{equation*}
\left\Vert Q_{j0}(t)\right\Vert=\left\Vert w%
^{(j)}\right\Vert\left\Vert v^{(j)}\right\Vert=f_j
\end{equation*}
and then $g_j(t)=1$.
\end{proof}


\begin{lemma}\label{Lemmacomplex}
\label{gj2} Let $j\in\{1,\ldots,q\}$. If $\Lambda _{j}$ is complex, then 
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&g_j(t,u)=2\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_j(t,u)\right\Vert,\ u\in\mathbb{R}^n, \\
&&g_j(t)=2\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_j(t)\right\Vert, 
\end{eqnarray*}
where 
\begin{eqnarray*}
&& \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t,u\right) =\left( \left\vert \widehat{v}%
_{k}^{\left( j\right) }\right\vert \cos \left( \omega _{j}t+\alpha
_{jk}+\gamma_j\left( u\right) \right) \right) _{k=1,\ldots
,n}\in \mathbb{R}^{n}\\
&& \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t\right) =\left( \left\vert \widehat{v}%
_{k}^{\left( j\right) }\right\vert \left\vert \widehat{w}_{l}^{\left(
j\right) }\right\vert \cos \left( \omega _{j}t+\alpha _{jk}+\beta _{jl}\right) \right) _{k,l=1,\ldots ,n}\in \mathbb{%
R}^{n\times n} \\
\end{eqnarray*}%
with $\alpha_{jk}$, $\beta_{jl}$ and $\gamma_j(u)$ defined by 
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\widehat{v}_k^{\left( j\right) }=\left\vert \widehat{v}_{k}^{\left(
j\right) }\right\vert \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\alpha _{jk}},\ k\in\{1,\ldots ,n\},\\
&&\widehat{w}_l^{\left( j\right) }=\left\vert \widehat{w}_{l}^{\left(
j\right) }\right\vert \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\beta _{jl}},\ l\in\{1,\ldots ,n\}, \\
&&\widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }u=\left\vert\widehat{w}^{\left( j\right)
}u\right\vert\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\gamma _j\left( u\right) },
\end{eqnarray*}
i.e. they are, respectively, the angles in the polar forms of the components of the complex vector $\widehat{%
v}^{(j)}$, the angles in the polar forms of the components of the complex vector $\widehat{w}^{(j)}$ and  the angle in the polar form of the complex number $%
\widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }u$. 

Moreover, we have
\begin{equation} \label{A==}
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t\right) \right\Vert =\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{R}^{n} 
\\ \Vert u\Vert =1}}\left(\left\vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }u\right\vert\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j \left( t,u\right)
\right\Vert\right).
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
In this lemma, $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_j(t)\right\Vert$  is the real induced norm (\ref{rin}) of the real matrix  $\widehat{\Theta}_j(t)$.

\begin{proof}
By (\ref{Qj0(t)ucomplex}), we have
	\begin{equation}
		Q_{j0}(t)=2\mathrm{Re}\left( \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega
			_{j}t}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}\right)=2f_j\mathrm{Re}\left( \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega _{j}t}%
		\widehat{v}^{(j)}\widehat{w}^{(j)}\right).  \label{Q2}
	\end{equation}

Thus, for $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$, we have
\begin{eqnarray*}
Q_{j0}(t)u&=&2f_j\mathrm{Re}\left( \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega _{j}t}%
\widehat{v}^{(j)}\widehat{w}^{(j)}\right)u\\
&=&2f_j\mathrm{Re}\left( \mathrm{e}^{%
\sqrt{-1}\omega _{j}t}\widehat{v}^{(j)}\widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right) \\
&=&2f_j\mathrm{Re}\left( \mathrm{e}^{%
	\sqrt{-1}\omega _{j}t}\left( \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }u\right)\widehat{v}^{(j)}\right) \\
&=&2f_j\left\vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }u\right\vert \mathrm{Re}%
\left( \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega _{j}t}\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{%
-1}\gamma_j\left( u\right) }\widehat{v}^{(j)}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}%
and 
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{Re}\left( \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega _{j}t}\mathrm{%
e}^{\sqrt{-1}\gamma_j\left( u\right) }\widehat{v}^{(j)}\right)=\widehat{\Theta }_j\left(
t,u\right). 
\end{equation*}
We have obtained
\begin{equation}
	Q_{j0}(t)u=2f_j\left\vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }u\right\vert\widehat{\Theta }_j\left(
	t,u\right). \label{Qj0u(t)}
\end{equation}
and then $g_j(t,u)=2\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_j(t,u)\right\Vert$.




Moreover, in (\ref{Q2}) we have 
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{Re}\left( \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega _{j}t}\widehat{v}^{(j)}%
\widehat{w}^{(j)}\right)=\widehat{\Theta}_j(t).
\end{equation*}
Therefore, we obtain
\begin{equation}
	Q_{j0}(t)=2f_j\widehat{\Theta }_j\left(
	t\right) \label{Qj0(t)}
\end{equation}
and then $g_j(t)=2\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_j(t)\right\Vert$.

Finally, by (\ref{Qj0(t)}) and (\ref{Qj0u(t)}), we have
\begin{equation*} 
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t\right) \right\Vert =\frac{\left\Vert
Q_{j0}(t)\right\Vert }{2f_{j}}=\frac{1}{2f_j}\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{R}^{n} 
\\ \Vert u\Vert =1}}\left\Vert Q_{j0}(t)u\right\Vert=\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{R}^{n} 
\\ \Vert u\Vert =1}}\left(\left\vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }u\right\vert\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j \left( t,u\right)
\right\Vert\right).
\end{equation*}
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
\label{remTheta} \quad

\begin{itemize}
\item[1.] The functions $t\mapsto \widehat{\Theta}_j(t,u)$ and $t\mapsto 
\widehat{\Theta}_j(t)$ are periodic functions of period $\frac{2\pi}{%
\omega_j}$ and the functions $t\mapsto \Vert \widehat{\Theta}_j(t,u)\Vert $ and $t\mapsto 
\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_j(t)\Vert $ are periodic functions of period $\frac{\pi}{%
\omega_j}$. 

\item[2.] When the vector norm is a $p$-norm, we have 
\begin{equation*}
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t,u\right) \right\Vert
_{p}=\left(\sum\limits_{k=1}^{n}\left\vert \widehat{v}_{k}^{\left( j\right)
}\right\vert^p \left\vert \cos \left( \omega _{j}t+\alpha _{jk}+\gamma_j\left( u\right) \right)
\right\vert^p\right)^{\frac{1}{p}} \leq \left\Vert \widehat{v}^{\left(
j\right) }\right\Vert_p =1.
\end{equation*}
\item[3.] When the vector norm is a $p$-norm, we have $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t\right)
\right\Vert_p\leq 1$. This follows by (\ref{A==}),
$$
\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{R}^{n} 
\\ \Vert u\Vert =1}}\left\vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }u\right\vert=\left\Vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert_{\mathbb{R}}\leq \left\Vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert=1\text{\ \ \ (recall(\ref{r<=c}))}
$$
and the previous point 2.
\end{itemize}
\end{remark}




\section{The generic real case. Part II: introduction (euclidean norm and complex conjugate eigenvalues)}

In this section, we consider the case where $\Lambda_{j}$, $j \in\{1, \ldots, q\}$, is complex and the vector norm $\Vert \ \cdot \ \Vert$ is the Euclidean norm. Consequently, the vectors $\widehat{v}^{(j)}$ and $\widehat{w}^{(j)}$ in (\ref{normalization}) are defined using the Euclidean norm. Our aim is to derive expressions for the norms $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_j(t,u) \right\Vert_2$ and $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_j(t) \right\Vert_2$.


In the following, for a complex row or column vector $z$, $z^T$ denotes the transpose of $z$, representing only transposition without conjugation. The conjugate transpose of $z$ is denoted by $z^H$.



\subsection{The set $\mathcal{W}_j$ and the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$.}

For $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$, we have (recall  (\ref{normQJ0ureal}) and Lemma \ref{Lemmacomplex})
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert Q_{j0}(t)u\right\Vert_2=2f_j\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right\vert \left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_{j}(t,u)\right\Vert_2.  \label{normQJ0u}
\end{equation}
The complex number $ \widehat{w}^{(j)}u $  plays a crucial role in this formula: its modulus is part of it, and the vector $ \widehat{\Theta}_j(t,u) $ is defined in Lemma \ref{Lemmacomplex} by means of  the angle $ \gamma_j(u) $ in the polar form of $ \widehat{w}^{(j)}u $.

For this reason, now we study the set 
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal{W}_j:=\{\widehat{w}^{(j)}u:u\in\mathbb{R}^n\text{\ and\ }\left\Vert
u\right\Vert_2=1\}  \label{set}
\end{equation*}
of the complex numbers $\widehat{w}^{(j)}u$, by varying the unit real vector $u$.

Next proposition describes the set $\mathcal{W}_j$ and states the real induced norm 
\begin{equation*}
	\left\Vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert_\mathbb{R}=\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{R}^{n}  \\ \Vert u\Vert_2 =1
			}}\left\vert w^{\left( j\right) }u\right\vert  
\end{equation*}
of the complex row vector $\widehat{w}^{(j)}$, whose complex induced norm is equal to $1$.

\begin{proposition}
\label{euclidean} Assume that $A$ is a real matrix. Let $j\in\{1,\ldots,q\}$ such
that $\Lambda _{j}$ is complex. Consider, in the complex plane, the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$ centered at the origin with semi-axes the vectors $\sigma_j
\alpha^{(j)}$ and $\mu_j\beta^{(j)}$, where $\sigma_j\geq \mu_j\geq 0$ are
the singular values of the $2\times n$ real matrix 
\begin{equation*}
R_j:=\left[%
\begin{array}{c}
\mathrm{Re}(\widehat{w}^{(j)}) \\ 
\mathrm{Im}(\widehat{w}^{(j)})%
\end{array}%
\right]  \label{matrix}
\end{equation*}
and $\alpha^{(j)},\beta^{(j)}\in\mathbb{R}^2$ are the corresponding left
singular vectors. We have
\begin{equation}
\sigma_j=\sqrt{\frac{1+W_j}{2}}\text{\ \ and\ \ }
\mu_j=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_j%
}{2}},  \label{wR}
\end{equation}
where $W_j$ is the modulus of the complex number $\widehat{w}^{(j)}\left(\widehat{w}^{(j)}\right)^T$.



If $n=2$, the set $\mathcal{W}_j$ is the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$. If $n>2$, the set $\mathcal{W}_j$ is the union of the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$ and its interior. Moreover, we have
\begin{equation*}
\left\Vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert_\mathbb{R}=\sigma_j=\sqrt{\frac{1+W_j}{2}}. \label{maxnormR}
\end{equation*}

\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
We begin by proving (\ref{wR}). The singular values $\sigma_j$ and $\mu_j$ of the matrix $R_j$ are the square roots of the largest
eigenvalue and the smallest eigenvalue, respectively, of the matrix 
\begin{equation*}
R_jR_j^T=\left[%
\begin{array}{c}
\mathrm{Re}(w^{(j)}) \\ 
\mathrm{Im}(w^{(j)})%
\end{array}%
\right]\left[\mathrm{Re}(w^{(j)})^T\ \mathrm{Im}(w^{(j)})^T\right]=\left[ 
\begin{array}{cc}
a_j^2 & p_j \\ 
p_j & b_j^2%
\end{array}%
\right], 
\end{equation*}
where 
\begin{equation*}
a_j=\left\Vert \mathrm{Re}\left( \widehat{w}^{(j)}\right) ^{T}\right\Vert
_{2},\ b_j=\left\Vert \mathrm{Im}\left( \widehat{w}^{(j)}\right)
^{T}\right\Vert _{2}\text{\ \ and\ \ }p_j=\mathrm{Re}\left( \widehat{w}%
^{(j)}\right) \mathrm{Im}\left( \widehat{w}^{(j)}\right) ^{T}.
\end{equation*}
Therefore, we obtain 
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_j^2=\frac{1+\sqrt{\left(a_j^2-b_j^2\right)^2+4p_j^2}}{2}\text{\ and\ }%
\mu_j^2=\frac{1-\sqrt{\left(a_j^2-b_j^2\right)^2+4p_j^2}}{2}.
\end{equation*}
by observing that $a_j^2+b_j^2=\left\Vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}\right\Vert_2^2=1$%
. Since 
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{w}^{(j)}\left(\widehat{w}^{(j)}\right)^T=a_j^2-b_j^2+%
\sqrt{-1}\cdot 2p_j, 
\end{equation*}
we get 
\begin{equation*}
W_j=\sqrt{\left(a_j^2-b_j^2\right)^2+4p_j^2} 
\end{equation*}
and (\ref{wR}) follows.

Now, we pass to describe the set $\mathcal{W}_j$ in terms of the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$. By looking to a complex number as the pair constituted by the real and
imaginary parts, the complex number $\widehat{w}^{(j)}u$, $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$, is $R_ju$. By
introducing the SVD of $R_j$, we see that
\begin{equation*}
R_ju=\sigma_jc_j(u)\alpha^{(j)}+\mu_jd_j(u)\beta^{(j)}
\end{equation*}
where $c_j(u)$ and $d_j(u)$ are the components of $u$ along the first and second right singular vectors of $R_j$, i.e. the right singular vectors corresponding to $\sigma_j$ and $\mu_j$, respectively. Thus,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{W}_j=\{\sigma_jc\alpha^{(j)}+\mu_jd\beta^{(j)}: (c,d)\in\mathbb{R}^2\text{\ and\ }%
\left\Vert (c,d)\right\Vert_2=1\}  \label{setn=2}
\end{equation}
when $n=2$ and
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{W}_j=\{\sigma_jc\alpha^{(j)}+\mu_jd\beta^{(j)}: (c,d)\in\mathbb{R}^2\text{\ and\ }%
\left\Vert (c,d)\right\Vert_2\leq 1\}   \label{setn>2}
\end{equation}
when $n>2$.




In the orthonormal basis of $\mathbb{R}^2$ given by the vector $\alpha^{(j)}$
and $\beta^{(j)}$, the coordinates $x=\sigma_jc$ and $y=\mu_jd$ of the
elements of the set (\ref{setn=2}) satisfies the equation 
\begin{equation*}
\frac{x^2}{\sigma_j^2}+\frac{y^2}{\mu_j^2}=1, 
\end{equation*}
which is the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with lengths of
the semi-axes $\sigma_j$ and $\mu_j$. Thus, we have $\mathcal{W}_j=\mathcal{E}_j$. On the other hand, the coordinates $x=\sigma_jc$ and $y=\mu_jd$ of the
elements of the set (\ref{setn>2}) satisfies the inequality 
\begin{equation*}
\frac{x^2}{\sigma_j^2}+\frac{y^2}{\mu_j^2}\leq 1. 
\end{equation*}
Thus, $\mathcal{W}_j$ is the union of the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$ and its interior.

Finally, we have
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left\Vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert_\mathbb{R}=\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{R}^{n} 
\\ \Vert u\Vert_2 =1}}\left\vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }u\right\vert=\max\limits_{\substack{ u\in \mathbb{R}^{n} 
\\ \Vert u\Vert_2 =1}}\left\Vert R_ju\right\Vert_2=\left\Vert R_j\right\Vert_2=\sigma_j.
\end{eqnarray*}
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{remsigma}
\quad
\begin{itemize}
\item [1.] By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality and Remark \ref{22}, or only by Remark \ref{22}, we have 
\begin{equation*}
W_j=\left\vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) } \left(\widehat{w}^{\left(
j\right) }\right)^T\right\vert = \left\vert \left(\left(\overline{\widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }}\right)^T\right)^H \left(\widehat{w}^{\left(
j\right) }\right)^T
\right\vert\leq 1. 
\end{equation*}
Moreover, $W_j<1$ holds, otherwise $\widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }$ and $%
\overline{\widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }}$ were linearly dependent, which is
impossible since $w^{\left( j\right) }$ and $\overline{w%
^{\left( j\right) }}$ are the rows of the non-singular matrix $W$
corresponding to the eigenvalues $\lambda_j$ and $\overline{\lambda_j}$,
respectively.
\item [2.] Remind the inequality (\ref{r<=c}). By the previous point 1, we have 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left\Vert \widehat{w}^{\left( j\right) }\right\Vert_\mathbb{R}=\sqrt{\frac{1+W_j}{2}}<1
\end{eqnarray*}
for the real induced norm of the row vector $\widehat{w}^{(j)}$. On the other hand,
$$
\left\Vert \widehat{w}^{(j)} \right\Vert_2=1.
$$
for the complex induced norm of $\widehat{w}^{(j)}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{remark}

\subsection{Projection onto the eigenspaces of $\lambda_j$ and $\overline{\lambda}_j$} \label{Pe}

The projection of $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$ onto the sum of the eigenspaces corresponding to $\lambda_j$ and $\overline{\lambda}_j$ is (see Remark \ref{project1})
$$
f_j\left(\widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right)\widehat{v}^{(j)}+f_j\overline{\left(\widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right)}\ \overline{\widehat{v}^{(j)}},
$$
where the first and the second terms are the projections onto the eigenspace corresponding to $\lambda_j$ and $\overline{\lambda_j}$, respectively. Since $\widehat{v}^{(j)}$ and $\overline{\widehat{v}^{(j)}}$ are linearly independent, the projection onto the sum of the eigenspaces is zero if and only if the projection onto the eigenspace corresponding to $\lambda_j$ is zero, i.e. $\widehat{w}^{(j)}u=0$.


The Euclidean norm of the projection onto the eigenspace corresponding to $\lambda_j$ is $f_j\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u \right\vert$, equal to the norm of the projection onto the eigenspace corresponding to $\overline{\lambda_j}$. The Euclidean norm of the projection on the sum of the eigenspaces is given in Remark \ref{project2} below.

\subsection{The right singular vectors of the matrix $R_j$}
Vectors in $\mathbb{R}^n$ can be  expressed by real components in the orthonormal basis of $\mathbb{R}^n$ of the right singular vectors of the matrix $R_j$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean}.

As we saw in the proof of Proposition \ref{euclidean}, for a vector $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$  we have 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{w}^{(j)}u=a+\sqrt{-1}b \label{whatu}
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation*}
	(a,b)=R_ju=\sigma_jc_j(u)\alpha^{(j)}+\mu_jd_j(u)\beta^{(j)},
\end{equation*}
where: $\sigma_j$ and $\mu_j$ are the two singular values of $R_j$; $c_j(u)$ and $d_j(u)$ are the components of $u$ along the first and second, respectively, right singular vectors of $R_j$; and $\alpha^{(j)}$ and $\beta^{(j)}$ are the left singular vectors of $R_j$. 
Therefore, $u$ has projection zero onto the sum of the eigenspaces corresponding to $\lambda_j$ and $\overline{\lambda}_j$, i.e. $\widehat{w}^{(j)}u=0$ by reminding the previous Subsection \ref{Pe}, if and only if  $u$ is spanned by the last $n-2$ right singular vectors of $R_j$.

By (\ref{whatu}), we have
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right\vert=\left\Vert R_ju\right\Vert_2=\sqrt{\sigma_j^2c_j(u)^2+\mu_j^2d_j(u)^2}. \label{size}
\end{equation}      

\begin{remark}
Suppose $n>2$. By considering the components $c_j(u)$ and $d_j(u)$ of $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$ with $\left\Vert u\right\Vert_2=1$ along the first and the second right singular vectors of $R_j$, the set $\mathcal{W}_j$ can be partitioned by level curves of
$$
\sigma_j^2c_j(u)^2+\mu_j^2d_j(u)^2=\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right\vert^2,
$$
whose set of possible values by varying $u$ is $[0,\sigma_j^2]$, or by level curves of
$$
c_j(u)^2+d_j(u)^2,
$$
whose set of possible values is $[0,1]$. In the first case, the level curves are concentric circles with center the origin. In the second case, the level curves are ellipses concentric to the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$.
\end{remark} 


\subsection{Notations}\label{notations}
Before continuing with our analysis, we recall two important notations and introduce a few others.

Here are the two previously introduced notations.
\begin{itemize}
	\item The modulus $W_j$ of the complex number $\widehat{w}^{(j)}\left(\widehat{w}^{(j)}\right)^T$, introduced in Proposition \ref{euclidean}; 
	\item The angle $\gamma_j(u)$ in the polar form of the complex number $\widehat{w}^{(j)}u$, where $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$, introduced In Lemma \ref{gj2}. 
\end{itemize}
Here are the new notations.
\begin{itemize}
	\item The modulus $V_j$ of the complex number $\left(\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }\right) ^{T}%
	\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right)}$.
	\item The angle $\delta_j$ in the polar form of the complex number $\left(\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }\right) ^{T}%
	\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right)}$.
	\item The angle $\theta_j$ in the complex plane between the real axis and the major axis $\sigma_j\alpha^{(j)}$ of the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean}.
	\item
	\begin{equation}
		x_j(t):=2(\omega_{j}t+\theta_j)+\delta_{j}. \label{xt}
	\end{equation}
	\item For $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$, 
	\begin{equation}
		\Delta_j(u):=2(\gamma_{j}(u)-\theta_{j}). \label{xt2}
	\end{equation}	
\end{itemize}
\begin{remark}
	\label{VW}
	\quad
	\begin{itemize}
		\item [1.]  Observe that $\widehat{w}^{(j)}\left(\widehat{w}^{(j)}\right)^H=1$ and $\left(\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }\right) ^{H}%
		\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right)}=1$. On the other hand, $\widehat{w}^{(j)}\left(\widehat{w}^{(j)}\right)^T$ and $\left(\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }\right) ^{T}%
		\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right)}$ are complex numbers and $W_j$ and $V_j$ are their modules, respectively.
		\item [2.] At point 1 of Remark \ref{remsigma}, we have seen that $W_j<1$, The same holds for $V_j$. In fact, by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality we have 
	\begin{equation*}
		V_j=\left\vert \left( \widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }\right) ^{T}\widehat{v}%
		^{\left( j\right) }\right\vert = \left\vert \left( \overline{\widehat{v}%
			^{\left( j\right) }}\right) ^{H}\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right)
		}\right\vert\leq 1. 
	\end{equation*}
	Moreover, $V_j<1$ holds, otherwise $\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }$ and $%
	\overline{\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }}$ were linearly dependent, which is
	impossible since $v^{\left( j\right) }$ and $\overline{v%
		^{\left( j\right) }}$ are the columns of the non-singular matrix $V$
	corresponding to the eigenvalues $\lambda_j$ and $\overline{\lambda_j}$,
	respectively. Therefore, we have $V_j,W_j\in[0,1)$.
			\end{itemize}
	\end{remark}
\subsection{The norm $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left(
t,u\right) \right\Vert _{2}$}

Next proposition provides an expression for  the norm $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left(
t,u\right) \right\Vert _{2}$.


\begin{proposition}
\label{oscillation} Assume that $A$ is a real matrix. Let $j\in\{1,\ldots,q\}$
such that $\Lambda _{j}$ is complex. For $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$, we have 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left( t,u\right) \right\Vert _{2}=\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}\cos \left( x_j(t)+\Delta_j(u)\right)}{2}}, \label{Theta1}
\end{equation}
where $ x_j(t)$ and $\Delta_j(u)$ are defined in (\ref{xt}) and (\ref{xt2}).
\end{proposition}


\begin{proof}
Let 
\begin{equation*}
\left( \widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }\right) ^{T}\widehat{v}^{\left(
j\right) }=V_{j}\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\delta _{j}}
\end{equation*}%
be the polar form of  $\left( \widehat{v}^{\left( j\right)
}\right) ^{T}\widehat{v}^{\left( j\right) }$ (remind Subsection \ref{notations}). We have (remind (\ref{Qj0(t)ucomplex})) 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\left\Vert Q_{j0}(t)u\right\Vert_{2}^{2}=\left\Vert \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}%
\omega_j t}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}u+\overline{\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega_j
t}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}u}\right\Vert _{2}^{2} \notag \\
&&=2\left\Vert \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega_j t}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}u\right\Vert
_{2}^{2}+2\mathrm{Re}\left( \left( \overline{\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega_j
t}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}u}\right) ^{H}\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega
_{j}}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}u\right) \notag \\
&&=2\left\Vert \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega_j t}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}u\right\Vert
_{2}^{2}+2\mathrm{Re}\left( \left( \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega_j
t}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}u\right) ^{T}\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\omega_j
t}v^{(j)}w^{(j)}u\right) \notag \\
&&=2\left(\left\Vert v^{(j)}\right\Vert _{2}^{2}\left\vert
w^{(j)}u\right\vert ^{2}+\mathrm{Re}\left( \mathrm{e}^{2\sqrt{-1}\omega_j
t}\left( \left(v^{(j)}\right)^{T}v^{(j)}\right) \left(w^{(j)}u\right)
^{2}\right) \right) \notag \\
&&=2\left(f_j^2\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u\vert^2+\mathrm{Re}\left(f_j^2\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u\vert^2\mathrm{e}^{2\sqrt{-1}\omega_jt}V_j\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\delta_j}\mathrm{e}^{2\sqrt{-1}\gamma_j(u)}\right)\right)\notag \\
&&=2f_j^{2}\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right\vert ^{2}\left( 1+V_j\cos
\left( 2\omega_j t+\delta_j +2\gamma_j (u)\right) \right)\label{expQ} \\
&&=2f_j^{2}\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right\vert ^{2}\left( 1+V_j\cos \left( x_j(t)+\Delta_j(u)\right)\right).  \notag
\end{eqnarray}%
Now, (\ref{Theta1}) follows by (\ref{normQJ0u}).

\end{proof}




When $V_j=0$, we have
$$
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left( t,u\right) \right\Vert _{2}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}}
$$
On the other hand, when $V_j\neq 0$, $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left( t,u\right) \right\Vert _{2}$, considered as $\frac{\pi}{\omega_j}$-periodic function of $t$, oscillates monotonically between the values
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left( t,u\right)
\right\Vert _{2}=\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}}{2}}\label{maxtheta}
\end{eqnarray}
and
\begin{eqnarray}
	&&\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left(
	t,u\right) \right\Vert _{2}= \sqrt{\frac{1-V_{j}}{2}}. \label{mintheta}
\end{eqnarray}
The maximum value is achieved if and only if $x_j(t)+\Delta_j(u)$ is an even multiple di $\pi$ and the minimum value is achieved if and only if $x_j(t)+\Delta_j(u)$ is an odd multiple di $\pi$.




\begin{remark} \label{project2}
By using $t=0$ in (\ref{expQ}), we obtain 
$$
\left\Vert Q_{j0}(0)u\right\Vert_2=f_j\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right\vert\sqrt{2\left(1+V_j\cos \left(\delta_j +2\gamma_j (u)\right)\right)}.
$$
and this is norm of the projection of $u\in\mathbb{R}^n$ onto the sum of the eigenspaces corresponding to  $\lambda_j$ and $\overline{\lambda_j}$ (remind Remark \ref{project1}). The norm of the projection on each eigenspace is $f_j\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(j)}u\right\vert$ (see Subsection \ref{Pe}).
\end{remark}


\subsection{The functions $f_{VW}$, $f_{VW}^{\max}$ and $f_{VW}^{\min}$} \label{fVWsect}

For the  analysis of  the norm $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left(
t\right) \right\Vert _{2}$, as well as for the analysis of the oscillating terms in Section \ref{OSFOT}, we introduce the functions $f_{VW}$, $f_{VW}^{\max}$ and $f_{VW}^{\min}$, where  $V$ and $W$ are parameters in the interval $[0,1)$. (In this subsection, $V$ is NOT the matrix $V$, with columns the Jordan basis, and W is NOT the inverse of the matrix $V$). We use the symbols $V$ and $W$ because we have in mind the values $V_j,W_j\in[0,1)$.


The function $f_{VW}:\mathbb{R}^2\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is given by
\begin{equation}
	f_{VW}\left(\alpha,x\right):=\frac{1+V\cos\left( x+\alpha\right)}{1-W\cos\alpha}
		,\ \left(\alpha,x\right)\in\mathbb{R}^2. \label{fVW}
\end{equation}
For $x\in\mathbb{R}$, let
\begin{equation*}
	U_{VW}(x):=V\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}x}+W. \label{Uvalue}
\end{equation*}
If $U_{VW}(x)=0$, i.e. $V=W=0$ or $V=W\neq 0$ and $x$ is an odd multiple of $\pi$, then 
$$
f_{VW}\left(\alpha,x\right)=1,\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}.
$$
The next lemma states the form of the $2\pi$-periodic function $f_{VW}(\cdot, x)$, for a given $x \in \mathbb{R}$ such that $U_{VW}(x) \neq 0$.

\begin{lemma}	\label{LemmafvW}Let $V,W\in[0,1)$ and let $x\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $U_{VW}(x)\neq 0$. The function $f_{VW}(\cdot,x)$ is not constant and it oscillates monotonically between its maximum value $f_{VW}(\alpha^{\max}_{VW}(x),x)$
and  minimum value $f_{VW}(\alpha^{\min}_{VW}(x),x)$,  where $\alpha^{\max}_{VW}(x)$ and $\alpha^{\min}_{VW}(x)$ are given by
\begin{equation*}
	\alpha^{\max}_{VW}(x)=\arcsin\frac{VW\sin x}{\vert U_{VW}(x)\vert}-\theta_{VW}(x)
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
	\alpha^{\min}_{VW}(x)=\pi-\arcsin\frac{VW\sin x}{\vert U_{VW}(x)\vert}-\theta_{VW}(x)
\end{equation*}
with $\theta_{VW}(x)$ the angle in the polar form of $U_{VW}(x)$.
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}
		In this proof, to simplify the notation, we write $U(x)$ for $U_{VW}(x)$ and $\theta(x)$  for $\theta_{VW}(x)$.

		We determine the stationary points of $f_{VW}(\cdot,x)$. By deriving $f_{VW}$ with respect to the argument $\alpha$, we obtain
	\begin{equation*}
		\frac{\partial f_{VW}}{\partial\alpha}\left(\alpha,x\right)=\frac{-V\sin(x+\alpha)-W\sin \alpha+VW\sin x}{\left(1-W\cos \alpha\right)^2},\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}.  \label{dalphaf}
\end{equation*}
		Thus, $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}$ is a stationary point of $f_{VW}(\cdot,x)$ if and only if
		\begin{equation}
			V\sin(x+\alpha)+W\sin \alpha=VW\sin x, \label{sinsin}
		\end{equation}
		which can equivalently rewritten as
		$$
		\mathrm{Im}\left(U(x)\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\alpha}\right)=VW\sin x
		$$
		since
		\begin{equation}
			V\sin(x+\alpha)+W\sin \alpha=\mathrm{Im}\left(U(x)\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\alpha}\right) \label{star}
		\end{equation}
		By writing $U(x)=\vert U(x)\vert \mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\theta(x)}$ in polar form, we obtain
		$$
		\sin(\alpha+\theta(x))=\frac{VW\sin x}{\vert U(x)\vert}
		$$
		and then the function $f_{VW}(\cdot,x)$ has the stationary points
		\begin{equation}
			\alpha=\arcsin\frac{VW\sin x}{\vert U(x)\vert}-\theta(x)+2\pi k \label{maxx}
		\end{equation}
		and
		\begin{equation}
			\alpha=\pi-\arcsin\frac{VW\sin x}{\vert U(x)\vert}-\theta(x)+2\pi k, \label{minn}
		\end{equation}
		where $k\in\mathbb{Z}$. In Appendix A, we show that
		\begin{equation}
			\left\vert\frac{VW\sin x}{\vert U(x)\vert}\right\vert=\frac{\vert VW\sin x\vert}{\vert U(x)\vert}< 1.  \label{AppA}
		\end{equation}

		The maximum and the minimum points of $f_{VW}(\cdot,x)$ are these stationary points. Since (recall (\ref{star}))
		$$
		\frac{\partial f_{VW}}{\partial\alpha}\left(\alpha,x\right)=\vert U(x)\vert\frac{-\sin(\alpha+\theta(x))+\frac{VW\sin x}{\vert U(x)\vert}}{\left(1-W\cos \alpha\right)^2},\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R},
		$$
the second derivative of $f_{VW}$ with respect to the argument $\alpha$ is
$$
\frac{\partial^2 f_{VW}}{\partial\alpha^2}\left(\alpha,x\right)=\vert U(x)\vert\frac{-\cos(\alpha+\theta(x))}{\left(1-W\cos \alpha\right)^2},
$$
when $\alpha$ is stationary point. Thus (\ref{maxx}) are maximum points and (\ref{minn}) are minimum points. 
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{diff}
Observe that the functions $\alpha_{VW}^{\max}$ and $\alpha_{VW}^{\min}$ are differentiable at every point where they are defined, i.e. at every point $x\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $U_{VW}(x)\neq 0$. This is a consequence of the fact that  the argument of $\mathrm{arcsin}$ in the definitions of $\alpha_{VW}^{\max}$ and $\alpha_{VW}^{\min}$ in Lemma \ref{LemmafvW} is neither $-1$ nor $1$, as can be seen in (\ref{AppA}).
\end{remark}

The functions $f_{VW}^{\max},f_{VW}^{\min}:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ are given by
\begin{equation}
f_{VW}^{\max}(x)=\max\limits_{\alpha\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}\left(\alpha,x\right),\ x\in\mathbb{R},  \label{fmax}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
f_{VW}^{\min}(x)=\min\limits_{\alpha\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}\left(\alpha,x\right),\ x\in\mathbb{R}.  \label{fmin}
\end{equation}

If $V=0$ or $W=0$, then
\begin{equation}
f_{VW}^{\max}(x)=\frac{1+V}{1-W},\ x\in\mathbb{R},\ \ \label{fVWmaxzero}
\end{equation}
and
$$
f_{VW}^{\min}(x)=\frac{1-V}{1+W},\ x\in\mathbb{R},
$$
are constant functions. Thus, assume $V\neq 0$ and $W\neq 0$. The forms of the $2\pi$-periodic functions $f_{VW}^{\max}$ and $f_{VW}^{\min}$ are stated in the next Lemma.

\begin{lemma} \label{maxminf}
	Let $V,W\in[0,1)$ such that $V\neq 0$ and $W\neq 0$.

	The function $f_{VW}^{\max}$ oscillates monotonically between the values
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		&&\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\max}\left(x\right)
		=\frac{1+V}{1-W}\label{maxPhi}
	\end{eqnarray*}
	and
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		&&\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\max}\left(x\right)
		=\left\{
		\begin{array}{l}
			\frac{1-V}{1-W}\text{\ if\ }V\leq W\\
			\\
			\frac{1+V}{1+W}\text{\ if\ }V\geq W.
		\end{array}
		\right.\label{minPhi}
	\end{eqnarray*}
The maximum value is achieved if and only if $x$ is an even multiple of $\pi$ and the minimum value is achieved if and only if $x$ is an odd multiple of $\pi$.

The function $f_{VW}^{\min}$ oscillates monotonically between the values
	\begin{eqnarray*}
			&&\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\min}\left(x\right)=\left\{
		\begin{array}{l}
			\frac{1+V}{1+W}\text{\ if\ }V\leq W\\
			\\
			\frac{1-V}{1-W}\text{\ if\ }V\geq W
		\end{array}
		\right.
	\end{eqnarray*}
	and
	\begin{eqnarray*}
	&&\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\min}\left(x\right)
	=\frac{1-V}{1+W}.
	\end{eqnarray*}
The maximum value is achieved if and only if $x$ is an odd multiple of $\pi$ and the minimum value is achieved if and only if $x$ is an even multiple of $\pi$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
We determine the stationary points of two-variable function $f_{VW}$. We have, for $(\alpha,x)\in\mathbb{R}^2$,
	\begin{eqnarray}
		&& \frac{\partial f_{VW}}{\partial\alpha}\left(\alpha,x\right)=\frac{-V\sin(x+\alpha)-W\sin \alpha+VW\sin x}{\left(1-W\cos \alpha\right)^2}\notag \\
		&& \frac{\partial f_{VW}}{\partial x}\left(\alpha,x\right)=\frac{-V\sin(x+\alpha)}{1-W\cos \alpha}	\label{partialx}	
	\end{eqnarray}
	Hence, $(\alpha,x)\in\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f_{VW}$ if and only if
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		&&-V\sin(x+\alpha)-W\sin \alpha+VW\sin x=0\\
		&&\sin(x+\alpha)=0,
	\end{eqnarray*}
	which is equivalent to $\alpha$ and $x$ being multiples of $\pi$. 

	In the following table, we see the values of
	$$
	f_{VW}(\alpha,x)=\frac{1+V\cos(x+\alpha)}{1-W\cos\alpha}
	$$
	at the stationary points $(\alpha,x)$.
\begin{equation}
		\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
			\hline
			     & $x$ even  multiple of $\pi$             & $x$ odd multiple of $\pi$\\
			\hline
		    $\alpha$ even multiple of $\pi$ & $\frac{1+V}{1-W}$  &  $\frac{1-V}{1-W}$\\
			\hline
	        $\alpha$ odd  multiple of $\pi$ & $\frac{1-V}{1+W}$ & $\frac{1+V}{1+W}$ \\
			\hline
		\end{tabular} \label{tab}
\end{equation}



	Observe that
	$$
	\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}f^{\max}_{VW}(x)=\max\limits_{(\alpha,x)\in\mathbb{R}^2}f_{VW}(\alpha,x)\text{\ \ and\ \ }
	\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}f^{\min}_{VW}(x)=\min\limits_{(\alpha,x)\in\mathbb{R}^2}f_{VW}(\alpha,x)
	$$
	are values of stationary points of $f_{VW}$. Since
	$$
	\frac{1+V}{1-W}\text{\ \ and\ \ }	\frac{1-V}{1+W}
	$$
	are, respectively, the maximum and the minimum among the values in (\ref{tab}), we conclude that
\begin{equation}
		\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\max}\left(x\right)
	=\frac{1+V}{1-W}\text{\ \ \ and\ \ \ }	\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\min}\left(x\right)
	=\frac{1-V}{1+W}\label{maxminx}
\end{equation}
	with both the maximum and minimum achieved for $x$ being an even multiple of $\pi$.

	Observe that
\begin{equation}
	\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\max}\left(x\right)\text{\ and \ }\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\min}\left(x\right) \label{mm}
\end{equation}
	also are values of stationary points of $f_{VW}$. In fact, for $x\in\mathbb{R}$, we have (remind Lemma \ref{LemmafvW} and Remark \ref{diff})
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		\left(f_{VW}^{\max}\right)^\prime\left(x\right)&=&\frac{d}{dx}f_{VW}(\alpha^{\max}_{VW}(x),x)\\
		&=&\frac{\partial f_{VW}}{\partial \alpha}(\alpha^{\max}_{VW}(x),x)(\alpha^{\max}_{VW})^\prime(x)+\frac{\partial f_{VW}}{\partial x}(\alpha^{\max}_{VW}(x),x)\\
		&=&\frac{\partial f_{VW}}{\partial x}(\alpha^{\max}_{VW}(x),x).
	\end{eqnarray*}
	and, analogously,
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		\left(f_{VW}^{\min}\right)^\prime\left(x\right)=\frac{\partial f_{VW}}{\partial x}(\alpha^{\min}_{VW}(x),x).
	\end{eqnarray*}
	Therefore, the values (\ref{mm}) are those in (\ref{tab}) different from (\ref{maxminx}), i.e. those in the second column.

	Indeed, when $V = W$, $x$ cannot be an odd multiple of $\pi$ in the previous argument, since $\alpha^{\max}_{VW}(x)$ and $\alpha^{\min}_{VW}(x)$ are not defined in this case. However, when $V=W$, we can still conclude that the values in (\ref{mm}) are stationary points of $f_{VW}$. In fact, for $x \in \mathbb{R}$ that is not an odd multiple of $\pi$, the previous argument works and then $\left(f_{VW}^{\max}\right)^\prime(x)$ and $\left(f_{VW}^{\min}\right)^\prime(x)$ are zero only when $x$ is a multiple of $\pi$, indeed an even multiple of $\pi$. Thus, the maximum and minimum values of $\left(f_{VW}^{\max}\right)(x)$ and $\left(f_{VW}^{\min}\right)(x)$ as $x$ varies can be achieved only for $x$ being a multiple of $\pi$. Now, we immediately conclude that the values in (\ref{mm}) are stationary points of $f_{VW}$. 

	When $V=W$, the values in the second column of (\ref{maxminx}) are both equal to $1$ and then the values (\ref{mm}) are both equal to $1$ and both achieved for $x$ being an odd multiple of $\pi$. 

	Suppose $V\neq W$. Since
	\begin{equation}
	\frac{\partial^2 f_{VW}}{\partial \alpha^2}\left(\alpha,x\right)=\frac{-V\cos(x+\alpha)-W\cos\alpha}{(1-W\cos \alpha)^2} \label{delta2fVW}
	\end{equation}
	at a stationary point $(\alpha,x)$, we obtain the following table of the values of $\frac{\partial^2 f_{VW}}{\partial \alpha^2}(\alpha,x)$ at stationary points.
		$$
	\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
		\hline
		& $x$ even  multiple of $\pi$             & $x$ odd multiple of $\pi$\\
		\hline
		$\alpha$ even multiple of $\pi$ & $-\frac{V+W}{(1-W)^2}$  &  $\frac{V-W}{(1-W)^2}$\\
		\hline
		$\alpha$ odd  multiple of $\pi$ & $\frac{V+W}{(1+W)^2}$ & $\frac{-V+W}{(1+W)^2}$ \\
		\hline
	\end{tabular}
	$$
	where a positive value indicates that $\alpha$ is a minimum point of the function $f_{VW}(\cdot,x)$ and then $f_{VW}(\alpha,x)$ is a value of $f^{\min}_{VW}$, while a negative value indicates  that $\alpha$ is a maximum point and then $f_{VW}(\alpha,x)$ is a value of $f^{\max}_{VW}$. This confirms (\ref{maxminx}) and shows that if $V<W$, then 
	$$
	\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\max}\left(x\right)
	=\frac{1-V}{1-W}\text{\ \ and\ \ }\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\min}\left(x\right)
	=\frac{1+V}{1+W},	
	$$
and if $V>W$, then 
	$$
	\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\max}\left(x\right)
	=\frac{1+V}{1+W}\text{\ \ and\ \ }\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}} f_{VW}^{\min}\left(x\right)
	=\frac{1-V}{1-W}.	
	$$
	with both the maximum and minimum achieved for $x$ being an odd multiple of $\pi$.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
	When $x$ is an even multiple of $\pi$, $f_{VW}^{\max}(x)$ and $f_{VW}^{\min}(x)$ are at their maximum value  and  minimum value, respectively. On the other hand, when $x$ is an odd multiple of $\pi$, $f_{VW}^{\max}(x)$ and $f_{VW}^{\min}(x)$ are at their minimum value  and maximum value, respectively.
\end{remark} 

\subsection{The norm $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left(
	t\right) \right\Vert _{2}$}


Next proposition provides an expression for the norm $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left(
t\right) \right\Vert _{2}$.


\begin{proposition}
\label{oscillation2} Assume that $A$ is a real matrix. Let $j\in\{1,\ldots,q\}$
such that $\Lambda _{j}$ is complex. We have
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left( t\right) \right\Vert _{2}=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_j^2}{4}f_{V_jW_j}^{\max}\left(x_j(t)\right)},  \label{Theta2}
\end{equation}
where $f_{V_jW_j}^{\max}$ is defined in the previous Subsection \ref{fVWsect} and $x_j(t)$ is defined in (\ref{xt}).
\end{proposition}



\begin{proof}
By (\ref{A==}), Proposition \ref{euclidean} and Proposition \ref{oscillation}, we have
\begin{equation*}
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t\right) \right\Vert_2 =\max\limits_{\rho\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\theta}\in \mathcal{W}_j}\rho\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}\cos \left( x_{j}(t)+2(\theta-\theta_j)\right) }{2}},
\end{equation*}
where $\mathcal{W}_j$ is the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean} when $n=2$ and $\mathcal{W}_j$ is the union of $\mathcal{E}_j$ and its interior when $n>2$.
We now show that (\ref{Theta2}) holds.

Suppose $n>2$. By using, in the complex plane, the cartesian coordinate with axes the axes of the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_j$, the set $\mathcal{W}_j$ is the set of point $(x,y)$ such that
$$
\left(\frac{x}{\sigma_j}\right)^2+\left(\frac{y}{\mu_j}\right)^2\leq 1,
$$
where $\sigma_j$ and $\mu_j$ are the lenghts of the axes of the ellipse. Thus 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\rho\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\theta}\in\mathcal{W}_j &\Leftrightarrow& \left(\frac{\rho\cos(\theta-\theta_j)}{\sigma_j}\right)^2+\left(\frac{\rho\sin(\theta-\theta_j)}{\mu_j}\right)^2\leq 1\\
&\Leftrightarrow& \rho^2\leq R_j\left(\theta\right)^2,
\end{eqnarray*}
where
\begin{equation}
R_j\left(\theta\right):=\sqrt{\frac{\sigma_j^2\mu_j^2}{\mu^2_j\cos^2(\theta-\theta_j)+\sigma^2_j\sin^2(\theta-\theta_j)}}.  \label{Rtheta}
\end{equation}
Thus
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t\right) \right\Vert_2=\max\limits_{\rho\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\theta}\in \mathcal{W}_j}\rho\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}\cos \left( x_{j}(t)+2(\theta-\theta_j)\right)}{2}}\notag \\
&&=\max\limits_{\theta\in[0,2\pi]}\max\limits_{\rho\in\left[0,R_j\left(\theta\right)\right]}
\rho\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}\cos\left(  x_{j}(t)+2(\theta-\theta_j)\right) }{2}}\notag\\
&&=\max\limits_{\theta\in[0,2\pi]}R_j\left(\theta\right)\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}\cos\left( x_{j}(t)+2(\theta-\theta_j)\right) }{2}}. \label{cos}
\end{eqnarray*}
In the case $n=2$, we have
$$
\rho\mathrm{e}^{\sqrt{-1}\theta}\in\mathcal{W}_j\Leftrightarrow  \rho^2= R_j\left(\theta\right)^2
$$
and then
$$
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t\right) \right\Vert_2=\max\limits_{\theta\in[0,2\pi]}R_j\left(\theta\right)\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}\cos\left( x_{j}(t)+2(\theta-\theta_j)\right) }{2}}
$$
in this case as well.

Recall (\ref{wR}). In (\ref{Rtheta}), we have
$$
\sigma_j^2\mu_j^2=\frac{1-W^2_j}{4}
$$
and, by introducing $\alpha=2(\theta-\theta_j)$, 
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\mu^2_j\cos^2(\theta-\theta_j)+\sigma^2_j\sin^2(\theta-\theta_j)\\
&&=\mu^2_j\cos^2\frac{\alpha}{2}+\sigma^2_j\sin^2\frac{\alpha}{2}\\
&&=\mu^2_j\frac{1+\cos\alpha}{2}+\sigma_j^2\frac{1-\cos\alpha}{2}\\
&&=\frac{\mu^2_j+\sigma^2_j+\left(\mu^2_j-\sigma^2_j\right)\cos\alpha}{2}\\
&&=\frac{1-W_j\cos\alpha}{2}.
\end{eqnarray*}
Now, we can write
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_j\left( t\right) \right\Vert_2=\max\limits_{\theta\in[0,2\pi]}R_j\left(\theta\right)\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}\cos\left( x_{j}(t)+2(\theta-\theta_j)\right)}{2}}\\
&&=\max\limits_{\alpha\in[-2\theta_j,2(2\pi-\theta_j)]}\sqrt{\frac{1-W_j^2}{2\left(1-W_j\cos\alpha\right)}}\cdot\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}\cos\left( x_j(t)+\alpha\right)}{2}}\\
&&=\max\limits_{\alpha\in[0,2\pi]}\sqrt{\frac{1-W_j^2}{2\left(1-W_j\cos\alpha\right)}}\cdot\sqrt{\frac{1+V_{j}\cos\left( x_j(t)+\alpha\right)}{2}},
\end{eqnarray*}
i.e. (\ref{Theta2}) by recalling the definition (\ref{fmax})-(\ref{fVW}) of $f^{\max}_{V_jW_j}$. 
\end{proof}

Recall Lemma \ref{maxminf} (and the observation just before it) and the previous Proposition \ref{oscillation2}. When $V_j=0$, we have
$$
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left( t\right) \right\Vert _{2}=\sqrt{\frac{1+W_j}{4}}.
$$
When $W_j=0$, we have
$$
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left( t\right) \right\Vert _{2}=\sqrt{\frac{1+V_j}{4}}.
$$
On the other hand, when $V_j\neq 0$ and $W_j\neq 0$, $\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left( t\right) \right\Vert _{2}$, considered as a $\frac{\pi}{\omega_j}$-periodic function of $t$, oscillates monotonically between the values
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left( t\right)
\right\Vert _{2}=\sqrt{\frac{(1+W_j)(1+V_{j})}{4}} \label{maxTheta}
\end{eqnarray}
and
\begin{eqnarray}
	&&\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \left\Vert \widehat{\Theta }_{j}\left(
	t\right) \right\Vert _{2}= \sqrt{\frac{a(V_j,W_j)}{4}}, \label{minTheta}
\end{eqnarray}
where
$$
a(V_j,W_j):=\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
(1+W_j)(1-V_j)\text{\ if\ }V_j\leq W_j\\
\\
(1-W_j)(1+V_j)\text{\ if\ }V_j\geq W_j.
\end{array}
\right.
$$
The maximum value is achieved if and only if $x_j(t)$ is an even multiple di $\pi$ and the minimum value  is achieved if and only if $x_j(t)$ is an odd multiple di $\pi$.




\section{The generic real case. Part III: asymptotic condition numbers}




In this section, we describe the asymptotic condition numbers $K_{\infty}(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$ and $K_{\infty}(t,y_0)$ for a real matrix $A$ and in a
generic situation for $A$, $y_0$ and $\widehat{z}_0$. This description is given in the next theorem.
\begin{theorem}
\label{Ar1} Assume that $A$ is a real matrix, $\Lambda_1$ consists of either a real simple eigenvalue(i.e. $\Lambda_1$ is real), or a single pair of complex
conjugate smple eigenvalues (i.e. $\Lambda_1$ is complex), and $w^{(1)}y_{0}\neq 0$ and $w^{(1)}\widehat{z}%
_{0}\neq 0$. We have (recall Subsections \ref{unitvectors} and \ref{thenumbers} )
\begin{equation}
K_{\infty}(t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})=\frac{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}%
\widehat{z}_{0}\right\vert}{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}%
_{0}\right\vert}\cdot\frac{g_1\left(t,\widehat{z}_0\right)}{g_1\left(t,%
\widehat{y}_0\right)}  \label{first}
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
K_\infty(t,y_{0})=\frac{1}{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}%
_{0}\right\vert}\cdot\frac{g_1\left(t\right)}{g_1\left(t,\widehat{y}_0\right)%
}.  \label{second}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By reminding that $v^{(1)}w^{(1)}$ is the projection onto the eigenspace of $\lambda_1$, the projections of $y_0$ and $\widehat{z}_0$ onto the eigenspace of $\lambda_1$ are   
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&v^{(1)}w^{(1)}y_{0}=\left( w^{(1)}y%
_{0}\right) v^{(1)}\neq 0
\end{eqnarray*}
and
\begin{eqnarray*}
	&&v^{(1)}w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_{0}=\left( w^{(1)}\widehat{z%
	}_{0}\right) v^{(1)}\neq 0,
\end{eqnarray*}%
respectively, and then $j^{\ast }=j^{\ast \ast }=1$. By points 2 of Remarks \ref{KtAyz} and \ref{KtAy}, we have
\begin{equation*}
K_\infty\left( t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0}\right)=\frac{\left\Vert
Q_{10}(t)\widehat{z}_{0}\right\Vert }{\left\Vert Q_{10}(t)%
\widehat{y}_{0}\right\Vert }.
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
K_\infty\left( t,y_{0}\right) = \frac{\left\Vert Q_{10}(t)\right\Vert }{\left\Vert Q_{10}(t)\widehat{y}_{0}\right\Vert }.
\end{equation*}
Now, (\ref{first}) and (\ref{second}) follows by (\ref{normQJ0ureal}) and (\ref{normQJ0}).
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}\label{remof}
The previous theorem considers a generic situation for the real
matrix $A$, the initial value $y_0$ and the direction of perturbation $%
\widehat{z}_0$. In fact, as previously noted, it is a generic situation
for $A$ real to have, for any $j\in\{1,\ldots,q\}$, $\Lambda_j$ consisting of either a
real simple eigenvalue, or a single pair of complex conjugate simple
eigenvalues. Moreover it is a generic situation for $y_0$ to satisfy $%
w^{(1)}y_{0}\neq 0$ and it is a generic situation for $\widehat{z}_0$ to
satisfy $w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_{0}\neq 0$.
\end{remark}


Now, we describe the result of the previous theorem by separating the two cases of $\Lambda_1$real and $\Lambda_1$ complex.

\begin{itemize}
\item If $\Lambda_1$ is real,
then 
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&K_\infty(t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})=\frac{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}%
\widehat{z}_{0}\right\vert }{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}%
_{0}\right\vert }\notag \\
&&K_\infty(t,y_{0})=\frac{1}{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}%
_{0}\right\vert }\label{K2}
\end{eqnarray*}
by Lemma \ref{Lemmareal}.
\item If $\Lambda_1$ is complex, then 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&K_\infty(t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})=\frac{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}%
\widehat{z}_{0}\right\vert }{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}%
_{0}\right\vert }\cdot \frac{\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_1
\left( t,\widehat{z}_{0}\right) \right\Vert }{\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_1
\left( t,\widehat{y}_{0}\right) \right\Vert }  \label{KK1}\\
&&K_\infty(t,y_{0})= \frac{1}{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}%
_{0}\right\vert }\cdot \frac{\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_1
\left( t\right) \right\Vert }{\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_1
\left( t,\widehat{y}_{0}\right) \right\Vert } \label{KK2}
\end{eqnarray}
by Lemma \ref{Lemmacomplex}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The oscillation scale factors and the oscillating terms}

Consider the case where $\Lambda_1$ is complex. We can rewrite (\ref{KK1}) and (\ref{KK2}) as
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&K_\infty(t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})=\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z_0})\cdot\mathrm{OT}(t,y_0,\widehat{z_0})\notag \\
&&K_\infty(t,y_{0})=\mathrm{OSF}(y_0)\cdot\mathrm{OT}(t,y_0), \label{KK22}
\end{eqnarray*}
where the constants 
\begin{eqnarray}
&&\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z_0}):=\frac{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{z}_{0}\right\vert }{\left\vert 
\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}\right\vert }\label{OSFz}\\
&&\mathrm{OSF}(y_0):=\frac{1}{%
\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}\right\vert }\label{OSF}
\end{eqnarray}
are called the \emph{oscillation scale factors}
and the functions
\begin{eqnarray}
&&t\mapsto \mathrm{OT}(t,y_0,\widehat{z_0}):=\frac{\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_1 \left( t,\widehat{z}%
_{0}\right) \right\Vert }{\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_1
\left( t,\widehat{y}_{0}\right) \right\Vert },\ t\in\mathbb{R}, \label{OTz}\\
&&t\mapsto \mathrm{OT}(t,y_0):=\frac{\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_1 \left( t\right) \right\Vert }{%
\left\Vert \widehat{\Theta}_1 \left( t,\widehat{y}%
_{0}\right) \right\Vert },\ t\in\mathbb{R}, \label{OT}
\end{eqnarray}
which are  periodic functions of period $\frac{\pi}{\omega_1}$, are called the \emph{oscillating terms}.


\begin{remark} \label{OSFOTRemark}

	The oscillation scale factor $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0, \widehat{z}_0)$ and the oscillating term $\mathrm{OT}\left(t, y_0, \widehat{z}_0\right)$ are two completely unrelated quantities, separating moduli and angles in the polar forms of $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}$ and $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{z}_{0}$. Specifically, $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0, \widehat{z}_0)$ depends on $y_0$ and $\widehat{z}_0$ through the moduli of the complex numbers $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}$ and $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{z}_{0}$. On the other hand, $\mathrm{OT}\left(t, y_0, \widehat{z}_0\right)$ depends on $y_0$ and $\widehat{z}_0$ through the angles $\gamma_1(\widehat{y}_0)$ and $\gamma_1(\widehat{z}_0)$ in these polar forms (refer to the definition of $\widehat{\Theta}(t, u)$ in Lemma \ref{Lemmacomplex}).

	Similarly, $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0)$ depends on $y_0$ through the modulus of the complex number $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}$ and $\mathrm{OT}\left(t, y_0\right)$ depends on $y_0$ through the angle $\gamma_1(\widehat{y}_0)$ in this polar form.
\end{remark}


\section{The generic real case. Part IV: oscillation scale factors and oscillating terms in case of the euclidean norm}

\label{OSFOT}

In this section, we study the oscillating scale factors and the oscillating terms in case of the Euclidean norm as vector norm.

\subsection{The oscillation scale factor $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$}
The oscillating scale factor\\ $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$ in (\ref{OSFz}) is the ratio of the modules of the non-zero complex numbers $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}$ and $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{z}_{0}$. In case of the Euclidean norm as vector norm, such complex numbers can be arbitrary non-zero points in the set $\mathcal{W}_1$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean}.


Thus, as $y_0$ and $\widehat{z}_0$ vary, $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$ can be any number in
$(0,+\infty)$ when $n>2$ and any number in
$$
\left[\frac{\mu_1}{\sigma_1},\frac{\sigma_1}{\mu_1}\right]=\left[\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1}{1+W_1}},\sqrt{\frac{1+W_1}{1-W_1}}\right],
$$
where $\sigma_1$ and $\mu_1$ are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_1$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean}, when $n=2$.

\begin{remark} 
By (\ref{size}), we can express $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$ in terms of the components $c_1(u)$ and $d_1(u)$ of $u=\widehat{y}_0,\widehat{z}_0$  along the first two right singular vectors of the matrix $R_1$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean}. We have
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\sqrt{\frac{(1+W_1)c_1(\widehat{z}_0)^2+(1-W_1)d_1(\widehat{z}_0)^2}{(1+W_1)c_1(\widehat{y}_0)^2+(1-W_1)d_1(\widehat{y}_0)^2}}. \label{OSFz1}
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

\subsection{The oscillating term $\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)$}
In case of the Euclidean norm as vector norm, by reminding Proposition \ref{oscillation}, we can write the oscillating term (\ref{OTz}) as
\begin{equation*}
	\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)
	=\sqrt{f_{V_1V_1}\left(\alpha,x\right)},  \label{OTyz}
\end{equation*}
where $f_{V_1V_1}$ is the function defined in (\ref{fVW}), 
\begin{equation*}
\alpha=x_1(t)+\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0)+\pi \label{alpha},
\end{equation*}
with $x_1(t)$ and $\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0)$ defined in (\ref{xt}) and (\ref{xt2}), and
\begin{equation*}
x=\Delta_1(\widehat{z}_0)-\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0)-\pi=2\left(\gamma
_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma
_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)\right)-\pi. \label{x}
\end{equation*}


In the case $V_1=0$, we have
$$
\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=1.
$$
Therefore, we assume $V_1\neq 0$. We are interested to the maximum and minimum values 
\begin{equation}
\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{f_{V_1V_1}^{\max}\left(x\right)} \label{maxyz}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
	\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{f_{V_1V_1}^{\min}\left(x\right)} \label{minyz}
\end{equation}
of the oscillating term, where $f_{V_1V_1}^{\max}$ and $f_{V_1V_1}^{\min}$ are defined in (\ref{fmax}) and (\ref{fmin}). Observe that (\ref{maxyz}) and (\ref{minyz}) hold since
$$
\alpha=x_1(t)+\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0)+\pi=2(\omega_{1}t+\gamma_1(\widehat{y}_0))+\delta_1+\pi
$$
varies in $\mathbb{R}$, as $t$ varies in $\mathbb{R}$.

Given $y_0$, we do not determine (\ref{maxyz}) and (\ref{minyz}) for any $\widehat{z}_0$ but we look only at their maximum and minimum values, by varying $\widehat{z}_0$. The next theorem gives such maximum and minumum values. They are independent of $y_0$.


\begin{theorem}
Assume that $A$ is a real matrix, $\Lambda_1$ is complex and $w^{(1)}y_{0}\neq 0$. Assume $V_1\neq 0$.

We have
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\max\limits_{\substack{\widehat{z}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ \left\Vert \widehat{z}_0\right\Vert_2=1\\ w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0\neq 0}}\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1+V_1}{1-V_1}}
\end{eqnarray*}
and
\begin{eqnarray*}
	&&\min\limits_{\substack{\widehat{z}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ \left\Vert \widehat{z}_0\right\Vert_2=1\\ w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0\neq 0}}\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=1.
\end{eqnarray*}
The maximum value is achieved if and and only if $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$ and the minimum value is achieved if and and only if $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)$ is a multiple of $\pi$.

Moreover, we have
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\max\limits_{\substack{\widehat{z}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ \left\Vert \widehat{z}_0\right\Vert_2=1\\ w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0\neq 0}}\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=1
\end{eqnarray*}
and
\begin{eqnarray*}
	&&\min\limits_{\substack{\widehat{z}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ \left\Vert \widehat{z}_0\right\Vert_2=1\\ w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0\neq 0}}\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-V_1}{1+V_1}},
\end{eqnarray*}
The maximum value is achieved if and and only if $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)$ is multiple of $\pi$ and  the minimum value is achieved if and and only if $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
As $\widehat{z}_0$ varies in $\mathbb{R}^n$ with $\Vert \widehat{z}_0\Vert_2=1$ and $w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0\neq 0$, $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0$ can be an arbitrary non-zero point in the set $\mathcal{W}_1$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean}. Then, $\gamma_1\left(\widehat{z}_0\right)$ varies in $[0,2\pi]$ and then
$$
x=2\left(\gamma
_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma
_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)\right)-\pi
$$
in (\ref{maxyz})-(\ref{minyz}) varies in $[-2\gamma_1\left(\widehat{y}_0\right)-\pi,-2\gamma_1\left(\widehat{y}_0\right)+3\pi]$, over two periods of the functions $f_{V_1V_1}^{\max}$ and $f_{V_1V_1}^{\min}$. We conclude that
\begin{eqnarray*}
	&&\max\limits_{\substack{\widehat{z}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ \left\Vert \widehat{z}_0\right\Vert_2=1\\ w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0\neq 0}}\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}f^{\max}_{V_1V_1}(x)}\\
	&&\min\limits_{\substack{\widehat{z}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ \left\Vert \widehat{z}_0\right\Vert_2=1\\ w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0\neq 0}}\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}f^{\max}_{V_1V_1}(x)}\\
	&&\max\limits_{\substack{\widehat{z}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ \left\Vert \widehat{z}_0\right\Vert_2=1\\ w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0\neq 0}}\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}f^{\min}_{V_1V_1}(x)}\\
	&&\min\limits_{\substack{\widehat{z}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ \left\Vert \widehat{z}_0\right\Vert_2=1\\ w^{(1)}\widehat{z}_0\neq 0}}\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}f^{\min}_{V_1V_1}(x)}.
\end{eqnarray*}
Now, the theorem follows by Lemma \ref{maxminf}.
\end{proof}




\begin{remark} \label{imprem1}
	\quad
	\begin{itemize}
	\item[1.] 	If $V_1$ is not close to $1$, then, for any $y_0$ and $\widehat{z}_0$, $\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)$ does not assume large or small  values, as $t$ varies. In fact, we have
		$$
		\sqrt{\frac{1-V_1}{1+V_1}}\leq \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)\leq \sqrt{\frac{1+V_1}{1-V_1}}.
		$$
\item [2.] When $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)$ is a multiple of $\pi$, we have
\begin{equation*}
\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=1=\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right) \label{maxminz}
\end{equation*}
and then
$$
\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=1,
$$
as it can be directly verified by the definition of $\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)$ and Proposition \ref{oscillation}.


\item [3.] When $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$, we have
\begin{equation}
\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1+V_1}{1-V_1}} \label{maxmaxz1}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
	\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-V_1}{1+V_1}} \label{maxmaxz2}
\end{equation}
with $\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)$  at its maximum value and $\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)$ at its minimum value, as $\widehat{z}_0$ varies.

\item [4.] If $V_1$ is close to $1$ and $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$, then $\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)$ assumes both large and small values, as $t$ varies. This follows by (\ref{maxmaxz1}) and (\ref{maxmaxz2}).  

\item [5.] The condition $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$ is equivalent to saying that the vectors $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}$ and $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{z}_{0}$ are orthogonal in the complex plane, i.e.
$$
(1+W_1)c_1(\widehat{y}_0)c_1(\widehat{z}_0)+(1-W_1)d_1(\widehat{y}_0)d_1(\widehat{z}_0)=0
$$
by reminding (\ref{whatu}).

The condition $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{z}_0)-\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)$ is a multiple of $\pi$ is equivalent to saying that the vectors $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}$ and $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{z}_{0}$ are parallel in the complex plane, i.e.
$$
c_1(\widehat{y}_0)d_1(\widehat{z}_0)-d_1(\widehat{y}_0)c_1(\widehat{z}_0)=0.
$$
\item [6.] Some specific situations are worth considering.
\begin{itemize}
	\item [a)] If $y_0$ is orthogonal to the first right singular vector of the matrix $R_1$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean} and $\widehat{z}_0$ is orthogonal to the second right singular vector (i.e. $c_1(\widehat{y}_0)=0$ and $d_1(\widehat{z}_0)=0$), then, by previous points 5 and 3, (\ref{maxmaxz1})-(\ref{maxmaxz2}) hold. Moreover, by (\ref{OSFz1}) we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\sqrt{\frac{1+W_1}{1-W_1}}\cdot \frac{\vert c_1(\widehat{z}_0)\vert}{\vert d_1(\widehat{y}_0)\vert}.
	\end{equation*}
	If $V_1$ is close to $1$,  then $\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)$ assumes both large and small values, as $t$ varies. If $W_1$ is close to $1$, it is expected to have $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$ large.  Regarding the asymptotic condition number
	$$
	K_\infty(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)\cdot \mathrm{OT}(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0),
	$$
	it is expected that it assumes  large values, as $t$ varies, if $V_1$ is close to $1$ or $W_1$ is close to $1$: we have
	\begin{equation}
	\max\limits_{t\in  \mathbb{R}}K_\infty(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\sqrt{\frac{(1+W_1)(1+V_1)}{(1-W_1)(1-V_1)}}\cdot \frac{\vert c_1(\widehat{z}_0)\vert}{\vert d_1(\widehat{y}_0)\vert}. \label{Kyz1}
	\end{equation}
	\item [b)] If $y_0$ is orthogonal to the second right singular vector of $R_1$ and $\widehat{z}_0$ is orthogonal to the first right singular vector (i.e. $d_1(\widehat{y}_0)=0$ and $c_1(\widehat{z}_0)=0$), then
	(\ref{maxmaxz1})-(\ref{maxmaxz2}) hold again. Moreover, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1}{1+W_1}}\cdot \frac{\vert d_1(\widehat{z}_0)\vert}{\vert c_1(\widehat{y}_0)\vert}.
	\end{equation*}
		If $V_1$ is close to $1$,  then $\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)$ assumes both large and small values, as $t$ varies. If $W_1$ is close to $1$, it is expected to have $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$ small. Regarding the asymptotic condition number $K_\infty(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$, it is expected that it assumes  large values, as $t$ varies, if the ratio $\frac{1-W_1}{1-V_1}$ is large: we have
		\begin{equation}
		\max\limits_{t\in  \mathbb{R}}K_\infty(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\sqrt{\frac{(1-W_1)(1+V_1)}{(1+W_1)(1-V_1)}}\cdot \frac{\vert d_1(\widehat{z}_0)\vert}{\vert c_1(\widehat{y}_0)\vert}. \label{Kyz2}
		\end{equation}

	\item [c)] If $y_0$ and $\widehat{z}_0$ are orthogonal to the first right singular vector of $R_1$ (i.e. $c_1(\widehat{y}_0)=0$ and $c_1(\widehat{z}_0)=0$), then, by previous point 2 and 5, $\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=1$.
	Moreover, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		K_\infty(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\frac{\vert d_1(\widehat{z}_0)\vert}{\vert d_1(\widehat{y}_0)\vert}.
	\end{equation*}
	\item [d)] If $y_0$ and $\widehat{z}_0$ are orthogonal to the second right singular vector of $R_1$ (i.e. $d_1(\widehat{y}_0)=0$ and $d_1(\widehat{z}_0)=0$), then $\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0\right)=1$. 
	Moreover, we have
	\begin{equation*}
		K_\infty(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\mathrm{OSF}(y_0,\widehat{z}_0)=\frac{\vert c_1(\widehat{z}_0)\vert}{\vert c_1(\widehat{y}_0)\vert}.
	\end{equation*}
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
\end{remark}


\subsection{The oscillation scale factor $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0)$}



The oscillating scale factor $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0)$ in (\ref{OSF}) is the reciprocal of the modulus  of the complex number $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}$. In case of the Euclidean norm as vector norm, such complex number can be an arbitrary non-zero point in the set $\mathcal{W}_1$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean}.


Thus, by varying $y_0$, $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0)$ can be any number in
$$
\left[\frac{1}{\sigma_1},+\infty\right)=\left[\sqrt{\frac{2}{1+W_1}},+\infty\right)
$$
when $n>2$ and any number in
$$
\left[\frac{1}{\sigma_1},\frac{1}{\mu_1}\right]=\left[\sqrt{\frac{2}{1+W_1}},\sqrt{\frac{2}{1-W_1}}\right].
$$
when $n=2$.
\begin{remark}
By (\ref{size}), we can express $\mathrm{OSF}(y_0)$ in terms of the components $c_1(\widehat{y}_0)$ and $d_1(\widehat{y}_0)$ of $\widehat{y}_0$ along the first two right singular vectors of the matrix $R_1$. We have
	\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{OSF}(y_0)=\sqrt{\frac{2}{(1+W_1)c_1(\widehat{y}_0)^2+(1-W_1)d_1(\widehat{y}_0)^2}}. \label{OSF2}
\end{equation}
\end{remark}


\subsection{The oscillating term $\mathrm{OT}(t,y_0)$}

In case of the Euclidean norm as vector norm, by reminding Propositions \ref{oscillation} and \ref{oscillation2}, we can write the oscillating term (\ref{OT}) as
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{OT}\left(t,\widehat{y}_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}\cdot\frac{f_{V_1W_1}^{\max}(x_1(t))}{1+V_1\cos(x_1(t)+\Delta_1(\widehat{y_0}))}}, \label{OTy}
\end{equation}
where $x_1(t)$ and $\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0)$ are defined in (\ref{xt}) and (\ref{xt2}).

In the case $V_1=0$, we have 
$$
\mathrm{OT}\left(t,\widehat{y}_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1+W_1}{2}}
$$
by reminding (\ref{fVWmaxzero}). Therefore, we assume $V_1\neq 0$. We are interested to the maximum and minimum values 
\begin{equation}
	\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)
	=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}\cdot\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}\frac{f_{V_1W_1}^{\max}(x)}{1+V_1\cos(x+\Delta_1(\widehat{y_0}))}} \label{maxy}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
	\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)
	=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}\cdot\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}\frac{f_{V_1W_1}^{\max}(x)}{1+V_1\cos(x+\Delta_1(\widehat{y_0}))}}. \label{miny}
\end{equation} 
of the oscillating term. Observe that (\ref{maxy}) and (\ref{miny}) hold since $x_1(t)$ varies in $\mathbb{R}$, as $t$ varies in $\mathbb{R}$. In the case $V_1\neq 0$ and $W_1= 0$, we have
\begin{eqnarray*}
	&&\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1+V_1}{2(1-V_1)}}\\
	&&\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}},
\end{eqnarray*}
by reminding (\ref{fVWmaxzero}). Observe that, for $V_1=0$ or $W_1=0$, such maximum and minimum are independent of $y_0$.

We proceed by assuming  $V_1\neq 0$ and $W_1\neq 0$. We do not determine (\ref{maxy}) and (\ref{miny}) for any $y_0$, but we look only at their maximum and minimum values as $y_0$ varies.
The next theorem gives such maximum and minumum values.




\begin{theorem} \label{formOTyz}
	Assume that $A$ is a real matrix and $\Lambda_1$ is complex. Assume $V_1\neq 0$ and $W_1\neq 0$.

	We have
	\begin{eqnarray}
	&&\max\limits_{\substack{{y}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ w^{(1)} y_0\neq 0}}\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)=a_{\max}(V_1,W_1):=\sqrt{\frac{(1+W_1)(1+V_1)}{2(1-V_1)}} \label{MAX}
\end{eqnarray}
and
\begin{eqnarray}
	&&	\min\limits_{\substack{{y}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ w^{(1)} y_0\neq 0}}\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)=\left\{
	\begin{array}{l}
		\sqrt{\frac{1+W_1}{2}\cdot\frac{1-V_1^2}{1-Q_1V_1}}\text{\ if\ }V_1\leq\frac{2W_1}{1+W_1}\\
		\\
		\sqrt{\frac{(1-W_1)(1+V_1)}{2(1-V_1)}}\text{\ if\ }\frac{2W_1}{1+W_1}\leq V_1,
	\end{array}
	\right.  \label{MINMAX}
\end{eqnarray}
	where
	\begin{equation}
	Q_1:=\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{V_1}{W_1}(1+W_1). \label{Quno}
	\end{equation}
	The maximum value is achieved if and only if $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)-\theta_{1}$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$ and the minimum value is achieved if and only if $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)-\theta_{1}$ is a multiple of $\pi$. The angle $\theta_1$ is defined in Subsection \ref{notations}. In the case $V_1\leq\frac{2W_1}{1+W_1}$, we have
	\begin{equation}
	\min\limits_{\substack{{y}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ w^{(1)} y_0\neq 0}}\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)\leq\sqrt{\frac{(1+W_1)(1+V_1)}{2}}.\label{minmax<=}
	\end{equation}


	Moreover, we have
	\begin{eqnarray}
	&&\max\limits_{\substack{{y}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ w^{(1)} y_0\neq 0}}\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)
	=\sqrt{\frac{1+W_1}{2}}  \label{MAXMIN}
	\end{eqnarray}
	and
	\begin{eqnarray}
		&&\min\limits_{\substack{{y}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ w^{(1)} y_0\neq 0}}\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)
		=a_{\min}(V_1,W_1):=\left\{
		\begin{array}{l}
			\sqrt{\frac{(1+W_1)(1-V_1)}{2(1+V_1)}}\text{\ if\ }V_1\leq W_1\\
			\\
			\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1}{2}}\text{\ if\ }V_1\geq W_1.
		\end{array}
		\right.  \label{MIN}
	\end{eqnarray}
	The maximum value is achieved if and only if $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)-\theta_{1}$ is a multiple of $\pi$ and the minimum value is achieved if and only if $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)-\theta_{1}$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We introduce the function $H:\mathbb{R}^2\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ given by
\begin{equation}
H(x,\beta)=\frac{f^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)}{1+V_1\cos(x+\beta)},\ (x,\beta)\in\mathbb{R}^2.  \label{Halphax}
\end{equation}
Thus, by (\ref{OTy}), (\ref{maxy}) and (\ref{miny}) we can write
\begin{equation}
	\mathrm{OT}\left(t,\widehat{y}_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}H(x_1(t),\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0))}  \label{OTty0}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{eqnarray}
	&&\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,\widehat{y}_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}H^{\max}(\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0))}\notag \\
	&&\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,\widehat{y}_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}H^{\min}(\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0))}, \notag\\
	\label{fourmax0}
\end{eqnarray}
where the functions $H^{\max},H^{\min}:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ are given by
\begin{equation*}
H^{\max}(\beta):=\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}H(x,\beta)\text{\ \ and\ \ }H^{\min}(\beta):=\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}H(x,\beta),\ \beta\in\mathbb{R}.
\end{equation*}
Observe that (\ref{fourmax0}) holds since, as $t$ varies, $x_1(t)$ in (\ref{xt}) varies in $\mathbb{R}$.

Therefore, we have
\begin{eqnarray}
	&&\max\limits_{\substack{{y}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ w^{(1)} y_0\neq 0}}\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}\max\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\max}(\beta))}\notag\\
	&&\min\limits_{\substack{{y}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ w^{(1)} y_0\neq 0}}\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}\min\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\max}(\beta))}\notag\\
	&&\max\limits_{\substack{{y}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ w^{(1)} y_0\neq 0}}\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}\max\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\min}(\beta))}\notag\\
	&&\min\limits_{\substack{{y}_0\in\mathbb{R}^n\\ w^{(1)} y_0\neq 0}}\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}}\mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)=\sqrt{\frac{1-W_1^2}{2}\min\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\min}(\beta))}.\notag\\
	\label{fourmax}
\end{eqnarray}
Observe that (\ref{fourmax}) hold since, as $y_0\in\mathbb{R}^n$ with $w^{(1)}y_0\neq 0$ varies, $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_0$ can be an arbitrary non-zero point in $\mathcal{W}_1$ and then $\gamma_1(\widehat{y}_0)$ varies in $[0,2\pi]$ and then
$$
\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0)=2(\gamma_1(\widehat{y}_0)-\theta_1)
$$
varies in $[-2\theta_1,-2\theta_1+4\pi]$, over two periods of the functions $H^{\max}$ and $H^{\min}$. 

The theorem will follow once we have determined
\begin{equation*}
	\max\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\max}(\beta),\ \min\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\max}(\beta),\ \max\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\min}(\beta)\text{\ and\ }\min\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\max}(\beta)
\end{equation*}
and for which values $\beta$ the maximum and minimum values are achieved.



By Lemma \ref{maxminf}, we immediately see  that
\begin{eqnarray}
\max\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\max}(\beta)&=&\max\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}H(x,\beta)=\max\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}\frac{f^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)}{1+V_1\cos(x+\beta)}\notag \\
&=&\frac{1+V_1}{(1-W_1)(1-V_1)}\notag \\
\label{MAXMAX}
\end{eqnarray}
with the maximum value achieved if and only if $x$ is an even multiple of $\pi$ (thus $f_{V_1W_1}^{\max}(x)$ achieves its maximum value
$$
\frac{1+V_1}{1-W_1}
$$
as $x$ varies) and $x+\beta$ is an odd multiple of $\pi$ (thus $1+V_1\cos(x+\beta)$ achieves its minimum value $1-V_1$ as $x$ and $\beta$ vary). In other words, the maximum is achieved if and only if $\beta$ is an odd multiple of $\pi$. Thus, by (\ref{fourmax}) and (\ref{MAXMAX}) we have (\ref{MAX}) with the maximum achieved if and only if $\gamma_1(\widehat{y}_0)-\theta_1=\frac{\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0)}{2}=\frac{\beta}{2}$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$. Remind that $\Delta_1(\widehat{y}_0)=\beta$ in (\ref{fourmax0}).

By Lemma \ref{maxminf}, we also immediately see  that
\begin{eqnarray}
	\min\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\min}(\beta)&=&\min\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}H(x,\beta)=\min\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}\frac{f^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)}{1+V_1\cos(x+\beta)}\notag\\
	&=&\left\{
	\begin{array}{l}
		\frac{1-V_1}{(1-W_1)(1+V_1)}\text{\ if\ }V_1\leq W_1\\
		\\
		\frac{1}{1+W_1}\text{\ if\ }V_1\geq W_1,
	\end{array}
	\right.\notag\\
	\label{MINMIN}
\end{eqnarray}
with the minimum value achieved if and only $x$ is an odd multiple of $\pi$ (thus $f_{V_1W_1}^{\max}(x)$ achieves its minimum value
$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
	\frac{1-V_1}{1-W_1}\text{\ if\ }V_1\leq W_1\\
	\\
	\frac{1+V_1}{1+W_1}\text{\ if\ }V_1\geq W_1
\end{array}
\right.
$$
as $x$ varies) and $x+\beta$ is an even multiple of $\pi$ (thus $1+V_1\cos(x+\beta)$ achieves its maximum value $1+V_1$ as $x$ and $\beta$ vary). In other words, the minimum is achieved if and only if $\beta$ is an odd multiple of $\pi$. Thus,  by (\ref{fourmax}) and (\ref{MINMIN}) we have (\ref{MIN}) with the minimum achieved if and only if $\gamma_1(\widehat{y}_0)-\theta_1$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$.

It remains to determine
$$
\min\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\max}(\beta)=\min\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}\max\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}H(x,\beta)\text{\ and\ }\max\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}H^{\min}(\beta)=\max\limits_{\beta\in\mathbb{R}}\min\limits_{x\in\mathbb{R}}H(x,\beta).
$$
By using the property
$$
f_{V_1W_1}^{\max}(-x)=f_{V_1W_1}^{\max}(x),\ x\in\mathbb{R},
$$
(this follows since $\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(-x)=-\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)$, with $\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)$ defined in Lemma \ref{LemmafvW}), we obtain
$$
H^{\max}(\pi-\beta)=H^{\max}(\pi+\beta)\text{\ and\ }H^{\min}(\pi-\beta)=H^{\min}(\pi+\beta),\ \beta\in\mathbb{R}.
$$
This fact shows that we can confine our analysis to the case $\beta\in[0,\pi]$ (remind that $H^{\max}$ and $H^{\min}$ have period $2\pi$).

For the remainder of the proof, we assume $V_1\neq W_1$. This ensures that the function $\alpha_{V_1W}^{\max}$ is defined and differentiable at every point $x\in\mathbb{R}$, by reminding Remark \ref{diff}. The annoying case $V_1=W_1$ is treated as a limiting case of the situation $V_1\neq W_1$. This thanks to the following proposition.
\begin{proposition}\label{fact}
Let $F:A\times B\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function, with $A$ and $B$  metric spaces. If
$$
\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow \infty}a_n=a,\ \lim\limits_{n\rightarrow \infty}b_n=b\text{\ \ and\ \ }F(a_n,b_n)=\max\limits_{x\in A}F(x,b_n),
$$
where $a_n,a\in A$ and $b_n,b\in B$, then
$$
F(a,b)=\max\limits_{x\in A}F(x,b).
$$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
	Let $x\in A$. We have
	\begin{eqnarray*}
		F(a,b)&=&F(x,b)+F(x,b_n)-F(x,b)+F(a_n,b_n)-F(x,b_n)+F(a,b)-F(a_n,b_n)\\
		&\geq&F(x,b)+F(x,b_n)-F(x,b)+F(a,b)-F(a_n,b_n)
	\end{eqnarray*}
	since
	$$
	F(a_n,b_n)-F(x,b_n)\geq 0.
	$$
	By letting $n\rightarrow \infty$, we obtain
	$$
	F(a,b)\geq F(x,b)
	$$
	for the continuity of $F$.
\end{proof}


For a given $\beta\in[0,\pi]$, the values $H^{\max}(\beta)$ and $H^{\min}(\beta)$ are values $H(x,\beta)$, where $x$ is stationary point of the function $H(\cdot,\beta):\mathbb{R}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ and satisfies
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial H}{\partial x}(x,\beta)=0. \label{dHdx}
\end{equation}
Since
\begin{eqnarray*}
&&\frac{\partial H}{\partial x}(x,\beta)=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{f_{V_1W_1}(\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x),x)}{1+V_1\cos(x+\beta)}\right)\\
&&=\frac{1}{(1+V_1\cos(x+\beta))^2}\\
&&\ \ \ \ \cdot\left(\left(\underset{=0}{\underbrace{\frac{\partial f_{V_1W_1}}{\partial \alpha}(\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x),x)}}(\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1})^\prime(x)+\frac{\partial f_{V_1W_1}}{\partial x}(\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x),x)\right)\right.\\
&&\left. \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \cdot(1+V_1\cos(x+\beta))\right.\\
&&\left. \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ -f_{V_1W_1}(\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x),x)\left(-V_1\sin(x+\beta)\right)\right)\\
&&=\frac{1}{(1+V_1\cos(x+\beta))^2}\\
&&\ \ \ \ \cdot\left(\frac{\partial f_{V_1W_1}}{\partial x}(\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x),x)(1+V_1\cos(x+\beta))+f_{V_1W_1}(\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x),x)V_1\sin(x+\beta)\right),
\end{eqnarray*}
we see (remind (\ref{fVW}) and (\ref{partialx})) that (\ref{dHdx}) is equivalent to 
\begin{equation}
-\sin(x+\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x))+\sin(x+\beta)-V_1\sin(\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)-\beta)=0. \label{eq}
\end{equation}
By solving this equation for $\beta=0$, we obtain (see Appendix B)
\begin{equation}
H^{\max}(0)=\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
	\frac{1}{1-W_1}\cdot\frac{1-V_1^2}{1-Q_1V_1}\text{\ if\ }V_1\leq\frac{2W_1}{1+W_1}\\
	\\
	\frac{1}{1+W_1}\cdot\frac{1+V_1}{1-V_1}\text{\ if\ }\frac{2W_1}{1+W_1}\leq V_1,
\end{array}
\right.\label{Hmax0}
\end{equation}
where $Q_1$ is defined in (\ref{Quno}), and
\begin{equation}
H^{\min}(0)=\frac{1}{1-W_1}.  \label{Hmin0}
\end{equation}
Moreover, in the case $V_1\leq \frac{2W_1}{1+W_1}$, we have
\begin{equation}
H^{\max}(0)\leq \frac{1}{1-W_1}(1+V_1). \label{H<=}
\end{equation}


The stationary points of $H(\cdot,\beta)$ (i.e. the solutions of (\ref{eq})) form, as function of $\beta\in[0,\pi]$, a family of continuous branches
$$
x_i:D_i\rightarrow \mathbb{R},\ \beta\mapsto x_i(\beta),\ i\in I,
$$
where $I$ is a finite set of indices and $D_i$ a closed interval included in $[0,\pi]$. These branches satisfy the differential equation
\begin{equation}
\frac{d}{d\beta}\frac{\partial H}{\partial x}(x_i(\beta),\beta)=\frac{\partial^2 H}{\partial x^2}(x_i(\beta),\beta)x_i^\prime(\beta)+\frac{\partial^2 H}{\partial \beta\partial x}(x_i(\beta),\beta)=0.
\label{odexi}
\end{equation}
The ends of the interval $D_i$ are $0$, $\pi$ or points $\beta$ where
$$
\frac{\partial^2 H}{\partial x^2}(x_i(\beta),\beta)=0.
$$
Each branch $x_i$, $i\in I$, is continuously differentiable in the interior of $D_i$. 

The values of $H(\cdot,\beta)$ at the stationary points also form a family of continuous branches
$$
h_i:D_i\rightarrow \mathbb{R},\ \beta\mapsto H(x_i(\beta),\beta),\ i\in I,
$$
continuously differentiable in the interior of $D_i$.

Since $H^{\max}(\beta)$ and $H^{\min}(\beta)$, $\beta\in[0,\pi]$, are values $H(x_i(\beta),\beta)$ for some $i\in I$, we have
\begin{equation}
H^{\max}(\beta)=\max\limits_{\substack{i\in I\\ \beta\in D_i}}h_i(\beta)\text{\ \ and\ \ }H^{\min}(\beta)=\min\limits_{\substack{i\in I\\ \beta\in D_i}}h_i(\beta),\ \beta\in[0,\pi]. \label{Hmaxmin}
\end{equation}
In Figure \ref{branches}, we see examples of such branches.

\begin{remark}
Observe that the equation (\ref{eq}) can be factorized as
$$
-2\sin\frac{\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)-\beta}{2}\left(\cos\left(x+\frac{\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)+\beta}{2}\right)+V_1\cos\frac{\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)-\beta}{2}\right)=0
$$
and then its solutions are the union of the solutions of
\begin{equation}
\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)-\beta\text{\ is a multiple of $2\pi$}\label{2pi}
\end{equation}
and
$$
\cos\left(x+\frac{\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)+\beta}{2}\right)+V_1\cos\frac{\alpha^{\max}_{V_1W_1}(x)-\beta}{2}=0.
$$
The branches $h_i$ corresponding to solutions of (\ref{2pi}) are given by
$$
h_i(\beta)=\frac{1}{1-W_1\cos \beta}
$$
as can be immediately verified by the definition (\ref{Halphax}) of $H$. In Figure \ref{branchespartial}, we see examples of such branches $x_i$ and $h_i$ corresponding to solutions of (\ref{2pi}).
\end{remark}
