with $\max\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)$ at its minimum value and $\min\limits_{t\in\mathbb{R}} \mathrm{OT}\left(t,y_0\right)$ at its maximum value, as $y_0$ varies. 


\item [4.] The condition $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)-\theta_{1}$ is an odd multiple of $\frac{\pi}{2}$ is equivalent to saying that the vector $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}$ is orthogonal  in the complex plane to the major semi-axis of the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_1$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean}, i.e. $c_1(\widehat{y}_0)=0$ by (\ref{whatu}).

The condition $\gamma_{1}(\widehat{y}_0)-\theta_{1}$ is a multiple of $\pi$ is equivalent to saying that the vector $\widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_{0}$ is  parallel in the complex plane to the major semi-axis of the ellipse $\mathcal{E}_1$, i.e. $d_1(\widehat{y}_0)=0$ by (\ref{whatu}).
\item [5.] Two specific situations are worth considering.
\begin{itemize}
	\item [a)] If $y_0$ is orthogonal to the first right singular vector of the matrix $R_1$ of Proposition \ref{euclidean} (i.e. $c_1(\widehat{y}_0)=0$), then, by previous points 4 and 2, (\ref{maxmax}) and (\ref{minmin}) hold. Moreover, by (\ref{OSF2}) we have
	\begin{equation*}
