\subsubsection{Cypher Syntax}

Cypher is a declarative graph query language designed for querying property
graphs. Cypher defines graph patterns to query property graphs by isomorphism-based
\cite{angles2017foundations} subgraph matching. As shown in
\listref{list:cypher-example}, (1) The \texttt{MATCH} clause serves as the
foundation of Cypher by defining a graph pattern
on property graphs to match subgraphs. The graph pattern consists of a 2-length
path that denotes nodes as () and relationships as {-}[]-> or <{-}[]-. Nodes and
relationships are labeled by $:label\_name$, e.g., the node $book$ with a label $Book$ is
denoted as $(book:Person)$. (2) The \texttt{WHERE} clause filters the matched
subgraphs by adding restrictions to the 
graph pattern in its paired \texttt{MATCH}, e.g., the
\texttt{WHERE} clause in \listref{} (i.e., WHERE $reader.name$ = ``Alice'') in
\listref{list:cypher-example} retrieves nodes named ``Alice''. (3)
\texttt{RETURN} clause specifies the returning results, e.g. the name of writer
nodes, which is ``J. K. Rowling'' in \figref{fig:property-graph}. Additionally,
(4) the \texttt{WITH} clause can be used for passing or refining the
intermediate results to the subsequent query parts. (5) The \texttt{UNWIND}
clause can be used to expand a list into a sequence of rows, turning each
element of the list into a separate record. 

\subsubsection{No-Repeated-Edge Semantics in Cypher}
\label{sec:No-Repeated-Edge-Semantic}

Most graph query languages query data under bag semantics
\cite{grumbach1996query} that retains all duplicates. Cypher queries graph
databases under bag semantics with additional restrictions namely
no-repeated-edge semantics. Cypher matches graph patterns by mapping variables
of pattern parts to real database entities (subgraph matching). No-repeated-edge
semantics apply isomorphism-based injective mapping to relationship variables,
which means that the mapping of relationship variables and real relationship
entities in GDBs must be one-to-one. Specifically in Cypher queries,
different relationship variables within the same \texttt{MATCH} clause are not
allowed to match the same relationship in the database. 

\subsubsection{Cypher vs. SQL} 
\label{sec:cypher-vs-sql}
\listref{list:joining} shows a Cypher query and its two semantical equivalence
SQL queries, which help us to illustrate the differences between Cypher and SQL.
Specifically, Cypher differs from SQL in the following three aspects. (1)
\textbf{Syntax differences}. Cypher defines graph structures using
\texttt{MATCH} and multiple \texttt{MATCH}s are chained together. Cypher
specifies returned elements using {RETURN} that is also chained at the end of
the query. A SQL query specifies the output tuples using \texttt{SELECT} and
\texttt{FROM} that contains nested queries. (2) \textbf{Semantics differences}.
In Cypher, nodes and relationships are directly defined and accessed. While SQL
lacks native support for structured data and \texttt{JOIN} is involved to
combine tables based on specified conditions. (3) \textbf{Schema differences}.
SQL requires schema to define data structures. Schema affects the joining
behavior e.g., self-join and many-to-many join, in SQL. As shown in
\listref{list:joining}, schema affects the way tables are joined in
SQL queries. Different implementations of $KNOWS$ cause the difference between $Q1$
and $Q2$ in self-join and many-to-many-join, while Cypher query shows its expressiveness by defining
relationships.\yingying{Using the elements in Listing 2 to illustrate the schema
differences?}

\begin{lstlisting}[float, floatplacement=h,style=cypherstyle, label=list:joining,caption={A translation example from Cypher into SQL with different SQL schemas.}]
/* Cypher: p1 knows p2 */
MATCH (n:Person)-[:KNOWS]->(:Person) 
RETURN n

/* Q1: SQL self-join: KNOWS as a foreign key */ 
SELECT p1.* FROM Person p1 JOIN Person p2 ON p1.knows_id = p2.id

/* Q2: SQL many-to-many-join: KNOWS as a table */ 
SELECT Person.* FROM Person JOIN KNOWS ON Person.id = KNOWS.person_id
\end{lstlisting}

