\subsection{Graph Relational Algebra\yingying{Remove this section.}}
\label{sec:graph-algebra}

Graph relational algebra (GRA for short)  \cite{holsch2016graphpattern,
marton2017formalising} provides a formal representation for property graph query
languages. \tabref{tab:graph-algebra} shows the definitions of GRA operators and
their corresponding Cypher clauses. GRA operators are nested that take inner
operators as inputs and append its own operations.

GRA operators represent graph patterns defined by graph
queries starting with \emph{get\_nodes} and traversing with \emph{expand}.
\emph{selection} filters graph patterns by defining restrictions. Collection
operators i.e., \emph{union\_all}, \emph{natural\_join}, and
\emph{left\_outer\_join} connects multiple graph patterns. We abandon the
\emph{union} operator \cite{marton2017formalising} but use \emph{union\_all} in
\emph{dedup} $\delta(r \cup s)$ instead.

Besides matching graph patterns starting with the operator \emph{get\_nodes},
Cypher queries can also take constant lists as source data that is omitted in
GRA operators \cite{holsch2016graphpattern,
marton2017formalising}. We use \emph{projection} without inner GRAs
($\pi_{list\_const\rightarrow x}$) to denote constant list declarations.

\subsection{SQL Query Equivalence Provers\yingying{Move this section to related work.}}
\label{sec:SQL-Query-Equivalence}

% \tl{Summary: introduce Query Equivalence studies on SQL and basic concept. How
% we do on cypher}

Proving SQL query equivalence is well studied. SQL queries are first transformed
into algebraic representations. Then, proving SQL query equivalence is
transformed into proving the equivalence of algebraic representations.
Syntax-based and semantic-based approaches are proposed for the decision
procedure.

\subsubsection{Syntax-Based Approaches}

Syntax-based approaches
\cite{chu2017cosette,chu2018udp,zhou2019equitas,zhou2020spes} prove the
equivalence of algebraic representations by checking their syntactic
isomorphism. State-of-the-art prover UDP \cite{chu2018udp} proposes an algebraic
structure, i.e., U-semiring, and construct SQL algebraic representations by
introducing axioms and predefined functions on U-semirings. Another novel
representative SPES \cite{zhou2020spes} proposes an algebraic representation to
model SQL tree structure and leverage SMT solver for handling logical predicates
in SQL queries. Normalization rules are manually designed and automatically
applied to formalize these algebraic representations for syntactic equivalence
checking. 

\subsubsection{Semantic-Based Provers}
Semantic-based approaches \cite{wang2022wetune, ding2023sqlsolver} transform
U-semiring based SQL algebraic representations (\uexpt{}) into logical
expressions and leverage SMT solvers for automatic proving. State-of-the-art
prover SQLSolver \cite{ding2023sqlsolver} uses the theory of Linear Integer
Arithmetic with stars (LIA*) \cite{piskac2008lia*}, which extends linear integer
arithmetic formulas to eliminate the unbounded summations in \uexpt{}s. Then
SQLSolver constructs first-order logic formulas that are proven equivalent using
an SMT solver. Compared to semantic-based approaches, SOLSolver supports more
SQL features and can prove SQL queries that differ significantly in syntactic
structures.

\subsubsection{Can These Approaches Prove Cypher Query Equivalence?}
Currently, there is a lack of equivalence provers for graph query languages.
Apparently, tree-based SQL algebraic representations cannot model Cypher queries
for the significant syntactic differences between Cypher and SQL. Although
\uexpt{} can model sophisticated SQL queries and semantic-based approaches have
gained good proving capability on it, whether U-semiring can be used to model
the semantics of Cypher queries needs further evaluations. Intuitively, \uexpt{}
defines functions on tables that cannot directly model Cypher queries with
different syntactic and semantical features (\secref{sec:cypher-vs-sql}). 

\subsection{U-semiring Semantics}
\label{sec:U-expression}

\subsubsection{U-semiring definitions} Chu et al. propose unbounded
semiring, i.e., U-semiring that extends the semiring semantics of SQL with
unbounded summations and other operators. U-semiring is a commutative semiring
defined as $(U,0,1,+,\times, \|\cdot\|,not(\cdot),\sum)$. Based on U-semiring,
SQL queries under bag semantics can be modeled as algebraic representations with
predefined functions for tables. We denote such algebraic representations with
tabular functions as \uexpt{}.

\subsubsection{U-semiring based SQL algebraic representation} \uexpt{}
models a SQL query as an algebraic expression $f(t)$ that returns the
multiplicity of arbitrary tuple $t$ in the query result. $f(t)$ is a connection
of algebraic terms that returns $\mathbb{N}$ by $\times$ or $+$. Predefined
tabular function $R(t)$ returns the multiplicity of tuple $t$ in the table $R$.
To express the predicate as $\mathbb{N}$, \uexpt{} leverages algebraic term
$[*]$ converts a bool type predicate $*$ to 0 or 1. To represent
\texttt{DISTINCT} in SQL, \uexpt{} leverages the algebraic term $\|*\|$ that
returns 1 for values greater than 1. For a specific SQL query \texttt{"select *
from R where R.x > 0"}, \uexpt{} models it as $f(t)=R(t)\times[x.t>0]$. For
queries returning attributes and aggregate functions, \uexpt{} uses an unbounded
summation $\sum$ over arbitrary tuple to accumulate the multiplicity of result
tuples. For example, \texttt{"select R.x from R where R.x > 0"} can be represented as
$f(t)=\sum_{t_{1}}(R(t_{1})\times[t_{1}.x>0]\times[t=t_{1}.x])$. 
\fi

\section{Overview}

To prove the equivalence of Cypher queries, we first formalize the problem of
Cypher query equivalence under bag semantics in
\secref{sec:overview-formalizing}. We then explain the basic idea of modeling
the Cypher semantics based on U-semiring in \secref{sec:overview-our-approach}.
Finally, we introduce the workflow of our Cypher equivalence prover in
\secref{sec:overview-workflow}.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:overview-formalizing}
% \tl{What is Cypher QE --> multiplicity of Cypher tabular result tuple --> how
% the multiplicity is calculated equivalent --> how to express it in \uexpg{}}
\iffalse
Before we define a formulation of Cypher query equivalence, we present the
features of the Cypher query tabular result under bag semantics including the
result tuple and its multiplicity.

\begin{itemize} [leftmargin=10pt]
  \item \textbf{Cypher tabular result tuple}. Cypher returns tabular
  results under bag semantics, in which columns are defined by the
  \texttt{RETURN} clause. Cypher tabular result can be sorted using
  \texttt{ORDER BY} clause and automatically grouped by aggregate functions.

  \item \textbf{Cypher result tuple multiplicity}. Under bag semantics, Cypher
  allows for duplicated tuples in the result set. Therefore we use the
  multiplicity of tuples to model Cypher query result. The multiplicity of a
  Cypher result tuple is the number it appears in the Cypher query result set.
\end{itemize}
\fi

Cypher queries return tabular results under bag semantics that allow for
duplicates. Two Cypher queries $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ are equivalent if their results
consist of the same tuples, and the multiplicity of any tuple $t$ in their
results is equal. Additionally, if Cypher queries return ordered results (e.g.,
queries with \texttt{ORDER BY} clauses), any two tuples $t_1$ and $t_2$ appear in
the same order in the results of both queries $Q_1$ and $Q_2$. 

For two Cypher queries $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ with \texttt{ORDER BY} clauses, if their
corresponding sub-queries $Q_{1}^{'}$ and $Q_{2}^{'}$ without \texttt{ORDER BY} clauses
are equivalent, and $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ sort the query results returned by
$Q_{1}^{'}$ and $Q_{2}^{'}$ according to the same \texttt{ORDER BY} expressions, we can say
that $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ are equivalent.

Therefore, the Cypher query equivalence under the ordered bag semantics can be
defined as follows.

\begin{definition}
  \label{def:cypher-equality-2}
  If two Cypher queries $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ are equivalent, they need to satisfy the
 following conditions.
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item The results of $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ contain the same tuples.
    \item The multiplicity of any tuple $t$ in the results of $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ is
equal.
    \item If $Q_1$ or $Q_2$ contains the \texttt{ORDER BY} clauses, their corresponding
    sub-queries $Q_{1}^{'}$ and $Q_{2}^{'}$ without \texttt{ORDER BY} clauses are
    equivalent, and $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ sort the query results returned by
    $Q_{1}^{'}$ and $Q_{2}^{'}$ according to the equivalent \texttt{ORDER BY} expressions.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

