$r_1$ and \texttt{(z)} matches node $n_3$, with $n_3$ and $n_1$ as the
outgoing and incoming nodes of relationship $r_1$, respectively. However,
$GP(n_1,n_1,n_2)$ outputs 0 because \texttt{-[y]->} cannot match node $n_1$.

For the predicates of a graph pattern, e.g., $p1.age=59$, we construct algebraic
expression $\phi(e_1,\dots,e_n)$ that outputs 1 if $e_1,\dots,e_n$ satisfy
$\phi$, and 0 otherwise. To model each tuple $t$ in the Cypher query result, we construct an algebraic expression $QR(t, e_1,\dots, e_n)$. This
expression represents how the columns of a tuple $t$ in the Cypher query result are projected from the mapped property graph entities $e_1,\dots, e_n$,
such as $t.col_1=e_1.name$. 

Finally, we use the unbounded summation to model a Cypher query as an
algebraic expression $f(t)=\sum_{e_1,\dots, e_n}(QR(t, e_1,\dots, e_n)\times
GP(e_1,\dots, e_n)\times \phi(e_1,\dots,e_n))$, which represents the
accumulation of all valid mappings from the Cypher graph pattern to any property
graph, determining the multiplicity of tuple $t$ in the Cypher query result.

Based on our model for Cypher queries, an illustrative Cypher query:
\begin{center}
  \texttt{MATCH (n1)-[r]->(n2) WHERE n1.age=59 RETURN n1.name}
\end{center}
can be modeled as:
\begin{align*}
  f(t)=&\sum_{e_1,e_2,e_3}(QR(t, e_1,e_2,e_3)\times GP(e_1,e_2,e_3)\times \phi(e_1,e_2,e_3))\\
  =&\sum_{e_1,e_2,e_3}([t=e_1.name]\times Node(e_1)\times Rel(e_2)\times Node(e_3)\\
  &\quad \times [in(e_2)=e_1]\times [out(e_2)=e_3]\times [e_1.age=59])
\end{align*}
where the predefined functions $Node()$, $Rel()$, $in()$ and $out()$ are
used to model the semantics of node and relationship patterns.

Based on our approach, two Cypher queries $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ are considered
semantically equivalent if their corresponding algebraic representations,
$f_1(t)$ and $f_2(t)$, always output the same value for any tuple $t$ on any
property graph. We will detail how Cypher queries are modeled as \uexpg{} in \secref{sec:U-semiring-Cypher-semantics}.
\fi
