\subsubsection{Modeling a Cypher Query as \uexpg{}}
We take \listref{list:cypher-example} as an example for modeling Cypher queries
into \uexpg{}. First, we normalize the graph pattern as\\
\texttt{(t1:Person)-[t2:READ]->(t3:Book)<-[t4:WRITE]-(t5:Person)}. We first
specify $t1$ as a node using $Node(t1)$. We then restrict $t2$ as a relationship
by applying $Rel(t2)$ and the rest graph pattern parts follows similarly. The
\uexpg{} for Cypher graph pattern becomes:
{\begin{align*}
p(\{t1,t2,t3,t4,t5\})=&Node(t1)\times Person(t1)\times Rel(t2)\times
READ(t2)\\ &\times Node(t3)\times Book(t3)\times [@1(t2)=t1]\\ &\times [@2(t2)=t3]\times
Rel(t4)\times WRITE(t4)\\ &\times Node(t5)\times [@1(t4)=t5]\times [@2(t4)=t3]\\ &
\times [name(t5)=``Alice"]
\end{align*}}

To model no-repeated-edge semantics of Cypher, we multiply $p$ by $[t2\neq t4]$.
With the query result defined as the $name$ property of $t1$, we model the result $t$
of the query by leveraging IF on index 1 $[@1(t)=t1.name]$ as the follows.
\begin{align*}
  r(t,\{t1,t2,t3,t4,t5\})=[@1(t)=t1.name]
\end{align*}
Then we multiply $p$ and $r$ and leverage $\sum$ to enumerate all possible
property graphs to calculate the multiplicity of result tuple $t$ as the follows.
\begin{align*}
f(t) = &\sum_{t1,t2,t3,t4,t5}(r(t,\{t1,t2,t3,t4,t5\})\times
p(\{t1,t2,t3,t4,t5\}))\\ =&\sum_{t1,t2,t3,t4,t5}([@1(t)=t1.name]\times Node(t1)\times Person(t1)\\ &\times Rel(t2)\times READ(t2)\times Node(t3)\times Book(t3)\times [@1(t2)=t1]\\ &\times [@2(t2)=t3]\times
Rel(t4)\times WRITE(t4)\times Node(t5)\\ &\times [@1(t4)=t5]\times [@2(t4)=t3]
\times [name(t5)=``Alice"]\\ & \times [t2\neq t4])
\end{align*}

Now, for arbitrary Cypher query result tuple $t$, $f(t)$ models its multiplicity
in the result set. The equivalence proving of Cypher query
semantics is then transformed into the equivalence proving of their
corresponding \uexpt{} $f(t)$ and $f'(t)$ on arbitrary input $t$.
