\subsubsection{Simplifying \uexpg{}}
\label{sec:special-case}
\tl{We do not need this para. Moved to normalization.}
To improve the performance of proving the equivalence of \uexpg{}, we adopt a
series of rules to simplify \uexpg{}. (1) \textbf{Replacing equivalent
entities}. For property graph entities equations such as $e_1=e_2$, we replace
all occurrences of $e_2$ with $e_1$. (2) \textbf{Eliminating empty sets}. For an
empty list declared by \texttt{WITH []} clause followed by a \texttt{UNWIND}
operation as the input of later graph patterns, e.g., \texttt{WITH [] AS tmp
UNWIND tmp AS tmpRow WITH tmpRow MATCH \dots}, we simply replace it with 0. (3)
\textbf{Eliminating true expressions}. When both sides of a binary equation are
the same, such as $e_1=e_1$, we directly eliminate the equation. (4)
\textbf{Eliminating duplicated functions}. The predefined functions, e.g.,
$Node(e)$, should be used only once for the same pattern. Therefore, we
eliminate duplicated functions functions on the same variable. 
