% Q1: MATCH (n)--(p)
%     RETURN n.name

% /* cypher query eliminated undirected relation */
% Q2: MATCH (n)-->(p) RETURN n.name
%     UNION ALL 
%     MATCH (n)<--(p) RETURN n.name
% \end{lstlisting}

\review{Reviewer A: How do you normalize unbounded var-length patterns?}
\textcircled{3} \textbf{Rewriting variable-length path }. The variable-length
path is an advanced expression used to specify path with multiple relationships
between two nodes, where the number of relationships can \tladd{be unbounded or} vary within a specified
range. For example, \texttt{(n1)-[*2]->(n2)} defines a path from node pattern
\texttt{(n1)} to \texttt{(n2)} with exactly two relationships, while
\texttt{(n1)-[*2..3]->(n2)} defines a path from \texttt{(n1)} to \texttt{(n2)}
with two to three relationships. For the first case, we rewrite variable-length
paths by expanding the relationships explicitly, e.g., for
\texttt{(n1)-[*2]->(n2)}, we expand it as \texttt{(n1)-[]->()-[]->(n2)}. For the
second case, we rewrite it using the \texttt{UNION ALL} clause to combine all
possible path lengths as subqueries, e.g., for the pattern
\texttt{(n1)-[*2..3]->(n2)}, we rewrite it as \texttt{(n1)-[]->()-[]->(n2)\\
UNION ALL (n1)-[]->()-[]->()-[]->(n2)}. \tladd{For those variable-length paths
with unbounded length, e.g., \texttt{(n1)-[*]->(n2)}, we simply replace the
defined relationship set (\texttt{-[*]->}) with a single relationship with a
predefined reserved type \texttt{\_UNBOUNDED}, i.e., \texttt{-[:\_UNBOUNDED]->}.
In this way, we do not model the semantics of unbounded length paths, instead we
use a predefined relationship type (\texttt{\_UNBOUNDED}) to denote such
relationships are unbounded relationship sets. Predicates on the unbounded relationship
sets is treated as predicates on relationships typed \texttt{\_UNBOUNDED}. Then
we construct \uexpg{}s for those relationships typed \texttt{\_UNBOUNDED} the same
as common relationships.}

