\textcircled{4} \textbf{Rewriting RETURN}. Cypher uses \texttt{RETURN *} to
return all the graph entities from graph patterns with variables. For anonymous
nodes and relationships, e.g., \texttt{MATCH ()}, \texttt{RETURN *} does not
return them. Therefore, We replace \texttt{RETURN *} with its explicit variables
to model tuple columns. The specific order of the pattern variables is
determined by the alphabetical sorting of their variable names. For example, we
rewrite \texttt{MATCH (x)-[z]->()-[y]->() RETURN *} to \texttt{MATCH
(n)-[z]->()-[y]->() RETURN x, y, z}.

\textcircled{5} \textbf{Eliminating non-impactful clause}. Cypher uses
\texttt{AS} in \texttt{RETURN} to rename tuple column name. As shown in
\tabref{tab:normalized}, since we do not model the column names of tuples in the
Cypher query result, we remove the \texttt{AS} clauses for renaming pattern
variables in the \texttt{RETURN} clause. Cypher uses the \texttt{WITH} clause to
pass node and relationship patterns. We remove those \texttt{WITH} clauses only
for passing pattern variables from the former subquery to the later subqueries.
For example, \texttt{MATCH (n1) WITH n MATCH (n1)-[]->(n2) RETURN n2} is
normalized into \texttt{MATCH (n1) MATCH (n1)-[]->(n2) RETURN n2}.

\textcircled{6} \textbf{Standardizing variable}. Cypher uses variables for the
references of node and relationship patterns. To distinguish different node or
relationship patterns, we assign a variable to each anonymous node or
relationship pattern in the Cypher graph pattern and rename those node and
relationship patterns that share the same variable in different subqueries.
Additionally, we rename property names and variable names to avoid unnecessary
naming conflicts to our predefined functions. 

\textcircled{7} \textbf{ID equality simplification}. $id$ serves as the unique
identifier for nodes and relationships in property graphs, which means if two nodes
(relationships) have the same $id$, they are indeed the same node
(relationship). This rule simplifies expressions in Cypher queries, where
\texttt{id(n1) = id(n2)} is replaced by \texttt{n1 = n2}. In other scenarios, we
treat the $id$ as a general property. After normalization, \approachname{}
accurately models the semantics of equivalent $id$s, ensuring correct
representation and comparison of property graph entities.
\vspace{-3mm}
\subsection{Normalizing Cypher Queries}
We normalize a Cypher query by employing normalization rules on its AST to
modify AST nodes or reform their structures. We follow the sequence in
\secref{sec:normalization-rules} to employ all normalization rules on a Cypher
query AST round by round until no rule is successfully employed. 

\review{Reviewer A: Where does 5 create anonymous node or relationship patterns? 5 is about removing things from the query text.
}
The sequence of normalization rules is restricted. Specifically, \textcircled{6}
should be employed after  \textcircled{3}, \textcircled{4} and \textcircled{5}.
This is because \textcircled{3} and \textcircled{5} create anonymous node or
relationship patterns and copy the existing patterns that should be
standardized. Since standardizing variables assign variables to anonymous graph
patterns, it must be employed after \textcircled{4}. We adopt \textcircled{7}
after \textcircled{6}, because we should not replace the variable names of node or
relationship patterns across subqueries since they are indeed different.

Cypher queries can become isomorphic equivalent after normalizations. For
example, there are two Cypher queries $Q_1$ and $Q_2$:\\
$Q_1$:\texttt{MATCH (n)-[]-() WHERE n.name=toLower("JACK")\\ RETURN *}\\
$Q_2$:\texttt{MATCH (n1)-[]->() WHERE n1.name=toLower("JACK")\\ RETURN n1 AS n UNION ALL MATCH
(n2)<-[]-()\\ WHERE n2.name=toLower("JACK") RETURN n2 AS n}

$Q_1$ and $Q_2$ then are isomorphic equivalent by applying normalization rules
\textcircled{1}, \textcircled{2}, \textcircled{4}, \textcircled{5},
\textcircled{6} into the same form as:\\
\texttt{MATCH (e1)-[e2]->(e3) WHERE e1.name="jack"\\ RETURN e1 AS c1 UNION ALL MATCH
(e4)<-[e5]-(e6)\\ WHERE e4.name="jack" RETURN e4 AS c1}
\fi

