\textbf{Generalizing GraphQE to GQL.}
Graph Query Language (GQL) [31] is the first standardized general graph query
language designed based on and compatible with Cypher. GQL borrows heavily from
Cypher, especially for core constructs like \texttt{MATCH}, \texttt{WHERE}, and
\texttt{RETURN}. However, GQL extends Cypher by incorporating support for
flexible data models, schema definitions, and advanced data types such as
temporal and spatial types. For the data model defined by GQL ISO/IEC, the
relationships can be either directed or undirected. For example, considering a
query segment that specifies an undirected relationship using
\texttt{(n1)-[r1]-(n2)}, GraphQE models it by importing $DIRECTED$ and
$UNDIRECTED$ relationship kind according to GQL ISO/IEC and constructs an
G-expression of $ Node(e1) \times Rel(e2) \times Node(e3) \times UNDIRECTED(e2)
\times ([in(e2) = e1] \times [out(e2) = Node(e3)] + [in(e2) = e3] \times
[out(e2) = Node(e1)]$). The other new features of GQL will not affect the
modeling capability of G-expressions, but need extensions to GraphQE.
\fi

\revision{\textbf{Supporting GQL.} GQL is a new standard graph query language
that extends Cypher and is fully compatible with Cypher. We can easily extend
\approachname{} to support GQL from the following aspects. First,
\approachname{} needs to support the property graph model adopted by GQL.
\approachname{} needs to remove its relationship-injective semantics when
constructing \uexpg{}s for GQL. \approachname{} needs to allow a relationship to
be specified with more than one label. Second, \approachname{} can support
undirected relationships using uninterpreted function. 
