\begin{abstract}
Despite their success in numerous fields, the potential of foundation models for modeling and understanding human behavior remains largely unexplored. We introduce Be.FM, one of the first open foundation models designed for human behavior modeling. Built upon open-source large language models and fine-tuned on a diverse range of behavioral data, \model can be used to understand and predict human decision-making. %
We construct a comprehensive set of benchmark tasks for testing the capabilities of behavioral foundation models. Our results demonstrate that \model can predict behaviors, infer characteristics of individuals and populations, generate insights about contexts, and apply behavioral science knowledge. %
The models are available upon request: \href{https://forms.gle/DAvxJYReqg7midQn9}{\texttt{LINK}}. 
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}

Foundation models have revolutionized numerous fields. Trained on vast and diverse datasets, these models exhibit remarkable adaptability across a wide range of simple and complex tasks. Applications built on these models now support both everyday and specialized tasks with performance approaching human experts. For instance, large language models (LLMs) assist in writing, communicating, and information seeking; multi-modal models enable rapid generation of images, videos, and music; and scientific foundation models provide literature reviews, code generation, data analysis, and pattern recognition \citep{bubeck2023sparks}.  In fields such as genomics, foundation models like AlphaFold have been used to map transcriptional activity across human cell types, providing new insights into gene regulation and advancing our understanding of biological processes \citep{jumper2021highly}. Integrating general language understanding with domain-specific reasoning abilities, foundation models have evolved beyond mere analytical tools. They now serve as catalysts for innovation, driving new discoveries and interdisciplinary collaborations.

Despite their transformative potential, foundation models remain largely underexplored for modeling \textit{human behavior} and addressing problems in \emph{behavioral science}---the analysis and prediction of human behaviors. Behavioral science spans multiple disciplines, including psychology, sociology, anthropology, and economics, and serves as a foundation for understanding decision-making dynamics at individual, group, and societal levels. Insights from this field inform policymaking, healthcare, education, and business strategies, highlighting the need for more advanced AI-driven approaches to enhance behavioral analysis and intervention.  

Traditional AI models used in behavioral science are often trained on task-specific datasets and handcrafted features \citep{turgeon2020tutorial,kliegr2020advances,dehghan2022applications}, limiting their ability to generalize across diverse contexts and tasks. Machine learning models built for specific tasks like sentiment analysis \citep{braig2023machine}, survey analysis \citep{heeringa2017applied}, and clickthrough prediction \citep{yang2022click} typically require extensive domain-specific tuning and struggle to capture the full complexity of human behavior.

A behavioral foundation model has the potential to overcome these limitations by leveraging vast and diverse datasets to develop a more comprehensive and generalizable understanding of human behavior. This allows the model to perform effectively across a wide range of tasks without requiring specialized training for each different application. Such a model could enhance the representation and application of behavioral knowledge, improve behavioral prediction and outcome estimation, provide deeper insights into how context shapes behavior, uncover latent motivations, and enable scalable analysis at both individual and population levels.

Developing a foundation model for human behavior presents two key challenges. 
First, the fragmented and dispersed nature of behavioral data makes it difficult to organize a diverse and high-quality portfolio of datasets for effective model training. 
Second, the lack of standardized benchmark tasks hinders the systematic evaluation and refinement of model performance, limiting its reliability and applicability across different behavioral science domains and tasks.

