We introduce \model, a family of foundation models specifically designed for modeling and emulating human behavior (Figure \ref{fig:fm-illus}). Built upon the powerful language capability of open-source large language models, \model models are trained on curated datasets encompassing multiple key categories of behavioral data: behavioral science literature, human-subject experimental data, survey data, and observational data (Sec. \ref{sec:model}). 
We introduce a comprehensive suite of benchmark tasks,  aligned with critical needs of behavioral science, to systematically evaluate the effectiveness of behavioral foundation models (Sec. \ref{sec:tasks}). 
Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that \model has obtained a broad range of capabilities to:
(1) \textbf{Predict and simulate behavior across diverse scenarios} (Sec. \ref{sec:infer-behavior}); 
(2) \textbf{Infer characteristics of individual subjects and populations} (Sec. \ref{sec:infer-subject});
(3) \textbf{Generate insights about contextual factors that influence behavior} (Sec. \ref{sec:infer-context}); and 
(4) \textbf{Represent behavioral science knowledge and apply it. %
} (Sec. \ref{sec:infer-knowledge}). 
Through these capabilities, \model provides a foundation for advancing behavioral science by enabling more comprehensive and scalable analysis. Additionally, it introduces a novel interface that integrates AI with human behavior research and applications, %
as well as real-world deployment. %

\section{Overview of \model}
\label{sec:model}

\model is %
built upon the open-source large language models (e.g., Meta Llama 3.1). \model models are further trained on diverse sources of behavioral data to enhance their ability to address a wide range of human behavior-related tasks. The foundation of \model is that human behavior is shaped by multiple factors, including the  environment and context of the behavior, the characteristics of the subject, and underlying motivations. The framework can be formally represented as
\begin{equation}
y = \mathcal{F}(\mathbb{K}, x, c),
\label{eqn:framework}
\end{equation}
where $x$ represents the set of characteristics of the human subject,
$c$ denotes the set of contextual variables that defines the environment,
$\mathbb{K}$ encompasses all relevant knowledge about human behaviors, and
$\mathcal{F}$ is a latent function that maps these inputs to a specific behavioral choice 
$y$. The training and benchmarking of \model are guided by this framework. 



The rest of this section outlines the behavioral data used for training (Sec. \ref{sec:data}) and the training process (Sec. \ref{sec:train}). 
Additional details, including the processing and formatting of the training data, are provided in Appendix \ref{app:train}.

