Training a behavioral foundation model requires a wide spectrum of high-quality behavioral data. However, existing data are often fragmented, scattered across disciplines, and not structured for integration into a language model. To address this challenge, we identify four key categories of behavioral data (Table \ref{tab:data}) that together provide a high coverage of variables in Equation~\ref{eqn:framework} and discuss how they can contribute to the overall capabilities of \model.

\paragraph{Literature data. }
The behavioral science literature documents existing theories and scientific findings about human behavior, forming a proxy of the behavioral knowledge $\mathbb{K}$ in Equation~\ref{eqn:framework}. To train the initial version of \model, we use publications from \textit{American Economic Review}. %
Our dataset includes a curated selection of 3,003 publications from this journal, ranging from the year 1963 to 2024. Among these, 2,703 publications are used for training the foundation model, while the remaining ten percent are held out for model evaluation. To precisely extract and represent the behavioral knowledge, we first summarize the title and abstracts of each study into a structured research workflow: context of research, key idea, method, outcome, and projected impact, following the practice in \citet{zhang2024massw}, resulting in a total of 3,129,462 tokens. %
Trained to predict the title of a paper using the research workflow and to predict the key idea given the context of research, \model incorporates key concepts, theories, methodologies, and empirical findings in behavioral sciences, strengthening its ability to complete tasks such as behavior prediction, context inference, research planning, and solving problems like the Economics Olympiad questions. %

\paragraph{Experimental data}
Experimental data captures behavioral patterns of diverse subject pools in controlled environments, offering key insights into individual and group decision-making, as well as social interactions. Such data is usually represented in the format of $\{x, c, y\}$, where $x$ represents subject characteristics (often partially labeled),
$c$ denotes contextual variables (usually partially identified, often including the experiment condition), and 
$y$ records the observed behavior. Valuable sources of such data are experimental platforms and published repositories that record the responses of subjects in various behavioral, economic, and psychological studies.
In the initial version, we utilize a collection of experiments from  MobLab (\cite{mei2024turing}), a platform for behavioral science and economic games, which includes game-playing experiment data from 68,779 subjects and 82,057 independent observations collected over a nine-year period (2015–2023). Individual sessions in this data are used for model training, and a different task is used for evaluation, to predict the behavioral distributions at the population level. 
Trained to predict individual behaviors given game conditions, \model captures behavioral tendencies across diverse scenarios, enhancing its accuracy in behavior prediction and alignment, which enables improved simulation and analysis of real-world behavioral patterns.

\paragraph{Survey data}
Survey data provides self-reported insights into personality traits, attitudes, and preferences. They mainly offer a representation of the characteristics of subjects ($x$ in Equation~\ref{eqn:framework}), although they sometimes also include self-reported behaviors $\Tilde{y}$ which provide a proxy of the observed behaviors $y$, and can also be useful in simulating responses to surveys that have not been run yet. These responses may be solicited directly without specifying the context, or with certain pre-specified scenarios $c$ in the survey questions. In the initial version of \model, we use a dataset of responses from the Big Five personality test. %
The dataset includes demographic information (race, age, and gender) of respondents and their single-choice answers to 50 questions across five personality dimensions: openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, and neuroticism (\cite{mei2024turing}). These responses are aggregated into five dimensional scores of individual personality traits. In total, records from 17,667 subjects are used for training, while 1,963 are reserved for evaluation.
The large survey data enables \model to infer individual behavioral patterns, predict correlations in responses across questions, and emulate subjective experiences. Trained to predict the personality traits of subjects given their demographic information, these data enhance \model's ability to generalize across demographic groups, improving behavioral modeling at both individual and population levels.

\paragraph{Observational data}

Observational data captures real-world behaviors by capturing unstructured or semi-structured actions in natural or semi-natural settings. It therefore overlaps with experimental data and survey data and provides a similar representation $\{x, c, y\}$, although usually associated with a much wider and noisier representation of $x$ and $c$. Examples include activity logs from online platforms and social media, search and browsing patterns, and video recordings of group interactions. These datasets are rich in variability, reflecting spontaneous, less controlled behaviors, which are valuable for generalizing to complex, real-life scenarios. Using observational data to train the behavioral foundation model follows a similar procedure as experimental data, promoting additional adaptability and robustness. %
Observational data have not used to train the initial version of \model, %
although they will be included in future versions. 

\subsection{Model Configuration and Training}
\label{sec:train}

To train the initial versions of \model, we adopt two releases of Meta Llama 3.1 (70B and 8B) \citep{grattafiori2024llama} as the foundation LLMs. To improve computational efficiency, the 70B model is quantized with 8-bit precision using the bitsandbytes library, reducing memory overhead while preserving performance.
We fine-tune the model using LlamaFactory \citep{zheng2024llamafactory}, employing supervised fine-tuning (SFT) with low-rank adaptation (LoRA) \citep{hu2022lora} to enable efficient weight updates. LoRA is applied across all model layers, ensuring fine-tuning without excessive computational costs.
Training data is formatted following the Alpaca template \citep{taori2023alpaca}, aligning inputs with the model’s expected structure. Preprocessing includes tokenization, truncation, and caching, with multiprocessing used to improve scalability and reduce latency. This fine-tuning approach optimizes \model for behavioral science applications while maintaining efficiency and flexibility.


