\section{Generalizable Capability of \model in Various Behavioral Science Tasks}
\label{sec:tasks}

Behavioral science encompasses a wide range of tasks, such as survey analysis, behavioral prediction, and simulations of interactions, which can be applied across populations and contexts. 
Traditional tools and AI models in this field often rely on task-specific datasets and handcrafted features \citep{turgeon2020tutorial,kliegr2020advances,dehghan2022applications}, limiting their ability to generalize across different applications. 
Trained with a wide spectrum of behavioral data, a behavioral foundation model has the potential to overcome these constraints and perform more effectively across various applications, and do better in novel scenarios. Also, it may hold a broader understanding of human behavior especially at the population level. 
We employ a comprehensive series of benchmark tasks designed to capture key dimensions of behavioral science, enabling a systematic evaluation of \model’s capabilities. These tasks and corresponding capabilities are organized surrounding the general framework in Equation~\ref{eqn:framework} and aligned with the key dimensions of behavioral science, as outlined in Table \ref{tab:benchmark}. 
Additional details--including the test data, prompts, evaluation metrics, and baselines--are provided in Appendix \ref{app:benchmarks}. 

\subsection{\model's ability to predict and simulate behaviors across scenarios}
\label{sec:infer-behavior}


Traditional methods for behavior prediction often struggle to generalize across diverse populations and settings \citep{muthukrishna2019problem,yarkoni2022generalizability,d2022underspecification,barocas2023fairness}. In contrast, the \model foundation model leverages large-scale data and contextual reasoning, enabling more adaptive and accurate inferences across real-world scenarios.



\paragraph{Predicting behaviors as a function of characteristics and settings in economic games. }
A key aspect of behavior prediction is the ability to infer behaviors or experiment outcomes at the population level, tracing out a distribution as a function of characteristics and settings. %
Building on prior work \citep{mei2024turing, xie2024different}, we evaluate \model using five classic economic games, covering seven distinct scenarios: (1) the Dictator Game, (2) the Ultimatum Game (as both Proposer and Responder), (3) the Trust Game (as both Investor and Banker), (4) the Public Goods Game, and (5) the Bomb Game. These games assess various behavioral tendencies, including altruism, fairness, trust, and risk preference. 
Formally, the prediction task can be represented as 
\begin{equation}
    X, c \rightarrow \mathbf{E}_{x \in X}[p(y|x, c)],
\end{equation}
where $c$ is a given game scenario, $X$ is a population of human subjects, and $\mathbf{E}[p(y|x, c)]$ is the expected behavioral choice as a function of this subject population characteristics in this game scenario. 

