\section{Model Configuration and Training}
\label{app:train}

\subsection{Backbone Foundation Model and Implementation Details}

\textbf{Model.}
We used \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct}\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct}, retrieved April 9, 2025. } and \texttt{LLaMA-3.1-70B-Instruct}\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct}, retrieved April 9, 2025. } as backbone models. The 70B variant was quantized to 8-bit precision using the \texttt{bitsandbytes}\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/docs/bitsandbytes}, retrieved April 9, 2025. } library.

\textbf{Method.}
Fine-tuning was conducted using LlamaFactory\footnote{\url{https://github.com/hiyouga/LLaMA-Factory}, retrieved April 9, 2025.} \citep{zheng2024llamafactory} with supervised fine-tuning (SFT). Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) \citep{hu2022lora} was applied across all model layers to enable efficient fine-tuning with reduced memory overhead.


\textbf{Training Configuration and Hyperparameters.}
Training was conducted with a per-device batch size of 1 and 8 gradient accumulation steps, using a learning rate of $10^{-4}$ with a cosine scheduler and a warmup ratio of 0.1. The model was trained for 3 epochs using \texttt{bf16} mixed precision. A distributed data parallel (DDP) timeout of 180,000,000 seconds was set. This configuration balanced efficiency and convergence stability for large-scale fine-tuning.




\textbf{Data Formatting.}
Data were formatted according to the Alpaca template \citep{taori2023alpaca}, aligning with the model’s expected input structure. Each training instance consisted of an \texttt{instruction}, \texttt{input}, and the corresponding ground-truth \texttt{output}. Preprocessing steps included tokenization, truncation, caching, and multiprocessing to enhance throughput and minimize latency.





\subsection{Training Data and Training Tasks}

\subsubsection{Literature Data}
\label{app:data-literature}

\paragraph{Publications. }

Our dataset comprises 3,003 publications from the leading journal, \emph{American Economic Review} (AER), which publish interdisciplinary research spanning economics, psychology, sociology, and human behavior. 
Articles were selected based on the availability of both title and abstract, which are essential for model training. Of the total, 2,703 publications are used for training and the remainder for evaluation. 


\paragraph{Research workflow reasoning. } 

We convert publication data into a research workflow reasoning format. Following \citet{zhang2024massw}, we extract five key components from each paper--context, key idea, method, outcome, and projected impact--using the paper's title and abstract. Extraction is performed using \texttt{gpt-4o-2024-05-01-preview}. The data are then structured into two subtasks: (1) idea generation, and (2) title prediction. 

For idea generation, the \texttt{input} consists of the extracted context (the template is provided below), and the \texttt{output} is the corresponding key idea. 

\begin{framed}
Given the context: '\{context\}', generate key ideas that could advance this area of study.
\end{framed}

For title prediction, the \texttt{input} includes all five extracted components (the template is provided below), with the original paper title as the \texttt{output}. These input-output pairs are then used to train the model.

\begin{framed}
Given the context: '\{context\}', the key idea: '\{key\_idea\}', the method: '\{method\}', the
outcome: '\{outcome\}', and the future impact: '\{future\_impact\}', predict the title of this
research paper. The title should be concise and reflective of the core aspects.
\end{framed}


The \texttt{instruction} is given as follows:
\begin{framed}
You are an expert in research tasked with generating detailed prompts for various aspects of academic research papers.
Each task involves creating a specific type of prompt based on the provided information. Here are the definitions of each part you will work with:

- Concept: Definition, Relative Time

- Context: The status quo of related literature or reality which motivated this study.
This could normally be a problem, a research question, or a research gap that has not been successfully addressed by previous work. This is anything that happened before this study.

- Key Idea: The main intellectual merit of this paper, often in comparison to the context.
This could normally be a novel idea or solution proposed in this paper that distinguishes it from what's already done in literature.
This is proposed in this study.

- Method: The specific research method that investigates and validates the key idea.
This could be an experimental setup, a theoretical framework, or other necessary methodology to implement and/or evaluate the key idea.
This is performed in this study.

- Outcome: The factual statement about the study output.
This could be the experiment results and any other measurable outcome that has occurred.
It marks whether the key hypothesis is testified or not. This is produced in this study.

- Projected Impact: The author-anticipated impact of the work on the field, and potential further research identified by the author that may improve or extend this study.
This is anything being anticipated but has not happened yet.
\end{framed}


\subsubsection{Experimental Data}
\label{app:data-experiment}

\paragraph{MobLab human-play records. }

We use data from MobLab\footnote{MobLab: \url{https://moblab.com/}, retrieved April 11, 2025.}, a platform for behavioral science and economic games. Our analysis focuses on five classic economic games across seven roles: (i) Dictator Game, (ii) Ultimatum Game (Proposer and Responder), (iii) Trust Game (Investor and Banker), (iv) Public Goods Game, and (v) Bomb Game. Additional background on these games is provided in \citet{mei2024turing}. The dataset includes first-round gameplay data from 68,779 subjects, yielding 82,057 independent observations collected between 2015 and 2023.
Since the evaluation regarding this data collection is performed at the distributional level, we do not separate the data into training and test sets.

\paragraph{Behavior distribution prediction. }

To train the model to predict behavioral distributions, we format each human-play record as a single data entry. The game instruction (as described in \citet{mei2024turing}) is provided as the \texttt{instruction}, and the observed human behavior is recorded as the \texttt{output} (e.g., ``[\$50]’’ in the Dictator Game). In total, we construct 82,057 such data entries for model training.


\subsubsection{Survey Data}
\label{app:data-survey}

\paragraph{BigFive personality test. }

We use the publicly available OCEAN Five Factor Personality Test Responses dataset\footnote{\url{https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/lucasgreenwell/ocean-five-factor-personality-test-responses}, retrieved April 11, 2025.}, sourced from the Open-Source Psychometrics Project\footnote{\url{https://openpsychometrics.org/}, retrieved April 11, 2025.}, a nonprofit initiative dedicated to public education and psychological research.
The dataset includes responses and metadata from 19,719 subjects. Participants represent a wide range of demographic backgrounds, covering individuals from over 11 racial groups and 161 countries and regions. Ages range from 13 and above, providing broad age coverage. Gender identity is self-reported, with participants identifying as male, female, or other, reflecting gender diversity in the sample.
Of the subjects, 17,667 are used for training, and 1,963 are reserved for evaluation.

\paragraph{BigFive personality traits prediction. }

To train the model to predict survey responses, each data entry is formatted as follows: for each subject and each personality dimension, we provide the subject’s demographic information in the \texttt{instruction}, the prediction target in the \texttt{input}, and the ground-truth dimensional score (an integer from 10 to 50) in the \texttt{output}. The \texttt{instruction} and \texttt{input} are templated as follows:

\begin{framed}
\texttt{instruction}:\newline
You are a helpful AI assistant. The user has the following demographics: \{demographics\}.

\texttt{input}:\newline
Based on this person's demographics, rate their \{personality\_dimension\} on a scale of [10] to [50]. 

For openness:
[10] means very conventional/traditional, [50] means very open to new experiences.\newline
For conscientiousness:
[10] means very spontaneous/flexible, [50] means very organized/responsible.\newline
For extroversion:
[10] means very introverted, [50] means very extroverted. \newline
For agreeableness:
[10] means very competitive/assertive, [50] means very cooperative/compassionate.\newline
For neuroticism:
[10] means very emotionally stable, [50] means very prone to stress/anxiety.\newline

The response should only be a number from 10 to 50 in square brackets.
\end{framed}


\paragraph{Demographics prediction. }

Another modeling direction involves predicting demographic attributes from survey responses. Each subject’s record is converted into a data entry, where the overall task description is provided in the \texttt{instruction}, the subject’s personality scores in the \texttt{input}, and the subject’s age (an integer) as the ground-truth \texttt{output}. The \texttt{instruction} and \texttt{input} are templated as follows:


\begin{framed}
\texttt{instruction}: \newline
You are an expert in psychology. Given a person's BigFive personality scores, your task is to predict this person's demographics. \newline

\texttt{input}: \newline
\#\# BigFive Dimensionality Scores\newline
Each dimensionality score ranges from 10 to 50, with 10 indicating the lowest score in that dimension and 50 indicating the highest score. \newline
Openness:  \{openness\_score\} \newline
Conscientiousness: \{conscientiousness\_score\} \newline
Extroversion: \{extroversion\_score\} \newline
Agreeableness: \{agreeableness\_score\} \newline
Neuroticism: \{neuroticism\_score\} \newline\newline
\#\# Output Format \newline
Please predict this person's gender. Choose from the following: 1=Male, 2=Female, 3=Other. Please only output a single number indicating the choice highlighted with [] (e.g., [x]).
\end{framed}

\section{Model Evaluation}
\label{app:benchmarks}

\subsection{Inferring Behaviors}

\paragraph{Predict behavior distribution in economic games. }

Using the human-play data described in Appendix \ref{app:data-experiment}, we evaluate how \model performs in predicting behavioral distributions in economic games. For each game, we generate behaviors from 1,000 independent chat sessions.
During generation, the game instructions (as provided in \citet{mei2024turing}) are given as user prompts, with system prompts left blank.
The generated behaviors are then compared with human play data from the MobLab platform. 
Performance is assessed at the distributional level using the Wasserstein distance between the observed and simulated distributions. LLaMA 3.1 models and GPT models (\texttt{gpt-4o-2024-08-01} and \texttt{gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18}) are included as baselines following the same procedure. Evaluation results are reported in Table \ref{tab:results-behavior-moblab}.



\paragraph{Predict Big Five personality traits. }

To predict subjects’ personality traits as assessed by the Big Five survey, we follow the data formatting described in Appendix \ref{app:data-survey}. Demographic information (including age, gender, nationality, race, etc.) is provided, and models are tasked with predicting scores for the five personality dimensions: openness, conscientiousness, extroversion, agreeableness, and neuroticism. Evaluation is conducted on the held-out set of 1,963 subjects to ensure no data leakage.
We report both individual-level metrics (mean absolute error and Spearman's rank correlation) and distributional alignment via the Wasserstein distance between predicted and ground-truth score distributions. In addition to general-purpose LLM baselines (LLaMA 3.1 and GPT models), we compare against expert models fine-tuned specifically for this task (\model 8B$^*$ and \model 70B$^*$), as well as an XGBoost regressor. Full results are presented in Table \ref{tab:results-behavior-bigfive-full}.

\input{tables/results-behavior-bigfive-full}



\subsection{Inferring Subjects/Populations}

\paragraph{Demographics prediction. }

Using the data formatting described in Appendix \ref{app:data-survey}, we predict subjects’ demographic attributes, specifically age, based on their personality dimension scores. Evaluation includes both individual level metrics (mean absolute error and Spearman’s rank correlation) and distributional alignment, measured by the Wasserstein distance between predicted and ground truth age distributions. Full results are reported in Table \ref{tab:results-subjects-bigfive-full}.

\input{tables/results-subjects-bigfive-full}

\subsection{Inferring Contexts}


\paragraph{Experiment design prediction. }

We conduct a qualitative analysis to evaluate \model’s ability to generate insights about contextual factors influencing behavior. Specifically, given a change in behavior distribution, we assess whether \model can infer plausible situational conditions or experimental settings that might have caused the shift. For this analysis, we reference a meta-study of the Dictator Game \citep{engel2011dictator}, which catalogs a range of experimental designs and their associated outcomes.

We task \model with proposing treatments that could increase/decrease sharing behavior in the Dictator Game, using the following prompt:

\begin{framed}
You are an expert in behavioral economics. Given the observation of an experiment on the Dictator game, your task is to infer what experiment designs could lead to the observed treatment effect. In the Dictator game, given an endowment of money, one player (the dictator) chooses how much of the money to keep and how much to give to a second player. \newline
In an economic experiment of the Dictator game, we observed the subject behaviors -- the proportion of money to share -- increased/decreased compared to the standard game design. Please list possible experiment designs that could lead to this treatment effect. Please rank output experiment designs by confidence. 
\end{framed}

The model outputs are listed in Tables \ref{tab:results-designs-70b-inc}-\ref{tab:results-designs-8b-dec} and discussed in Sec. \ref{sec:infer-context}. 

\input{tables/results-experiment-designs}


\subsection{Inferring Knowledge}

\paragraph{Research workflow reasoning. }

The evaluation of this task follows the data formatting described in Appendix \ref{app:data-literature}. Specifically, we use 300 AER publications held out from training for evaluation. Two subtasks are considered: idea generation and title prediction. Following the evaluation metrics used in \citet{zhang2024massw}, we report BLEURT and ROUGE-1 scores. Full results, including those from expert models fine-tuned for this task, are presented in Table \ref{tab:results-sci-flow-full}.

\input{tables/results-sci-flow-full}

\paragraph{Complex problem solving. } 

To further evaluate \model’s capabilities in solving complex problems in behavioral science and economics, we use standard problems from the International Economics Olympiad (IEO)\footnote{Official website of the International Economics Olympiad (IEO): \url{https://ieo-official.org/}, retrieved April 9, 2025.}. 
These problems test the ability to represent, reason about, and apply domain-specific knowledge. 
We curate multiple-choice questions from five years of IEO contests (2019, 2021–2024), yielding a total of 91 questions. To ensure consistency, we include only questions that do not require reference to images.
For evaluation, each question is run 10 times in separate chat sessions. 

For each problem, we prompt the model with the following instruction:
\begin{framed}
\textbf{System prompt:}\\
    You are an expert in behavioral economics. Given a multiple-choice economics contest question, your task is to answer this question.

\textbf{User prompt:}\\
    \#\# Question:\\
    \qquad Topic: (topic)\\
    \qquad Question: (question)\\ \\
    \#\# Potential Choices:\\
    \qquad    A. (choice A)\\
    \qquad    B. (choice B)\\
    \qquad    C. (choice C)\\
    \qquad    D. (choice D) \\
    \qquad    Choose the correct answer from A, B, C, or D. Respond only with the letter. 
\end{framed}

The results are presented and discussed in Sec. \ref{sec:infer-knowledge} (Table \ref{tab:results-qa}).

