It should be noted that  the continuous problem \eqref{eq:inhomalb1} is known to have several invariants, namely
\begin{equation}
I_1[u(t)] := \int\limits_x u(x,y,t)\Big|_{y=x} dx , 	
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
I_2[u(t)] :=  \int\limits_x (\partial_x - \partial_y)u(x,y,t)\Big|_{y=x} dx , 
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
I_3[u(t)] := \left( \frac{q}p \int\limits_x u^2(x,y,t)\Big|_{y=x} dx + \int\limits_x (\partial_x - \partial_y)^2u(x,y,t)\Big|_{y=x} dx \right),
\end{equation}
i.e. $\frac{d}{dt} I_j[u(t)]=0$ for $j\in \{1,2,3\}.$ These first appeared in \cite{Ribal2013AlberEquation}, and are derived in our notation for completeness in Lemma \ref{lm:inva} in the Appendix. They are based on the symmetries of $u,$ and stay constant even if $u$ grows by factors of hundreds (i.e. they involve large cancellations in general).
They can be used as a diagnostic to gauge the quality of the numerical solution. $I_1$ and $I_2$ can be preserved to 2 decimal digits in most computations. $I_3$ involves second order derivatives of the solution, and can be hard to preserve to good accuracy when the solution grows hundreds of times in size. 
Nevertheless, all invariants $I_j[u],$ $j=1,2,3,$ are preserved to at least two digits in all the runs presented in  Section \ref{sec:2}. 

\section{Landau damping and modulation instability} \label{sec:LD}

\subsection{The bifurcation}\label{subsec:41}

A central feature of the Alber equation \eqref{eq:inhomalb1} is the bifurcation between Landau damping and modulation instability. In the stable case, the inhomogeneity $u$ disperses despite the presence of the homogeneous background $\Gamma,$ which would be expected to act as a source term. On the other hand, in the unstable case, the inhomogeneity $u$ triggers unstable modes that grow exponentially -- at least initially. 

The linear  instability condition is formulated in terms of the background power spectrum of the problem, $P(k) = \mathcal{F}_{x\to k} [\Gamma(x)]$  \cite{Alber1978}. The sufficient condition for instability  is \cite{Alber1978,Athanassoulis2018}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:instability}
	\exists X_* \qquad \exists \omega_* \mbox{ with } \Rea(\omega_*)>0 \qquad \mathbb{H}[D_{X_*}P](\omega_*) = 4\pi\frac{p}q,
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb{H}$ is the Hilbert transform and
 $D_XP(k)$ is the divided difference of the background spectrum $P$ with increment $X,$
\begin{equation}\label{eq:DXP}
	D_X P(k)=\left\{\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{P\left(k+\frac{X}{2}\right)-P\left(k-\frac{X}{2}\right)}{X}, & X \neq 0, \\[6pt]
P^{\prime}(k), & X=0.
\end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
We will discuss constructive ways to check \eqref{eq:instability} in the end of Section \ref{subsec:41}.

If \eqref{eq:instability} holds for some $X_*,$ then that $X_*$ is an unstable wavenumber. The instability  is a baseband modulation instability in the sense that  unstable wavenumbers (when they exist) are of the form $|X_*|< X_{\text{max}},$ i.e., large enough wavenumbers are always stable. Moreover, the bandwidth of unstable wavenumbers, $2X_{\text{max}},$ controls the lengthscales in the emerging coherent structure (larger bandwidth leads to more localized coherent structures), while the largest $\Rea(\omega_*)$ controls the rate of growth of the inhomogeneity, according to the linear stability analysis \cite{Athanassoulis2017RogueWaves}. An important question is whether the fully nonlinear dynamics stay close to the linearized dynamics, especially for physically realistic inhomogeneities.  For example, many nonlinear Landau damping results have extremely stringent smallness conditions for the inhomogeneity and its derivatives up to high order, which are questionable in any real-world system.


Another question that is hotly debated in the ocean waves community is the nature of the transition from stable to unstable behavior, and especially how  it manifests phenomenologically in the ocean waves context. To fix ideas, consider the one-parameter family of  spectra of the form $P(k)= C P_0(k)$ for some smooth, unimodal profile $P_0(k).$  It is easy to see that there exists a critical value $C_*$ so that if $C<C_*$ the spectrum is stable (in the sense of condition \eqref{eq:instability}), while for $C>C_*$ it is unstable. So the question is, how different are the solutions of \eqref{eq:inhomalb1} for $C=C_*-\epsilon$ versus $C=C_*+\epsilon.$ Is there a striking change in the qualitative behavior of solutions around $C=C_*?$ 

A key piece of context  for this question is the timescales of interest: physically, we expect the original equation \eqref{eq:inhomalb1} to be valid for an $O(1)$ timescale $T,$ but not for $t\to\infty.$ In that context, instability that takes an asymptotically long time to manifest, would appear similar to stability when $t\in[0,T]$ \cite{Athanassoulis2017RogueWaves}.  In Section \ref{subsec:42} we investigate  numerically how well this asymptotic prediction of slow growth of the instability holds up in the fully nonlinear simulation. 

Another aspect of this question is how large the inhomogeneity eventually becomes. In the ocean wave context, $u$ is compared to an $O(1)$ homogeneous background. Thus, even if $u$ itself changes a lot, if it remains dominated by the background then the phenomenology of the sea state does not change noticeably. In this context the amplification factor is relevant: how much larger than the background can the inhomogeneity become? An amplification factor of at least $\approx 1$ is needed in order for the instability to produce dominant effects.   This question is investigated in Section \ref{subsec:42} for the first time by solving the fully nonlinear equation \eqref{eq:inhomalb1}. In particular, this question cannot be studied through the linear stability analysis. 

%Moreover, we will investigate for the first time the amplitude of the emerging coherent structures.

%Thus, the presence of an instability that would take $t\gg 1$ to manifest is qualitatively not very different from stability. Moreover, any realistic instability would have to grow within a localized enough area (e.g., hundreds of meters as opposed to thousands of kilometers). Thus, to see a clear difference from dispersion, sufficiently fast and sufficiently localized growth is required. In other words, not much is expected to change from $C=C_*-\epsilon$ to $C=C_*+\epsilon$ if we look at the problem for $x,y \in [-\frac{L}2,\frac{L}2],$ $t\in[0,T].$ Instead, a sharp change in behavior is expected if an unstable  problem suddenly moves from small bandwidth of unstable wavenumbers, small max rate of growth to  large bandwidth of unstable wavenumbers, large max rate of growth. As we will see in the examples in this section, this abrupt qualitative change can happen, and would all take place in the linearly unstable regime.

A subtle issue in this discussion is the localization of the problem from full space $(x,y)\in \mathbb{R}^2$ to a finite domain $(x,y)\in[-\frac{L}2,\frac{L}2]^2.$ The linear stability analysis is typically carried out on full space. In   \cite{Athanassoulis2023b} the question of how to localize the Alber equation and its linear stability analysis on a finite computational domain $(x,y)\in[-\frac{L}2,\frac{L}2]^2$ was investigated.  It was found that setting
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gammasoec}
	\Gamma(x) = \frac{1}L \sum\limits_{n\in\mathbb{Z}} P(\frac{n}L) e^{2\pi i \frac{n \cdot x}L}
\end{equation}
leads to the same stability condition as full space, when $L$ is large enough. In other words, we start from the continuous power spectrum $P(k)$ (often measured in the field in the ocean waves context), and proceed to generate an autocorrelation function $\Gamma$  according to \eqref{eq:gammasoec}. This in turn provides a benchmark for the size of $L$ required: as long as the effective support of $\Gamma_{\text{inf}}(x):= \mathcal{F}^{-1}_{k\to x}[P(k)]$ is well contained in $[-\frac{L}2,\frac{L}2],$ then numerically $\Gamma_{\text{inf}}$ and the $\Gamma$ of \eqref{eq:gammasoec} are interchangeable.

\medskip 

The original instability condition \eqref{eq:instability} is not straightforward to check, as it involves the existence or not of  solutions in a system of two equations (real and imaginary parts of $\mathbb{H}[D_XP](\omega)=4\pi$) in three unknowns ($X,\Rea(\omega),\Imag(\omega)$). A constructive way to check stability was introduced in \cite{Athanassoulis2018}; the curve
\begin{equation}\label{eq:curveS}
	S_X(t) := \mathbb{H}[D_XP](t) - i D_XP(t), \qquad t \in \mathbb{R},
\end{equation}
is drawn on the complex plane. This is a closed curve starting and ending at $0$ (and it might be self-intersecting for multimodal spectra $P$). If the winding number of $S_X$ around $4\pi p/q$ is nonzero, then the wavenumber $X$ is unstable. This method computes the solutions $X_*$ of \eqref{eq:instability}, but it only clarifies the existence of $\omega_*$ -- not their values. In Figure \ref{Fig:Nyquist} this technique is demonstrated for the Gaussian spectrum \eqref{eq:gamma} with $C=1.6.$ From this, the bandwidth of unstable wavenumbers can be computed. For the required computation of the Hilbert transform we use Weideman's rational eigenfunction method \cite{weideman1995computing}.

Finally, it is worth noting that, in the ocean waves problem, the potential $V(x,t)=u(x,x,t)$ controls how large the standard deviation of the sea state from the homogeneous background is  at location $x$ and time $t.$ Thus, an appropriate way to keep track of the size of the inhomogeneity over space and time is through $|u(x,x,t)|.$

