\section{Discussion and Outlook} \label{sec:Conclusions}


A relaxation-Crank-Nicolson scheme for the von Neumann equation with power nonlinearity was introduced. The scheme is second-order in time, and requires the solution of a linear system in each timestep, i.e., it is explicit in the nonlinearity. This is achieved by the introduction of an auxiliary variable on a staggered time-grid. 
The scheme is implemented with fourth-order finite differences in space. It inherits at the  discrete level the problem's $L^2$ balance law (Proposition \ref{prop:discrconbll}), making it  particularly well suited to investigate the possible growth of the solution. %The key modeling decision is to treat equation \eqref{eq:inhomalb1} as a Schr\"odinger-type equation, and not pass to one of the different formulations e.g. as a kinetic-type equation, as is often done in the literature for von Neumann dynamics (cf. Section \ref{sec:backgr} for more context).

In Section \ref{sec:2} the scheme is validated with a soliton-derived exact solution. It is found that the initialization of the auxiliary variable is important so that the scheme  achieves the expected order in both space and time, for both the main and auxiliary variables. Moreover, it is found that the main variable is to a surprising extent not affected by the error in the auxiliary variable, for example exhibiting the correct order of convergence even if the auxiliary variable does not (Tables \ref{eoch6} and \ref{eoch2}). This seems to indicate some kind of averaging, and understanding it fully could be vital in fully analyzing this kind of scheme. Finally, in Figure \ref{Fig:2} the bulk of the error is seen to be due to error in phase velocity, at least for this soliton-like solution. %Preliminary results indicate that this holds true for the standard nonlinear Schr\"odinger equation.

In Section \ref{sec:LD} the bifurcation between Landau damping and modulation instability is discussed and numerically investigated. The universal space-time cone  \cite{biondini2018universal} is recovered when  modulation instability is present. The prediction that, in borderline unstable cases, the inhomogeneity grows very slowly \cite{Athanassoulis2017RogueWaves} is confirmed. Moreover, when the instability is weak, the inhomogeneity maxes out  to  small $O(1)$ amplitudes. Perhaps more importantly for ocean waves,  weak instabilities lead to small amplification factors, i.e. the inhomogeneity remains dominated by the homogeneous background in the fully nonlinear dynamics.  On the other hand, strong enough instabilities do lead to dominant effects compared to the homogeneous background.  Finally, if the homogeneous background becomes smaller than a threshold ($C_* \approx 0.99$ in this case), there is no modulation instability at all. Instead,  nonlinear Landau damping is observed -- a dispersion-dominated behaviour, where the linearized Alber equation \eqref{eq:inhomalb1LIN} stays very close to the fully nonlinear dynamics of \eqref{eq:inhomalb1}.



In the context of ocean waves where this equation originates \cite{Alber1978}, the scaling of the problem would be $q \ll 1 \ll p.$  Modulation instability is indeed expected in the most intense sea states in the North Atlantic, \cite{Athanassoulis2018}. However, because of the aforementioned scaling of the problem, long times and large domains would be required to simulate it where it is present. Using the numerical method introduced here, it is now possible for the first time to carry out a systematic  study of the amplification factors, and their sensitivity to the size of the computational domain, initial condition etc. This is a fully nonlinear question, only accessible by simulation.



