\section{Motion Generator}
The motion generator is one of the main components of PARC's self-augmentation loop, and acts as a planner that generates kinematic motions for traversing a given terrain.
The motion generator is represented as a diffusion model \cite{ho2020denoising} trained to generate motion sequences while conditioned on a local terrain heightmap and target direction. An overview of our network architecture is shown in Figure \ref{fig:mdm-arch}.
Given %a heightmap of the surrounding terrain, a target direction of travel, and optionally the first two frames of the sequence, 
an input context $\mathcal{C}$, 
the motion generator predicts a motion sequence $\rvx = \{ \rvx^1, \rvx^2, \cdots, \rvx^{N} \}$ for traversing the terrain along the desired direction.
Each frame $\rvx^i$ of the motion sequence $\rvx$ is represented using a set of features consisting of:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\rvp^0 \in \mathbb{R}^3$, root position 
