\noindent {\bf{\underline{Effectiveness of Verbal Cueing:}}}  
To assess the effectiveness of \emph{cueing}, we analyzed gaze fixations during the navigation task and their distribution across cued and non-cued objects. Figure~\ref{fig:CDFplot} presents the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the normalized RGB pixel intensity of fixation heatmaps (generated by the GazeRecorder application) on the $x$-axis, where lower intensity values indicate higher visual focus. Our results reveal that over 80\% of cued objects exhibited intensity values below 0.5, whereas only 20\% of non-cued objects reached the same fixation intensity level. Additionally, we conducted a one-way ANOVA with the input method (EEG, EEG Recon, EEG Recon + GSR + PPG) as the categorical independent variable and the proportion of change points corresponding to cued objects as the dependent variable. The analysis revealed significant differences in cued moment detection across input modalities \((F = 10.68, p < 0.0232)\).



