\section{Discussion}
\label{discussion}
In summary, we showed that it is possible to train very deep (100+ layer) networks reliably with a local learning algorithm. We achieved this via a reparameterisation of PCNs using Depth-$\mu$P which we called $\mu$PC. We found that $\mu$PC is capable of training very deep networks on simple classification tasks with competitive performance and little tuning (Fig. \ref{fig:mupc-vs-pc-mnist-accs}), while also enabling transfer of both weight and activity learning rates across widths and depths (Fig. \ref{fig:mupc-hyperparam-transfer-tanh}). We make $\mu$PC available as part of \textsc{JPC} \cite{innocenti2024jpc}, a recent JAX library for PCNs at \url{https://github.com/thebuckleylab/jpc}.

\paragraph{$\mu$PC and inference ill-conditioning.} Despite these results, $\mu$PC did not solve the growing ill-conditioning of the inference landscape with the depth (Desideratum \ref{des3}; Figure \ref{fig:des-trade-off}). On the face of it, this suggests that a stable forward pass is all that matters for successful training. Indeed, we found that $\mu$PC seems to initialise the activities much closer to the inference solution than SP (Fig. \ref{fig:mupc-activity-inits}), at least for linear networks where we know the solution (Eq. \ref{eq:pc-infer-solution}). However, when we trained $\mu$PC networks with a single inference step, performance degraded not only asymptotically but also with depth (Figs. \ref{fig:mupc-one-step-mnist} \& \ref{fig:mupc-one-step-fashion}). Together, these results suggest that while stable forward initialisation is critical, some (empirically determined) number of inference steps is necessary to achieve stable training dynamics with depth and good performance. It would be interesting to study $\mu$PC in more detail in linear networks given their analytical tractability. We make code to compute the inference solution (Eq. \ref{eq:pc-infer-solution}, including the activity Hessian, Eq. \ref{eq:activity-hessian}) for different linear networks and parameterisations available as part of \textsc{JPC} \cite{innocenti2024jpc}.

\paragraph{$\mu$PC and the other Desiderata.} Did $\mu$PC satisfy some other Desiderata (\S \ref{desiderata}) besides the stability of the forward pass at initialisation (Desideratum \ref{des1})? When experimenting with $\mu$PC, we tried including the Depth-$\mu$P scalings only in the forward pass (i.e. removing them from the energy or even just the inference or weight gradients). However, this always led to non-trainable networks even at small depths, suggesting that the Depth-$\mu$P scalings are also beneficial for PC inference and learning and that the resulting updates are likely to keep the forward pass stable during training (Desideratum \ref{des2}). However, $\mu$PC did not seem to prevent the ill-conditioning of the inference landscape from growing with training (see Figs. \ref{fig:mupc-train-cond-nums-mnist} \& \ref{fig:mupc-train-cond-nums-fashion}), thus violating Desideratum \ref{des4}.

\paragraph{Is $\mu$PC optimal?} To better understand $\mu$PC, we studied a regime---specifically when the width is much larger than the depth---where the equilibrated energy converges to the loss and so PC effectively implements BP (\S \ref{mupc-theory}, Theorem \ref{thm1}). However, we found that this correspondence broke down during training (Fig. \ref{fig:mupc-loss-energy-ratios-init-1e-1}), and yet $\mu$PC was clearly capable of training very deep networks away from this BP regime. In addition, all our experiments used Adam as parameter optimiser, and we did not rescale the learning rate by $\sqrt{NL}$ as prescribed by Depth-$\mu$P \cite{yang2023tensorinfdepth}. However, we note that depth transfer has also been achieved without this scaling \cite{bordelon2023depthwise, noci2025super} and that the optimal depth scaling is still under development \cite{dey2025don}. It would also be useful to better understand the relationship between $\mu$PC and the (width-only) $\mu$P parameterisation for PC proposed by \cite{ishikawa2024local} (see \S \ref{related-work} for a comparison). A better theoretical grasp of all these questions could lead to an even better parameterisation of PCNs.

\paragraph{Other future directions.} Given the recent successful application of Depth-$\mu$P to convolutional networks and transformers \cite{bordelon2023depthwise, noci2025super}, it would be interesting to investigate whether these more complex architectures can be successfully trained on large-scale datasets with $\mu$PC. Future work could also study whether $\mu$PC provides any compute or memory efficiencies over BP. Finally, our analysis of the inference landscape can be applied to any other algorithm performing some kind of inference minimisation (see \S \ref{other-algos} for a preliminary investigation of equilibrium propagation), and it could be interesting to see whether these algorithms could also benefit from $\mu$P.


