\subsubsection{Is inference convergence necessary and sufficient?}
\label{infer-converge-necessary}
Our analysis of the conditioning of the inference landscape (\S \ref{ill-cond-infer}) could be argued to rely on the assumption that converging to the analytical solution of the inference dynamics is beneficial for learning and ultimately performance. This question has yet to be resolved, with some works showing both theoretical and empirical benefits for learning close to the inference equilibrium \cite{song2022inferring, innocenti2025only}, while others argue to take only one step \cite{salvatori2022incremental}. Our experiments with $\mu$PC clearly suggest that one step is worse (e.g. Fig. \ref{fig:mupc-one-step-mnist}) than as many as hidden layers (e.g. Fig. \ref{fig:mupc-vs-pc-mnist-accs}).

Here we report another experiment that speaks to this question and in particular suggests that \textit{inference convergence is necessary but not sufficient}, at least for standard PC. If we train standard linear ResNets of varying depth on MNIST with analytical inference (using Eq. \ref{eq:pc-infer-solution}), we observe that even the deepest ($H= 32$) networks now become trainable with standard PC in the sense that the training and test losses decrease monotonically (Fig. \ref{fig:pc-analytic-infer}). However, the starting point of the test losses substantially increases with the depth, and the test accuracies of the deepest networks remain at chance level. One possible interpretation is that there are limitations to how much weight updates at even the exact inference solution can help with an inherently unstable forward pass (\S \ref{vanish-explode-ffwd}). These results do not contradict our analysis but highlight the important distinction between trainability and generalisation. Our analysis addresses the former, while the latter is beyond the scope of this work and could be interesting to investigate.
