%Since there are $2^{n-i}$ rows of $\widehat{U}^{(l)},$ for eigenpairs $\left(\lam_r, \tilde{u}_r=\bmatrix{\tilde{u}_{r1} \\ \tilde{u}_{r2}}\right),$ $r=1,2$ of $U$ there will be $2\times 2^{n-i}=2^{n-i+1}$ eigenpairs of $\widehat{U}$ corresponding to an odd value of $l$ are of the form $$(\widehat{\lam}, \widehat{u}=\bmatrix{\widehat{u}^{(1)} \\ \widehat{u}^{(2)} \\ \vdots \\ \widehat{u}^{(2^{i-j}-1)} \\ \widehat{u}^{(2^{i-j})} \\ \hline \\ \underline{\widehat{u}}^{(1)} \\  \underline{\widehat{u}}^{(2)} \\ \vdots \\  \underline{\widehat{u}}^{(2^{i-j}-1)}})$$ where $\widehat{u}^{(l)}\in \C^{2^{n-i}}, 1\leq l \leq 2^{i-j}$, $\underline{\widehat{u}}^{(l)}\in \C^{2^{n-i}}, 1\leq l\leq 2^{i-j}-1.$ Indeed it is easy to verify from the structure of $\widehat{U}$ that the following are eigenpairs of $\widehat{U}:$ for odd $l,$ $\widehat{\lam}=\lam_r$ and a corresponding orthonormal eigenvectors $\widehat{u}$ with $$\widehat{u}^{(l')}=\left\{ \begin{array}{ll} \tilde{u}_{r1}\ket{e_{m_1}}, & \hbox{if $l=l'$} \\ 0, & \hbox{$l\neq l',$} \end{array} \right. \underline{\widehat{u}}^{(l')}=\left\{ \begin{array}{ll} \tilde{u}_{r2}\ket{e_{m_1}}, & \hbox{if $l=l'$} \\ 0, & \hbox{$l\neq l';$} \end{array} \right.$$ for even $l,$ $\widehat{\lam}=1$ with the corresponding orthonormal eigenvectors as $$\widehat{u}^{(l')} \, \mbox{or} \, \underline{\widehat{u}}^{(l')} =\left\{ \begin{array}{ll} \ket{e_{m_1}}, & \hbox{if $l=l'$} \\ 0, & \hbox{$l\neq l',$} \end{array} \right.$$ where $\{\ket{e_{m_1}} : 1\leq m_1\leq 2^{n-i}\}$ is the canonical basis of $\C^{2^{n-i}},$ $r=1,2.$ Hence, a unit eigenvectors corresponding to eigenvalues $\lam_r$  and $1$ are $$\widehat{u}_{r,l}=\bmatrix{0 \\ \vdots \\ 0 \\ \widehat{u}^{(l)} \\ 0 \\ \vdots \\ \hline \\ 0 \\ \vdots \\ 0 \\ \underline{\widehat{u}}^{(l)} \\ 0 \\ \vdots }=\bmatrix{0 \\ \vdots \\ 0 \\ \tilde{u}_{r1}e_{m_1} \\ 0 \\ \vdots \\ \hline \\ 0 \\ \vdots \\ 0 \\ \tilde{u}_{r1}e_{m_1} \\ 0 \\ \vdots }, l\, \mbox{is odd}; \,\, \mbox{and} \,\, \w