\subsection{Details about Ablation Studies} \label{appendix:ablation}
$\gamma$ is derived from the simplification of $\beta$ and $\lambda$. Specifically, starting from the initial total loss function $L_{total} = L_{rec} + \lambda L_{GIB}$, substituting $L_{GIB}$ yields $L_{total} = L_{rec} + \lambda(I(\mathbf{X};\mathbf{Z})-\beta I(\mathbf{Z};\mathbf{Y}))$. Research \cite{alemi2022deep} has shown that the reconstruction loss can be approximated as the negative of mutual information, i.e., $L_{rec} \approx -I(\mathbf{Z};\mathbf{Y})$. After replacing $L_{rec}$ with $L_{res}$, we finally obtain $L_{total} = L_{res}+\frac{\lambda}{1+\beta} I(\mathbf{X};\mathbf{Z})$, where $\gamma = \frac{\lambda}{1+\beta}$.


