	\section{Conclusion}

	% This paper studied the social power maximization problem for an external influencer, modeled as a fully stubborn agent in the FJ model. The influencer was able to select a subset of normal agents to add links, whose number is limited by a budget. For this problem, a greedy algorithm was proposed by utilizing the monotonicity and submodularity of the payoff function. An augmented Markov chain was constructed to provide analytical insights to the problem, and was also applied to speed up the algorithm for large networks. Specific graph topologies were considered. For complete graphs with rank-$1$ weight matrix, it was proved that an accurate solution could be obtained by an algorithm in polynomial time. For ring graphs with symmetric circulant weight matrix and homogenous agent stubbornness, if at most two agents were selected, the solution should be selecting the agents that were sufficiently dispersed over the network. While the conclusion seemed also hold for multiple selected agents, as suggested by the example, a strict theoretical proof was left for future work. 
	This paper examined the problem of maximizing social power for an external influencer, modeled as a fully stubborn agent in the FJ model. The influencer could form a limited number of links with regular agents, subject to a budget constraint. To address this problem, a greedy algorithm was developed by leveraging the monotonicity and submodularity of the payoff function. An augmented Markov chain was introduced to provide analytical insight and was further utilized to save some computational burden for large networks. Several specific network topologies were analyzed. For complete graphs with a rank-one weight matrix, it was shown that an exact solution can be obtained in polynomial time. For symmetric ring graphs with circulant weight matrices and homogeneous agent stubbornness, it was demonstrated that when at most two agents are selected, the optimal choice is to connect to agents that are sufficiently dispersed across the network. While numerical examples suggested this pattern may extend to larger selections, a formal theoretical proof remained an open direction for future work.


