\section{Details of User Study}
\subsection{Experiment Setup and Procedure}

The user study consists of three main stages:

\textbf{Step 1: Dynamic Scene and Monaural Audio Generation.}  
In the first stage of our pipeline, as described in the \textit{Dynamic Scene and Monaural Audio Generation} section, we used TrajectoryCrafter~\cite{yu2025trajectorycrafter} to predict dynamic point clouds for 4D scene construction. Since TrajectoryCrafter~\cite{yu2025trajectorycrafter} uses the relative transformation between the user-specified camera trajectory and the source camera as the basis for novel view rendering and requires a stabilized source video, we selected only source videos that meet this requirement. To generate these videos, we manually composed prompts for commercial text-to-video models (e.g., Kling~\cite{kling2024}, Dreamina~\cite{dreamina2025}, Vidu~\cite{bao2024vidu}), and for each sample, we designed a custom camera trajectory to render the target view. We then leveraged Sonic4D to generate both the novel view video and the corresponding spatialized audio.

\textbf{Step 2: Interface Setup and Counterbalancing.}  
As illustrated in Figure\ref{fig:screenshot}, we created a local web-based interface to facilitate user evaluation. The landing page provided clear instructions on the task and rating criteria as shown in \ref{fig:screenshot_1}. We created 33 question pages for participants to complete. To ensure counterbalancing, we randomized the order of all audio-video pairs as shown in \ref{fig:screenshot_2}, as well as the internal ordering of each pair (left/right assignment), to mitigate any potential order effects or positional biases. Participants were asked to rate each video on the following two aspects using a 5-point Likert scale. The detailed rating criteria are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Spatial Localization Accuracy:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 5 – The position of the subject or camera can be clearly perceived from the audio.
        \item 4 – The direction or positional changes can generally be perceived.
        \item 3 – Some spatial positioning is conveyed through the audio.
        \item 2 – Audio provides vague or weak spatial cues.
        \item 1 – Little to no sense of spatial position from the audio.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Audio–Visual Spatial Congruence:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 5 – The direction indicated by audio cues (e.g., volume changes, binaural differences) perfectly matches the visual motion.
        \item 4 – The audio directionality mostly matches the visuals.
        \item 3 – The audio and visual alignment is partially consistent.
        \item 2 – The audio and visuals are misaligned or direction is hard to perceive.
        \item 1 – The spatial audio cues conflict with or fail to reflect the visuals.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

