\section{Internal Logic, Subobject Lattices, and the Subobject Classifier}

\subsection{4.1. The Subobject Classifier: Sieves and Ancestry}

A subobject classifier $\Omega$ is central to the internal logic of any Grothendieck topos. In the mnēmaic site $(\mathcal{M}, J)$, the classifier and closure operators are described in purely combinatorial terms, indexed by the difference-sequence ancestry of objects.

\begin{proposition}[Subobject Classifier in $\mathbf{Sh}(\mathcal{M})$]
The subobject classifier $\Omega$ in $\mathbf{Sh}(\mathcal{M})$ assigns to each $M_w \in \mathcal{M}$ the set of sieves on $M_w$:
\[
  \Omega(M_w) = \left\{\, S \subseteq \Hom_{\mathcal{M}}(-, M_w) \mid S \text{ is a sieve} \right\}
\]
with restriction along $f: M_{w'} \to M_w$ by pullback: $\Omega(f)(S) = \{\, g : M \to M_{w'} \mid f \circ g \in S\,\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
See \cite{maclanemoerdijk}*{II.7.2}. The ancestral topology ensures sieves are closed under composition with any morphism in $\mathcal{M}$, and this explicit construction is canonical for any free category with prefix extension.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Thus, logical predicates in $\mathbf{Sh}(\mathcal{M})$ correspond to geometric substructures within symbolic memory, and truth values encode all historically possible continuations from any given mnēma.
\end{remark}

\subsection{4.2. Subobject Lattices as Heyting Algebras: The Logic of Ancestry}

For each representable sheaf $yM_w$, the subobject lattice $\Sub(yM_w)$ is completely determined by the prefix structure on $w$, and forms a finite distributive (Heyting) algebra.

\begin{theorem}[Subobject Lattice as Heyting Algebra]
For any $M_w \in \mathcal{M}$, the poset of subobjects of $yM_w$ is isomorphic to the poset of all prefixes of $w$ (including the empty word), ordered by inclusion:
\[
  \Sub(yM_w) \cong \mathsf{Pref}(w)
\]
with meet as greatest common prefix and join as least common extension.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the Yoneda lemma and the explicit combinatorics of $\mathcal{M}$, a subobject is uniquely specified by which ancestral sub-mnēmata it includes, i.e., which prefixes of $w$. The distributive lattice structure follows directly, as detailed in \cite{daveypriestley}*{Ch.\ 5}.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
For $w = (a, b)$, $\mathsf{Pref}(w) = \{\varnothing, a, ab\}$, so the subobject lattice is a chain:
\[
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=1em]
ab \\ a \ar[u, "\subseteq"] \\ \varnothing \ar[u, "\subseteq"]
\end{tikzcd}
\]
The Heyting implication and negation operate as in any finite distributive lattice.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
No enrichment, dependent types, or higher categorical structure is introduced here: the logic is fully internal to the ancestry structure. This strict minimality is foundational—every logical property is inherited from the combinatorics of symbolic difference, with no external addition.
\end{remark}

\subsection{4.3. Booleanization: Forks Yield Boolean Algebras}

\begin{theorem}[Booleanization at Branching Points]
Suppose $M$ is a mnēma with at least two distinct immediate extensions (i.e., $d_1 \neq d_2$). Then $\Sub(yM)$ is a finite Boolean algebra: the power set of the set of branches. The law of excluded middle holds internally at $M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $M$ branches via $d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_n$, the extensions $(d_i, M)$ are disjoint, and the subobject lattice at $M$ is isomorphic to the Boolean algebra $2^n$. This is the power set of branches, with meet as intersection and join as union, as proven in \cite{daveypriestley}*{Thm.~1.10}, and \cite{johnstone1982stone}*{2.12, 8.11}.
\end{proof}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
& (d_1, M) \ar[dr, dashed] & \\
M \ar[ur, "d_1"] \ar[dr, "d_2"'] & & \{\varnothing, d_1, d_2, \{d_1, d_2\}\} \\
& (d_2, M) \ar[ur, dashed] &
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
\emph{Figure 4.1: Forking at $M$ produces a Boolean subobject lattice—a power set of branches.}

\begin{remark}
This realization is standard in lattice theory and categorical logic: see Davey–Priestley \cite{daveypriestley}*{Ch.~5} or Johnstone \cite{johnstone1982stone}*{Ch.~2, 8}. No external Booleanization or further enrichment occurs: the Boolean logic is entirely a local, combinatorial property of forking in symbolic memory.
\end{remark}

\subsection{4.4. Modal Operators and Internal Closure}

With subobject lattices and their Booleanization in place, modal operators arise naturally. Each difference label $d \in D$ defines an internal “necessity” closure on subobjects.

\begin{definition}[Elementary Modal Operator]
For $d \in D$, the operator $\Box_d$ acts on subobjects $A \hookrightarrow yM$ via the Lawvere–Tierney topology $j_d$:
\[
  \Box_d(A) = \text{the largest subobject whose sections at $N$ extend, along $d$, into $A$}.
\]
Explicitly, for $f: N \to M$, $f \in \Box_d(A)(N)$ iff $(d, f): (d, N) \to (d, M)$ lands in $A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
Each $\Box_d$ is an internal closure operator, corresponding to “necessity along $d$.” No modal structure is imposed externally; all such operators are generated from the ancestral logic.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
These modal operators, and their associated closure/interior structure, are internal to the minimal topos and have no dependency on enrichment, dependent types, or higher modalities. The logic remains strictly foundational, combinatorial, and symbolic.
\end{remark}

\subsection{4.5. Literature Alignment and Foundational Position}

This internal logic and Booleanization build directly on classical results from Davey–Priestley’s *Introduction to Lattices and Order* \cite{daveypriestley}, Johnstone’s *Stone Spaces* \cite{johnstone1982stone}, and the categorical semantics of Joyal, Diaconescu, and others \cite{joyal1977cellular, diaconescu1979change}. No structure is added beyond the recursive ancestry of symbolic difference. The result is a strictly minimal, fully internal logic—one of the central strengths of the mnēmaic topos for foundational analysis.

