\section{Lawvere–Tierney Topologies, Modal Closure Operators, and Internal Spatiality}

\subsection{5.1. Lawvere–Tierney Topologies from Difference Monoids}

A Lawvere–Tierney topology $j: \Omega \to \Omega$ is an internal closure operator on the subobject classifier, compatible with the logical structure of the topos. In $\mathbf{Sh}(\mathcal{M})$, such topologies are classified by submonoids $S \subseteq D^*$, encoding “closure” under symbolic memory extension along patterns in $S$.

\begin{theorem}[Classification of Lawvere--Tierney Topologies]\label{thm:LT-classification}
Let $\mathcal{M}$ be the difference category as above, with ancestral topology $J$. There is a canonical bijection:
\[
  \left\{
    \begin{array}{c}
      \text{Lawvere--Tierney topologies} \\
      j : \Omega \to \Omega
    \end{array}
  \right\}
  \longleftrightarrow
  \left\{
    \begin{array}{c}
      \text{Submonoids $S \subseteq D^*$} \\
      \text{containing the identity}
    \end{array}
  \right\}
\]
where $S$ determines $j_S$ by declaring as “covering” all sieves generated by morphisms with label-sequences in $S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
See Mac Lane--Moerdijk \cite{maclanemoerdijk}*{VI.10} and Johnstone \cite{johnstone1982stone}*{8.3}. Each submonoid $S$ yields a topology $j_S$ via closure under extensions in $S$, and every Lawvere--Tierney topology is induced by such a closure system.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Canonical Modal Systems as Submonoids]
Let $D = \{d\}$ (unary difference).
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Trivial (K):} $S = \{\varepsilon\}$ yields the indiscrete topology; only the identity is covering, so only global necessity holds.
    \item \textbf{Reflexive (T):} $S = \{\varepsilon, d\}$ includes single steps, modeling the modal logic T ($\Box \varphi \Rightarrow \varphi$).
    \item \textbf{Transitive (S4):} $S = \{d^n : n \ge 0\}$, i.e., all finite powers of $d$, models S4 modal logic: closure under any finite sequence. (In particular, S4 is the canonical “ancestral” topology.)
    \item \textbf{Universal (S5):} If $S = D^*$ (all words), we recover the maximal closure: everything is necessary.
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
These examples directly mirror the classic modal logics K, T, S4, S5 in their sheaf-theoretic incarnations, as discussed in \cite{johnstone1982stone}*{Ch.~8}. The algebraic structure of $S$ translates to the frame properties of the modal system.
\end{remark}

\subsection{5.2. Modal Operators: Internal Closure and Dynamics}

Each Lawvere--Tierney topology $j_S$ defines a corresponding modal operator $\Box_S$ on subobjects:

\begin{definition}[Modal Operators from Topologies]
Let $S \subseteq D^*$ be a submonoid. Define the modal operator $\Box_S$ on subobjects $A \hookrightarrow yM$ by:
\[
  \Box_S(A) = \text{the largest subobject whose sections are stable under all extensions $w \in S$}.
\]
That is, $f: N \to M$ lies in $\Box_S(A)(N)$ iff, for all $w \in S$, the extension $(w, f): M_{w'} \to M_{w}$ lands in $A$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[2-step Path Modal Operator]
Suppose $S$ is generated by $d, d'$ in $D$. Then $\Box_S$ enforces necessity along either $d$ or $d'$. For instance, if $D = \{a, b\}$ and $S = \{\varepsilon, a, b\}$, then sections must persist under extension by $a$ and $b$—modeling the basic branching modal necessity.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
This internalizes the spectrum of modal closure logics, as each $j_S$ acts as a “modal context” or “semantic closure” for formulas and subobjects—see also Jacobs \cite{jacobs1999categorical}*{10.4}.
\end{remark}

\subsection{5.3. Booleanization: Forks and Local Classicality}

The combinatorics of the difference category guarantee that, whenever a mnēma admits nontrivial forks (distinct immediate extensions), the induced Lawvere--Tierney topology yields local Boolean subtopoi.

\begin{theorem}[Booleanization via Forking]\label{thm:booleanization-fork}
If, for some $M \in \mathcal{M}$, the submonoid $S$ contains all words realizing mutually exclusive extensions at $M$, then the subobject lattice $\Sub(yM)$ is Boolean. This local Booleanization is characterized by the presence of a “power set” structure at branching points, as in Davey--Priestley \cite{daveypriestley}*{5.10} and Johnstone \cite{johnstone1982stone}*{2.14}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At any such fork, the subobject lattice is isomorphic to the power set of the set of immediate extensions, yielding a finite Boolean algebra. This follows from the general lattice-theoretic result that every finite set of independent generators yields a Boolean algebra on its power set.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Booleanization emerges as a *combinatorial property* of forking, with no need for external classicalization or enrichment: the logic is locally classical precisely where symbolic memory branches.
\end{remark}

\subsection{5.4. Internal Spatiality and Points of the Topos}

A major structural property is that the mnēmaic topos is spatial—its points correspond to infinite difference-sequences, with the canonical product topology.

\begin{theorem}[Spatiality of $\mathbf{Sh}(\mathcal{M})$]
$\mathbf{Sh}(\mathcal{M})$ is spatial: points correspond bijectively to elements of $D^\omega$ (infinite streams), and the intrinsic topology is the prefix-product topology. This aligns with Stone-type duality in Johnstone \cite{johnstone1982stone}*{8.13}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
See Section~3.3 and \cite{maclanemoerdijk}*{VI.10}, \cite{johnstone1982stone}*{Ch.~8}. Each point $\alpha = (d_0, d_1, \ldots)$ corresponds to the flat functor $F_\alpha: M_w \mapsto \{\ast\}$ if $w$ is a prefix of $\alpha$, else $\varnothing$. The basic open sets are the prefix cones $U_{M_S}$.
\end{proof}


