\subsection{6.5. Literature Alignment and Conceptual Synthesis}

The modal fixed-point logic developed here rests on the completeness of $\mathbf{Sh}(\mathcal{M})$ and the internalization of modal and bisimulation-invariant logics. The Janin–Walukiewicz theorem for infinite-branching trees, as extended categorically, confirms the expressive completeness of the internal $\mu$-calculus (see~\cite{janin1995expressive}, \cite{kozen1983results}, \cite{bekic1984programming}, \cite{lawvere1969adjointness}). Tree-unfolding in $\mathcal{M}$ provides the foundation for these generalizations, and the absence of extrinsic enrichment or higher structure ensures the framework remains fundamentally constructive and minimal.

