Participants were classified using established criteria for mild cognitive impairment (MCI), which identifies individuals with cognitive changes that do not yet produce major functional impairments. Table~\ref{tab:participants} shows the detailed demographic and cognitive layout of the participants. Key classification methods include:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Jak criteria:}~\cite{Jak_Preis_Beiser_Seshadri_Wolf_Bondi_Au_2016} Two tests in one domain scoring more than 1 standard deviation (SD) below the mean, offering higher longitudinal accuracy but detecting fewer MCI cases.
    \item \textbf{Peterson criteria:}~\cite{Portet714} One test in one domain scoring more than 1.5 SD below the mean, detecting more MCI cases but with reduced longitudinal accuracy.
\end{itemize}

MCI classifications primarily identify amnestic impairments, often associated with short-term and episodic memory, though impairments in other domains, such as language and visuospatial skills (atypical presentations), are increasingly recognized. As cognitive impairment progresses, it often becomes a multi-domain process, ultimately impacting functional abilities and transitioning to the dementia stage of clinical Alzheimer's disease (AD). The study design for collecting in-lab data focused on assessing functional abilities and activities through driving behavior in various scenarios. Key questions included identifying participants' characteristics and health status, determining cognitive impairments and their probable link to AD, evaluating co-morbid sources of cognitive decline, and assessing overall health, cognitive function, mobility, vision, and signs of dependence, depression or quality-of-life decline.

To address challenges associated with compliance and self-reporting accuracy in cognitively impaired participants, a study partner was recruited for each participant. Data collection included contributions from both participants and study partners, with comparative analyses used to ensure data reliability and robustness. This longitudinal dataset provides a valuable resource for studying the progression of cognitive decline and functional impairment.


Clinical evaluation of MCI due to AD is primarily a rule-out process aimed at determining whether the primary impairment is due to another type of dementia. However, the majority of people with AD ($\sim$60\%) also have other types of dementia~\cite{dementiaRizzi}. While this study does not exclude individuals with co-morbid non-AD dementias, clinical assessments are designed to capture these data. Participants recruited into this study are included because they exhibit primary AD. It is important to note that AD presents a complex and heterogeneous landscape, making its study both challenging and critical.

In addition to clinical diagnosis and functional assessments, composite cognitive scores can offer a quantitative summary of neuropsychological performance across multiple domains. A composite cognition score (COGSTAT) integrates standardized measures from several well-established neuropsychological tests. This approach captures impairments in memory, language, visuospatial skills, and executive function—core domains often affected in AD and MCI. The formula for COGSTAT is as follows:

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{COGSTAT} = 350 &+ \frac{C - 38.7}{11.1} + \frac{L - 26.2}{3.5} + \frac{R - 10.08}{3.2} \\
                     &+ \frac{B - 4.4}{2.4} + \frac{R_{cs} - 31}{3.7} + \frac{R_{rs} - 15.7}{5.7} + \frac{T - 46.1}{32.6}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\vspace{0.5em}

\noindent Where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $C$ = Controlled Oral Word Association test (continuous value)
  \item $L$ = Judgment of Line Orientation test
  \item $R$ = Auditory Verbal Learning test: 30-minute recall score
  \item $B$ = Number of errors in Benton Visual Retention test
  \item $R_{cs}$ = Rey-O Complex Figure test: copy score
  \item $R_{rs}$ = Rey-O Complex Figure test: 30-minute recall score
  \item $T$ = Trail Making test Part B: time in seconds
\end{itemize}


While such neuropsychological testing provides a comprehensive assessment of cognitive abilities, it often requires in-person clinical visits, participant compliance, and substantial time and resources — all of which may be challenging for individuals in the early stages of AD. In this context, analyzing naturalistic driving video offers a promising, non-invasive alternative to capture subtle behavioral cues of cognitive decline in real-world settings. Studies show that behavioral patterns in everyday driving can be an alternative and effective approach to detect early-stage AD, complementing traditional cognitive testing and potentially enabling earlier intervention.


