Data was collected during a 3-month baseline - including driving video, actigraphy, sleep log, driving behavior and in-lab data by the University of Nebraska Medical Center in Omaha. Ninety legally licensed and active, aged between 65 - 90 years old participants were recruited with a study partner near Omaha, NE. They were recruited through community outreach, including flyers, local news, and community engagement~\cite{Merickel25112019}. Over a 2-year period, driving data were collected in 3-month intervals using custom-installed “Black Boxes” in each participant’s personal vehicle. These systems passively recorded driving behavior throughout each trip, capturing synchronized forward roadway and cabin video alongside sensor data from GPS, speed, accelerometer, gyroscope, and throttle. All sensor data were processed at a 1Hz sampling rate. All participants provided informed consent in accordance with institutional protocols.


While analyzing the collected driving video data, we encountered some missing and corrupted video clips that required filtering before analysis. Fig.~\ref{fig:missing-data} presents the breakdown of the processed driving data for our analysis. While processing the in-vehicle \texttt{Blackbox} video data, we identified a total of 520,662.7 minutes of driving footage, segmented into pure drive duration (72.6\%), blackframe drive duration (4.1\%), and missing drive duration (23.3\%). For downstream analysis, only valid segments—comprising both pure and black-frame video—were retained. Segments with missing or corrupted data were excluded to ensure input quality and consistency for driver behavior modeling and cognitive health assessment tasks.

Subjects included in this study were required to pass at least a predefined fraction of the \texttt{Omaha most transited route} road segments during the three-month baseline period. This criterion was applied to ensure adequate video data coverage for each participant and to make sure we have diverse driving scenario exposure in the dataset. By focusing on these criteria, our framework extracts meaningful insights into the cognitive status of aging drivers, correlating observed behaviors with functional decline.
For our analysis, a total of $N=69$ subjects were utilized for video-based analysis using Year-1 data, after excluding the subjects without class labels and drivers outside the Omaha region. Figure~\ref{fig:cogstat_plot} shows the distribution of COGSTAT scores across three aging groups— AD, MCI, and Normal aging. Among 69 subjects, 35 drivers (26 with MCI and 9 with Alzheimer’s Disease) were categorized as ``AD-aging", while 34 were classified as ``Normal-aging". This balanced distribution enabled a robust comparison between normal and AD aging groups in terms of cognitive test scores and driving behavior.  Subjects with Alzheimer’s consistently show the lowest scores, while normal-aging individuals cluster at the higher end. MCI subjects span the intermediate range, overlapping with both groups. It also highlights the transitional nature of cognitive decline and the inherent classification challenge.

To ensure consistency and relevance in our analysis, we focused on short segments. We selected roadway segments where a drive can be $ \leq 0.31$ mile or $ \leq 500$ meters and defined it as a ``route segment" (discussed in \ref{subsec_4.1}). These segments provide concentrated snapshots of driver behavior under controlled road conditions. This approach minimizes variability due to changes in driving context or prolonged periods of observation, which may dilute critical behavioral signals. Additionally, the time of day and traffic conditions (peak vs. off-peak hours) were considered to account for environmental stressors that could uniquely challenge drivers with Alzheimer’s clinical syndrome (ACS).



\subsubsection{Scenario-specific Video Sample}

The overall pipeline for scenario-specific video sample preparation is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:video_sample}. Let $\mathcal{D} = {D_1, D_2, \dots, D_d}$ be the set of subjects that participated in this study. Each subject $D_i$ performs a set of drives \{${dr_1, dr_2, \dots, dr_n}$\} where $n$ is the maximum number of drives per subject. Each drive is further segmented into short video clips ${V_1, V_2, \dots, V_m}$, where $m$ is the maximum number of video clips per drive. These video clips are recorded under different driving scenarios $\mathcal{S} = {Sc_1, Sc_2, \dots, Sc_s}$. From these clips, a subset of scenario-specific video samples $V_k^{(D_i, Sc_j)}$ is filtered for each subject-scenario pair $(D_i, Sc_j)$ where $k$ is the index of the sampled video clip. This resulted in a quality dataset of labeled video samples that were used as inputs to the large vision model in the next stage. Additionally, the hierarchical rollout of video samples across subjects, drives, and scenarios ensures a structured and scalable representation across varying cognitive profiles and driving contexts.

\subsubsection{Large Vision Model-based Framework}
We feed the video samples into a pre-trained Large Vision Transformer model~\cite{kolesnikov2020big} to perform the video-to-vector generation. The extracted vector representations are stored in Amazon AWS Cloud for further analysis. The goal of this framework is to transform raw driving videos into discriminative vector representations for downstream classification of cognitive aging groups. This is achieved via the following four-step pipeline:
The framework follows four main steps:
The entire framework includes four steps as follows:


\textbf{Step 1: Video-to-embedding Generation}\\
Each video sample $V_k^{(D_i, Sc_j)}$ is processed through a pre-trained large vision model $F_{\theta}$ to obtain a fixed-size vector space:
\[
Z_k = F_{\theta}(V_k^{(D_i, Sc_j)}), \quad Z_k \in \mathbb{R}^m
\]
where $Z_k$ represents the vector representation of each video sample $V_k^{(D_i, Sc_j)}$ in an $m$-dimensional latent space.

\textbf{Step 2: Dimensionality Reduction}\\
To interpret the structure of the vector space and the separability of the aging groups in each scenario setting, we apply a dimensionality reduction function $g: \mathbb{R}^m \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^n$ (e.g., PCA~\cite{WOLD198737}, t-SNE~\cite{van2008visualizing}) to obtain:
\[
\tilde{Z}_k = g(Z_k), \quad \tilde{Z}_i \in \mathbb{R}^n, \text{ where } n \in \{50, 100, 200, \dots\}
\]
This step is performed in a way to preserve the majority of the information embedded in the high-dimensional video representation while mapping it into a lower-dimensional vector space. It allows us to observe how the visual features related to the driving behavior in different scenarios are clustered in a low-dimensional space and select the prominent scenarios for the next steps. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:video-to-vector}, a demo reduced 2D $(n=2)$ representations are plotted to visualize group separability within each scenario.

\textbf{Step 3: Discriminative Scenario Selection}\\
To evaluate the relevance of a scenario $Sc_j \in \mathcal{S}$ for classification, we compute a pairwise distance metric (e.g., centroid, Euclidean) over the embeddings belonging to different classes (e.g., Normal, AD). The scenario with the highest inter-group distance is selected:
\[
Sc^* = \arg\max_{Sc_j \in \mathcal{S}} \ \text{Dist}_{\text{avg}}(Z_{\text{class}_{normal}}^{(Sc_j)}, Z_{\text{class}_{AD}}^{(Sc_j)})
\]
where $Z_{\text{class}_{normal}}^{(Sc_j)}$ and $Z_{\text{class}_{AD}}^{(Sc_j)}$ denote the set of embeddings under scenario $Sc_j$ for class Normal and AD respectively.

\textbf{Step 4: Classification using RF Model}\\
The scenario-specific embeddings are then used to train a Random Forest (RF)~\cite{breiman2001random} classifier $H: \mathbb{R}^m \rightarrow \mathcal{Y}$, where $\mathcal{Y}$ is the set of class labels:
\[
\hat{Y}_k = H(Z_k)
\]
Final model performance is assessed using metrics such as accuracy\( \mathbf{(a)} \), precision\( \mathbf{(P)} \), recall\( \mathbf{(R)} \) and F1-score\( \mathbf{(F1)} \) under multiple sampling strategies (e.g., random vs. driver-level separation) to ensure generalizability. We report average classification accuracy under two sampling strategies:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Random Sampling:} A total of $n$ video samples are randomly selected from the full dataset for training and testing, without enforcing subject- or scenario-level separation. This strategy evaluates the model's performance under unconstrained sampling conditions.

    \item \textbf{Driver-level Separation (DLS):} To evaluate the generalizability of the proposed framework across different individuals, we employed a leave-$k$-drivers-out cross-validation strategy~\cite{leave-out}, where $k$ drivers are excluded from the training set and reserved exclusively for testing. This procedure is repeated over $r$ independent runs, each with a different subset of left-out drivers. Finally, the results across these runs are averaged to get the performance metrics \( \mathbf{(a, P, R, F1)} \). It ensures robustness and mitigates variance due to subject-specific biases.
    \end{itemize}
This setup allows us to evaluate both intra-subject consistency and inter-subject generalization in the learned representations. 



\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Scenario-Based Video Analysis}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Input:} Set of video samples $\mathcal{V} = {V_k^{(D_i, Sc_j)}}$ for subject $D_i$ in scenario $Sc_j$
\State \textbf{Output:} Trained classifier $H$, scenario relevance ranking ${\text{Dist}_{\text{avg}}(Sc_j)}$

\For{each scenario $Sc_j \in \mathcal{S}$}
\State Initialize $\mathcal{E}_{Sc_j} \gets \emptyset$ \Comment{Store embeddings}
    \For{each subject $D_i \in \mathcal{D}$}
        \For{each video $V_k^{(D_i, Sc_j)}$}
        \State $Z_k \gets F_\theta(V_k^{(D_i, Sc_j)})$ \Comment{Video-to-vector}
        \State $\mathcal{E}_{Sc_j} \gets \mathcal{E}_{Sc_j} \cup {Z_k}$
        \EndFor
    \EndFor
    \State $\tilde{\mathcal{E}}_{Sc_j} \gets g(\mathcal{E}_{Sc_j})$ \Comment{Dimensionality reduction, $g:\mathbb{R}^m \to \mathbb{R}^n$}
    \State Compute $\text{Dist}_{\text{avg}}(Sc_j) \gets \text{Distance}(\tilde{\mathcal{E}}^{\text{normal}}, \tilde{\mathcal{E}}^{\text{mci}})$
\EndFor

\State $Sc^* \gets \arg\max_{Sc_j} \ \text{Dist}_{\text{avg}}(Sc_j)$ \Comment{Most discriminative scenario}

\State Train RF classifier $H: \mathbb{R}^m \rightarrow \mathcal{Y}$ using $\mathcal{E}_{Sc^*}$ and corresponding labels

\State \Return $H$, scenario ranking ${\text{Dist}_{\text{avg}}(Sc_j)}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}





\section{Experimental Setup}
\subsection{Scenario-based Roadway Selection}
\label{subsec_4.1}

To investigate the relationship between driving behavior and cognitive status, we selected two distinct types of roadway scenarios where older drivers commonly exhibit prominent visual or maneuver-based patterns. These patterns can serve as interpretable behavioral cues for identifying cognitive decline. The selected driving scenarios for our video-based analysis are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Freeway-interchange}: merging or diverging behavior in ramp entries or exit segments. e.g., JFK N to I-80 E Ramp, I-480 W to Dodge W Ramp.
    \item \textbf{Interstate}: high-speed, lane-keeping scenarios on long uninterrupted roads. e.g., Omaha I-80 W, I-80 E.
\end{itemize}

