We utilize \texttt{Omaha most transited route} database for filtering the roadway scenarios. From different federal functional categories (e.g., Local, Major collector, Minor collector etc.), we observe and select the most prominent scenario combination for this study. Figure~\ref{fig:interstate_Fwy-int_GIS} shows the GIS map highlighting the two roadway scenarios for our video analysis. We considered a single driving video having $\leq 0.31$ mile or $\leq 500$ meter length as a route segment. \texttt{Omaha most transited route} database consists of 24,106 route segments. In total, we filter out $964$ unique route segments, consisting of $632$ freeway-interchange segments (highlighted in green in Figure~\ref{fig:interstate_Fwy-int_GIS}(b)) and $332$ interstate segments (highlighted in blue in Figure~\ref{fig:interstate_Fwy-int_GIS}(a)). Table~\ref{tab:drive_stats_summary} summarizes the key statistics -- a balance of the selected data and driving exposure of the cognitive groups. Additionally, we selected the route segments that maximize the number of drives for both cognitive groups. The avg. trips per unique subject column indicates that each driver has driven on the chosen roadway types at least 3.5 times, ensuring sufficient behavioral representation per subject.


To interpret the driving exposure data distribution, we define several key variables below:

\begin{itemize}
  \item $N_u$: Number of unique trips by the Normal-aging subjects
  \item $M_u$: Number of unique trips by the subjects with MCI
  \item $A_u$: Number of unique trips by the subjects with AD
  \item $T_u$: Total unique trips across all subject groups\\
  $T_u = N_u + M_u + A_u$ \\ It does not include repeated trips which means multiple driving sessions recorded on the same drive segment by the same individuals
  \item $\overline{T}_u$: Total number of trips including repeated ones (non-unique). \\ It includes repeated trips which means multiple driving sessions recorded on the same drive segment by the same individual.
  \item $T_{avg}$: Average number of trips per subject in a specific drive segment. It gives an approximation that drivers, on average, drove more on Interstate than on Freeway interchanges.

\end{itemize}

