\subsection{Executing Video-based Model}

To evaluate our video-based embedding framework, we conducted experiments across two distinct driving scenarios that capture both emergency maneuvers and straight-path driving behaviors. The aim was to analyze whether these movement types could be effectively distinguished through video-derived embeddings.

The experiments were conducted under the following evaluation protocols:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Random Sampling:} Video segments are randomly split into training and testing sets without enforcing driver-level separation.

    \item \textbf{Driver-level Separation:} A leave-$k$-drivers-out cross-validation strategy is employed, where $k=5$ drivers were excluded during training and used exclusively for testing. This protocol was repeated over $r=3$ independent runs with different left-out driver groups, and the final metrics were averaged to capture subject variability.
\end{itemize}

In our experiments, the parameters are set as: (1) Input image size (960x752x3), (2) Embedded dimension (6144x1), (3) Frame Rate—$\{1, 10\}$, (3) Low-dimension size—  $n \in \{50, 100, 200\}$, and (4) Distance Metrics—L2 and centroid-distance. These experiments provide a comprehensive understanding of how video-derived embeddings behave under varying sampling conditions and data splits and lay the foundation for downstream classification tasks.


