\begin{abstract}

In this study, we evaluate the performance of current automotive communication middlewares under various operating conditions. 
Specifically, we examine FastDDS, a widely used open-source middleware, the newly developed Zenoh middleware, and vSomeIP, COVESAs open-source implementation of SOME/IP. 
Our objective is to identify the best performing middleware for specific operating conditions.
To ensure accessibility, we first provide a concise overview of middleware technologies and their fundamental principles. 
We then introduce our testing methodology designed to systematically assess middleware performance metrics such as scaling performance, end-to-end latency, and discovery times across multiple message types, network topologies, and configurations.
Finally, we compare the resulting performance data and present our results in nine findings.

Our evaluation code and the resulting data will be made publicly available upon acceptance.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Middleware, \aclp{AV}, vSomeIP, FastDDS, Zenoh, Communication Middlewares
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

\subsection{Motivation}
The automotive industry faces new trends and challenges: electrification, \acp{SDV}, and automated driving functions.
Electrification challenges the electrical and hardware architecture of vehicles, in contrast \ac{SDV} and automated driving primarily challenge the software and its underlying architecture in modern vehicles \cite{mckinsey_case_nodate}.

In \acfp{SDV} hardware and software components are no longer rigidly coupled and high-performance dynamic communication enables new flexibility \cite{liu_impact_2022, nichitelea_automotive_2019}.
Dynamic communication is realized by switching from static, predefined fieldbus-based communication methods to new automotive Ethernet-based methods \cite{vetter_development_2020}.
Automated vehicles must perform complex functions, such as perception, planning and control. 
For these tasks, they require more sensors than conventional vehicles. 
The data from these sensors increases the bandwidth requirements on the \ac{IVN}\cite{mckinsey_case_nodate, wu_oops_2021}.

As a consequence, new software architectures are required to address these challenges \cite{zhu_requirements-driven_2021, wang_review_2024}.
A fundamental part of these new automotive software architectures are middlewares, as they enable data exchange between applications on different \acp{ECU} \cite{autosar_ap_explanation_sw_arch}. 

Multiple middlewares, such as FastDDS, vSomeIP and Zenoh, are used in the automotive domain, but use different communication protocols, communication patterns, and target domains \cite{kluner_modern_2024, zhang_comparison_2023}.
How do these differences affect the middlewares performance and which should optimally be used for a given software architecture?
To address this question, we evaluate the performance of the three middlewares in this paper. 
FastDDS is included, as it is the default middleware in \ac{ROS 2} and is supported by a large open source community. 
Zenoh is considered as a new competitor promising higher performance, and vSomeIP due to its industrial origin and recent public availability.
We evaluate their throughput, latency, and reliability characteristics in different realistic topologies, parametrization, and message types. Finally, we interpret our experimental results and derive recommendations for the use of middlewares.

\subsection{Main Contributions}
\label{sec:intro:contrib}
The main contributions of this paper are:
\begin{enumerate} 
    \item An introduction to automotive middlewares and underlying concepts.
    \item Evaluation of their performance in experiments with varying topologies, parameters, and message types.
    \item Interpretation of results and recommendations for the application of middlewares.
\end{enumerate}

\pagebreak

\subsection{Outline}
\label{sec:intro:outline}
The paper is structured as follows:
\Cref{sec:background} provides an introduction to automotive middlewares and distributed systems in vehicles. 
\Cref{sec:method} presents the parameters that we varied in our experiments, our test methodology, and our experimental setup.
In \Cref{sec:results}, we present the results of our experiments and interpret them.
Finally, \Cref{sec:conclusion} presents our conclusion and the applicability of middlewares under varying conditions.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}
The latency characteristics of middlewares have been investigated in previous studies, especially the characteristics of the \ac{ROS 2} framework have been evaluated in depth.
In 2016, \citeauthor{maruyama_exploring_2016} \cite{maruyama_exploring_2016} investigated the performance of \ac{ROS 2} and its predecessor \ac{ROS}. 
Similarly, \citeauthor{kronauer_latency_2021} \cite{kronauer_latency_2021} investigate the latency effects of \ac{ROS 2} in larger, multi-node systems. 
They determined that the main contributing factors to communication latency in \ac{ROS 2} originated with the middleware and message callback. They also showed that latency scales mostly linearly with node count in a given system.
\citeauthor{wu_oops_2021} \cite{wu_oops_2021} examined the performance of \ac{ROS}, \ac{ROS 2} and Cyber for varying parameters, topologies, and message types. They found that local topologies, where the communication does not leave the machine or even the application, perform best. They also determined that QoS can negatively impact performance and that, for higher performance platforms, communication is more likely to become a bottleneck \cite{wu_oops_2021}.
Breaking with this trend, \citeauthor{zhang_comparison_2023} \cite{zhang_comparison_2023} compare \ac{DDS}, \ac{MQTT} and Zenoh in the context of distributed \ac{ROS 2} systems. Their results indicated that the throughput between \ac{DDS} and Zenoh were roughly equal in Ethernet networks, while Zenoh's performance in \ac{Wi-Fi} and \ac{4G} was better. For latency, this finding was also true, as CycloneDDS outperformed Zenoh in Ethernet networks, while Zenoh outperformed \ac{DDS} in \ac{Wi-Fi} and \ac{4G}.
In contrast to these papers, we extend the evaluation to automotive use cases by examining additional topologies, message types, and QoS configurations. 
We also include Zenoh and vSomeIP, which have not been comprehensively respectively at all evaluated in contemporary literature.
We also evaluate each middleware directly, without intermediary Frameworks such as \ac{ROS 2}.

\section{Fundamentals}
\label{sec:background}
Before discussing middleware performance, we present the three middlewares we evaluate. We give brief introductions to the core features of FastDDS, vSomeIP and Zenoh as well as their underlying protocols and network stacks.

\subsection{Communication Middlewares}
\label{sec:background:middlewares}
Middlewares are software frameworks that act as an intermediate layer between the \ac{OS} of an \ac{ECU} and automotive applications \cite{maruyama_exploring_2016}. 
They enable communication within \aclp{IVN}, the fieldbus or Ethernet-based networks in vehicles by connecting various applications, often relying on IP-based communication protocols \cite{kluner_modern_2024}. 
Middlewares provide a simplified \ac{API} for communication, using the IP stack of the underlying operating system \cite{henle_architecture_2022}.

Publish-Subscribe and Request-Response are the most common communication patterns offered by middlewares.
In publish-subscribe communication, messages are organized by topics, allowing participants to publish or subscribe to specific topics \cite{eprosima_fastdds_docs}. In request-response communication, a participant sends a request and receives a response from a remote participant. This mechanism is often used to call functions provided by other applications \cite{autosar_spec_com_mgmt}. 

Many middlewares also include discovery features, allowing communication paths to be dynamically established at run-time without requiring manual configuration \cite{eprosima_fastdds_docs}. Some middlewares further support \ac{QoS} configurations to ensure reliable message transmission, along with security options for authentication and encryption. 
We will now provide a brief overview of three popular middleware implementations.

