\subsubsection{SOME/IP}
\label{sec:middleware:someip}

Scalable service-Oriented MiddlewarE over IP (SOME/IP) is an automotive middleware protocol designed specifically for in-vehicle communication. 
The AUTOSAR consortium, composed of \acp{OEM} and tier-one suppliers, standardizes and maintains SOME/IP \cite{autosar_some_ip}.
SOME/IP builds upon conventional transport layer protocols, using \ac{TCP} for reliable transmission and \ac{UDP} for best-effort communication. It supports three interaction models:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Methods} enable remote procedure calls using request-response semantics, with support for both synchronous interactions and fire-and-forget communication.
    \item \textbf{Events} implement publish-subscribe communication, allowing services to notify subscribers of state changes based on cyclical updates, value changes, or threshold-based triggers (epsilon-change).
    \item \textbf{Fields} represent shared variables exposed through getter and setter interfaces, typically transmitted using event notifications.
\end{itemize}

SOME/IP specifies a service discovery mechanism, SOME/IP-SD \cite{autosar_some_ip_sd}, as an extension to the base protocol, enabling dynamic detection of services on the \ac{IVN}. 
This mechanism relies on \ac{UDP} multicast communication within the local network segment.
Similarly, SOME/IP-TP \cite{autosar_some_ip_tp} specifies segmentation mechanisms for large messages that do not fit in one single \ac{UDP} packet.

Although SOME/IP itself does not provide built-in encryption or authentication, the AUTOSAR Adaptive Platform specifies security services through the Communication Management (CM) module. 
These services can integrate transport-layer security mechanisms, such as \ac{TLS}, to protect communication \cite{autosar_spec_com_mgmt}.

In this paper, we evaluate vSomeIP, an open-source implementation of the SOME/IP standard by COVESA \cite{vsomeip}. vSomeIP supports the core features of SOME/IP, including SOME/IP-SD. However, it does not implement SOME/IP-TP.

\subsubsection{FastDDS}
\label{sec:middleware:fastdds}
FastDDS is an open-source DDS implementation by eProsima, based on the DDS standard maintained by the \ac{OMG}.
FastDDS features a large community, as well as integration with \ac{ROS 2}.
It defines communication patterns and a wire protocol \cite{eprosima_fastdds_docs}.
FastDDS uses a publish-subscribe model where data is associated with topics and accessible to subscribed participants \cite{eprosima_fastdds_docs}.
FastDDS supports discovery and uses multicast \ac{UDP} for participant advertisement.
It also offers dynamic and static discovery options for flexible topology management \cite{eprosima_fastdds_docs}.
FastDDS uses the  Real-Time Publish-Subscribe Protocol (RTPS) protocol over \ac{TCP} or \ac{UDP} and supports shared memory communication \cite{eprosima_fastdds_docs}.
The protocol enables seamless switching between transport layers, such as \ac{UDP} or shared memory \ac{IPC} based on network topology.
The \ac{DDS} standard and FastDDS implements extensive \ac{QoS} settings for reliability, message history, and transmission behavior.
Basic configurations are designed for reliability versus best-effort trade-offs \cite{eprosima_fastdds_docs}.
FastDDS also offers extensive security options, such as participant authentication, access control, and traffic encryption defined in the \ac{DDS} security extensions \cite{eprosima_fastdds_docs}.


\subsubsection{Zenoh}
\label{sec:middleware:Zenoh}

Zenoh, released in 2024 as a project under the Eclipse Foundation, is a recently introduced middleware that promises lower latency, reduced transmission overhead, and optimized performance compared to existing solutions. 
According to Zettascale, Zenoh is scalable and designed for resource-constrained environments \cite{corsaro_zenoh_2023}.
Data is structured as \textit{resources} in a $(\texttt{key},\texttt{value})$ format, identified by unique keys.
Data access is enabled by selectors to match multiple resources (e.g., \textit{ / key / *}).
Zenoh has three primitive operations \textit{put}, \textit{delete} and \textit{get}, which creates new resources, removes a resource, and queries for a resource \cite{corsaro_zenoh_2023}. 
To optimize access to resources, Zenoh offers components for the three basic operations: 
\textit{Publishers}: Origin of resources for specific keys or key expressions. 
\textit{Subscribers}: Receivers of resources matching specific keys or expressions. 
\textit{Queryables}: Respond to queries by delivering resources when keys match expressions.


\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:method}

In this section, we describe our methodology to evaluate the performance of the three middlewares. We present the measurement metrics, the parameterization of the experiment scenarios, the network topologies, and the quality of service (QoS) configurations employed in the study. In addition, we describe the experimental setup, including the testbed configuration, the automation framework used to execute the experiments, and the process followed to measure, record, and interpret performance data.

\subsection{Measurement Metrics}
\label{sec:method:metrics}

We measure middleware performance using the following three metrics, illustrated in \cref{fig:measurements}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Time-To-First-Message ($T_{TFM}$)}: Defined as the time elapsed between the synchronized initialization of all communicating applications and the reception of the first message by all recipients. It includes the time required for multicast discovery, establishing the communication link, and initiating message transmission; thereby reflecting the overall delay in application discovery and communication startup.

    \item \textbf{Communication Time ($T_{Com}$)}: The time between invoking a message transmission, e.g.\ \texttt{publish}, and the message’s reception by the receiving application. It captures end-to-end transmission latency. We measured $T_{Com}$ from send to receive callback across all three middlewares, in steady state after $T_{TFM}$.

    \item \textbf{Scaling Factor ($S$)}: This metric captures the ratio between communication time ($ T_{\text{Com}} $) and the message size ($ S_{\text{Mes}} $). It is defined as
\begin{equation}
    S = \frac{T_{\text{Com}}}{S_{\text{Mes}}}
\end{equation}
and provides insight into how the communication performance of a middleware scales with increasing data volumes. A lower value of $S$ indicates better scaling behavior and more efficient handling of high data rates.

\end{itemize}

We recorded transmission times using LTTng \cite{lttng}, an open source tracing framework for Linux, with tracepoints instrumented in the middleware and application layers for accurate timing.

\subsection{Experiment Parametrization}
\subsubsection{Message Types}

As outlined by \citeauthor{wu_oops_2021}, common sensors in automated vehicles include \acp{IMU}, cameras, lidars and radars \cite{wu_oops_2021}. To ensure comparability with their findings, we adopt these four sensor types as test cases, as detailed in \cref{tab:datasets}.
The \textit{IMU} case generates high-frequency, small-sized acceleration data, along with directional and positional estimates. 
The \textit{Camera} case produces high-frequency, large-sized images. 
The \textit{Lidar} case provides moderate-frequency point clouds, each comprising multiple megabytes of data. 
Finally, the \textit{Radar} case generates data at a frequency similar to the Lidar but with smaller data sizes due to its lower resolution.

