Sensor data processing in automated vehicles can be performed centrally on a vehicle computer or distributed among zone controllers, depending on the vehicle’s \ac{E/E} architecture \cite{zhu_requirements-driven_2021}. 
We evaluate two network topologies: a \textit{centralized} topology, where all applications run on a single platform using efficient \ac{IPC}, and a \textit{distributed} topology, where applications are deployed across separate zone controllers, relying on network-based communication over the \ac{IVN}. In both topologies, we consider four combinations of sending and receiving applications: \textit{1-1}, \textit{2-2}, \textit{3-1}, and \textit{1-3}. In our notation, D1-1 representing one sender transmitting to one receiver with each participant running on a different \acp{ECU}.
Consequently, C3-1 indicates that three senders communicate with a single receiver on the same \ac{ECU}.
\cref{fig:topologies} presents all transmission and network topologies evaluated in this paper.

\subsubsection{Quality of Service}

Many middleware solutions support configurable \ac{QoS} settings, enabling applications to adjust their behavior based on specific communication requirements \cite{eprosima_fastdds_docs}. Typically, middlewares offer at least two communication modes: best-effort and reliable. In best-effort communication, message loss is tolerated in order to reduce overhead and improve efficiency. However, reliable communication ensures message delivery, though it incurs additional overhead due to the exchange of acknowledgments and control messages.
To assess the impact of these configurations, we evaluated one best-effort and one reliable setup for each middleware.

FastDDS provides extensive \ac{QoS} configurability. To limit the scope of the evaluation, we selected two representative configurations for reliable and best-effort communication. The specific settings used for FastDDS are summarized in \Cref{tab:option_sets_comparison}. In contrast, vSomeIP and Zenoh offer limited \ac{QoS} options, relying on the underlying transport protocol to guarantee message delivery. 
Consequently, we configured both Zenoh and vSomeIP to use TCP for reliable communication and UDP for best-effort transmission. It is important to note that we did not test Zenoh's reliable configuration, as it was not implemented at the time of testing.

