\begin{abstract}
The growing adoption of Deep Learning (DL) applications in the Internet of Things has increased the demand for energy-efficient accelerators. Field Programmable Gate Arrays (FPGAs) offer a promising platform for enabling such acceleration due to their flexibility and power efficiency. However, deploying DL models on resource-constrained FPGAs remains challenging due to limited resources, workload variability, and the need for energy-efficient operation.

This paper presents a systematic methodology for designing energy-efficient DL accelerators tailored to resource-constrained FPGAs. The proposed approach integrates optimized RTL templates, execution strategies adapted to workload characteristics, and application-specific knowledge into a flexible design framework. This framework efficiently explores design configurations, balancing energy consumption, resource utilization, and inference performance.

The contributions of this work include: 
(1) analyzing the challenges of achieving energy efficiency on resource-constrained FPGAs for DL applications; 
(2) proposing a systematic methodology for designing energy-efficient DL accelerators within a comprehensive framework that integrates RTL-level optimizations, workload adaptation strategies, and application-specific constraints; and 
(3) conducting a literature review to demonstrate the necessity of this work and highlight gaps addressed by the proposed approach.

% This study lays the groundwork for advancing energy-efficient DL acceleration on embedded systems and highlights open challenges for future exploration.

%--------------------------------------------------------------------------
\keywords{FPGA \and Deep Learning \and Configuration Optimization \and Sustainable Computing}
\end{abstract}

