\begin{comment}
\begin{abstract}
  Cloud data centers are the cornerstone of modern computing, acting as enablers to a diverse array of applications and services. Efficient power management  is crucial for reducing operational costs, enhancing performance, and minimizing environmental impact. GPUs, integral to high-performance tasks like machine learning, data analytics, and scientific simulations, are prime contributors to data center power consumption. The introduction of Multi-Instance GPU (MIG) technology enables the partitioning of GPUs, allowing multiple tenants to share resources without compromising performance. This capability is particularly valuable in cloud environments, where precise carbon emission reporting for individual clients is essential. However, accurately measuring the power consumption of individual MIG instances remains a challenge due to the lack of dedicated hardware support. This study addresses this challenge by developing predictive power models based on lightweight metrics from the NVIDIA Data Center GPU Manager (DCGM). Through comprehensive analysis and correlation of DCGM metrics with overall power usage, we construct models that estimate the power consumption of individual MIG partitions. We demonstrate that advanced metrics that incur high overheads add benefits towards power prediction accuracy, however the high overheads render them unsuitable for production environments. We leverage these models to estimate the power consumption of GPU partitions, enabling accurate, partition-level power estimation and contributing to fair and transparent carbon reporting. We demonstrate our methodology on state-of-the-art GPUs A100 and V100 coupled with software workloads varying from matrix multiplication, the dominating computation of GPU workloads to the ever commonplace Large Language Model inference systems. Our analysis indicates that no single method is universally effective for GPU partition power estimation across diverse workloads.
\end{abstract}
