To define the labelled transition system (LTS), we introduce
\emph{actions}, defined by the following grammar:
\begin{mathpar}
  \mu\quad::=\quad \tau \midd \inpA a v{b} \midd \out avb\midd
  \inpACT h v\midd \out hv{}\midd\omega
\end{mathpar}
Actions are \emph{early} for first-order values, and \emph{late} for
the transmission of \Nho names. We
write $\bnames\mu$ (resp.\ $\fnames\mu$) for the bound (resp.\ free)
names of action $\mu$, defined by saying that 
$b$ is a {bound} name in actions $\inpA
avb$ and $\out avb$, and the other names in actions are {free}.

We suppose that any closed first-order expression $e$ can be evaluated to
some value $v$. We write \eval ev{} for the corresponding evaluation
relation. We furthermore suppose that equality between values can be
tested.


The LTS is defined, on closed processes, by the rules in Figure~\ref{f:lts}.

