As highlighted in~\cite{Hirschkoff_2021}, 
continuation names allow us to formulate a property of
well-bracketing for traces, enforced by our type system. 
In contrast with~\cite{Hirschkoff_2021}, however, we 
 we do not
impose input receptiveness on continuation names.
% the distinction between \nser and \ncon induces a
% distinction between \emph{questions} and \emph{answers}, a terminology
% from game semantics. This allows us to establish that all traces of a
% typable process are well-bracketed.

\begin{df}[Well-bracketing]
  \label{d:wb}
An action of the form $\inpACT a{v}(b,p)$ or $\out a{v}{b,p}$ is
called a \emph{question}.
An action of the form $\inpACT p{v}(b)$ or $\out p{v}{b}$ is
called an \emph{answer}.

A trace $\mu_1\dots\mu_n$ is \emph{well-bracketed} if for all
$i < j$, if $\mu_i$ is a question and $\mu_j$ is an answer with
$\mu_i\njustif \mu_k$ and $\mu_k \njustif \mu_j$ for all $i < k < j$,
then $\mu_i \justif \mu_j$.
\end{df}

